#{[{:activity/expected-seconds 1020.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167934} :activity/id #uuid "bffb8473-8281-5879-a88a-b0d8ca02dcb1" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048344 :topic/id #uuid "9732d0de-0c87-56d2-9aa0-68532de74382" :topic/math-academy-id 3197} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167934 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186168044 :tutorial/content "In the earlier topics, we saw that *symmetry* can force many Fourier coefficients to be zero automatically.\nIn particular, if a $2\\pi$-periodic function is *even*, then its Fourier series has *cosine terms only*.\n\nA **Fourier cosine series** uses this idea when a function is initially given only on the half-interval $[0,\\pi),$ but we want a full Fourier series on $[-\\pi,\\pi)$ (and then a $2\\pi$-periodic extension to all real $x$).\n\nTo do this, we start with a function $F(x)$ on $[0,\\pi)$ and define its **even extension** $f(x)$ to $(-\\pi,\\pi)$ by\n![](images/e2/e2e07048ac4750968947e45443f59096f395b159d4f7a846410a711194b11a14.svg)\n\nFor example, a function $F(x),$ defined on $x \\in [0,1),$ and its even extension $f(x)$ to $x \\in (-1,1)$ are shown below.\n\n![](images/33/33d92f280a5672c8d9d94553ccc1ada2a9c5ea1ff12344ca8c9aeca9f44c40c6.png)\n\nBy construction, $f$ is an *even* function, meaning $f(-x)=f(x)$. Because $f$ is even, its Fourier series on $[-\\pi,\\pi)$ has *cosine terms only*:\n\n$$\nf(x)\\sim \\dfrac{a_0}{2}+\\sum_{n=1}^{\\infty} a_n\\cos(nx)\n$$\n\nSince $f(x)$ is even and $\\cos(nx)$ is even for any $n\\ge 1,$ the product $f(x)\\cos(nx)$ is even too. Therefore, the cosine coefficients can be computed using the *half-range formulas*:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{0} & =\\frac{1}{\\pi }{\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\pi }\\cdot 2{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }f(x)\\,\\text{d}x,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand for $n\\ge 1,$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & =\\frac{1}{\\pi }{\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }f(x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(nx)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\pi }\\cdot 2{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }f(x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(nx)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }f(x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(nx)\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo in practice, the workflow is the following:\n\n- Start with $F$ on $[0,\\pi)$ and form the even extension $f$ on $(-\\pi,\\pi).$\n\n- Compute $a_0$ and $a_n$ from the half-range integrals on $[0,\\pi).$\n\n- Write the Fourier cosine series of $f.$\n\nLet's see some concrete examples." :tutorial/id #uuid "e9cc2bdd-3ebe-5b87-ad23-c43cf508923b" :tutorial/math-academy-id 14676 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "70e9a79d-82de-580b-aef4-7b85b63184e6" :step/math-academy-id 595703 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167935}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167935 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166957 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166572 :question/id #uuid "b0dbd2f9-8fef-5af8-b4ce-a371e65c6441" :question/math-academy-id "e-21830"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a32afd19-5779-56f0-ad6d-1d6b541899f8" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049873}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167285} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167286} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186315070} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186315071} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186315072} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186315073} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186420184}] :knowledge-point/title "Understanding the Even Extension of a Function"} :step/id #uuid "8aa6cef0-eda2-592b-9aca-4965f3055e89" :step/math-academy-id 595704 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167936}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167936 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166958 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166574 :question/id #uuid "c8b4459b-e0ed-56cc-ab06-5be09baa7363" :question/math-academy-id "e-21833"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "96ac89a1-4179-5afe-8c1b-ebd9d47bde79" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049306} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050489}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167289} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167290} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186315079} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186315080} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186315081} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186372785} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186372788}] :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Constant Term From the Half-Range Formula"} :step/id #uuid "8896eaaa-38ce-50d6-9371-a6e16a56ffbe" :step/math-academy-id 595707 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167937}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167937 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166959 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166573 :question/id #uuid "43229a59-0b01-55ff-8b10-b1238360d277" :question/math-academy-id "e-21831"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f1e90af1-3fbd-5aa3-ac32-ac54c27c7224" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050730} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050929}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167287} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167288} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186315074} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186315075} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186315076} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186315077} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186315078} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186372764} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186372771} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186372778} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186411855}] :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Cosine Coefficients From the Half-Range Formula"} :step/id #uuid "f1fe41d9-fcaa-5738-8ea0-e820f8f39957" :step/math-academy-id 595710}] :activity/title "Fourier Cosine Series" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186166992}] [{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148455} :activity/id #uuid "792dc37c-94db-5522-a998-3f524ef92eee" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048605 :topic/id #uuid "adefe8a5-2a87-5789-91e4-05399a499f4a" :topic/math-academy-id 2531} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148455 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148756 :tutorial/content "In this lesson, we will solve first-order *initial value problems* using Laplace transforms.\n\nThe key idea is that the Laplace transform converts derivatives into algebraic expressions involving the transform of the unknown function.\nIf we write\n\n$$\nY(s) = \\mathcal L\\{y(t)\\},\n$$\n\nthen the initial condition appears automatically through the **derivative rule**\n\n$$\n\\mathcal L\\{y'\\} = sY(s) - y(0).\n$$\n\nSo, when we take the Laplace transform of a differential equation like\n\n$$\ny' + ay = f(t),\n$$\n\nwe get an algebraic equation in $Y(s),$ which we can solve using standard algebra.\n\nIn the first few problems, our goal will be to find an explicit formula for $Y(s).$\nLater, we will finish the process by taking the inverse Laplace transform to recover $y(t).$" :tutorial/id #uuid "19b12099-79ea-57ad-8d49-b0ec631e099d" :tutorial/math-academy-id 4111 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "ce168e7b-e179-5ef7-a408-d6641bda4d96" :step/math-academy-id 612632 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148456}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148456 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148667 :tutorial/content "The **Laplace transform method** is a powerful tool that converts a differential equation (a calculus problem) into an algebraic equation, which is often much easier to solve.\n\nTo solve a first-order initial value problem (IVP) of the form\n\n$$\ny' + ay = f(t), \\quad y(0)=y_0,\n$$\n\nwe follow a standard workflow.\n\n- First, we take the Laplace transform of both sides of the equation to obtain an algebraic equation in $Y(s)=\\mathcal L\\{y(t)\\}.$ Using linearity and the derivative formula\n\n$$\n\\mathcal L\\{y'\\} = sY(s) - y(0),\n$$\n\nwe convert the IVP into an algebraic equation for the unknown transform, $Y(s).$\n\n- After substituting the initial condition $y(0)=y_0$ and finding the transform of $f(t),$ we solve the resulting algebraic equation for $Y(s).$ The calculus portion of the problem is now complete.\n\n- Before applying the inverse transform, we rewrite $Y(s)$ as a sum of simpler expressions found in a standard table of Laplace transforms. This step often requires techniques like **partial fraction decomposition**.\n\n- Finally, we take the inverse Laplace transform, $\\mathcal L^{-1},$ of each term in $Y(s)$ to find the solution $y(t)$ to the original initial value problem.\n\nNext, we consider the example of finding $Y(s)$ for the concrete initial value problem." :tutorial/id #uuid "2a58657b-748f-56e6-9ebc-7cb481b9bf57" :tutorial/math-academy-id 14889 :tutorial/title "Using Laplace Transforms to Solve First-Order IVPs"} :step/id #uuid "fa47c1d6-1923-5ff4-aafc-fbb54002fb57" :step/math-academy-id 612633 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148457}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148457 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148668 :tutorial/content "Consider the following initial value problem.\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}t} + 4y = 2t-1, \\qquad y(0)=0.\n$$\n\nTo find $Y(s) = \\mathcal L \\{y(t)\\},$ we first take the Laplace transform of both sides of the differential equation. Using the linearity property, we have\n\n$$\n\\mathcal{L}\\{y'\\} + 4\\mathcal{L}\\{y\\} = 2\\mathcal{L}\\{t\\}-\\mathcal{L}\\{1\\}.\n$$\n\nTo calculate $\\mathcal L\\left\\{ y' \\right\\},$ we use the formula for the Laplace transform of a derivative and the initial condition $y(0)=0{:}$\n\n$$\n\\mathcal{L}\\left\\{y'\\right\\}  = sY(s) - y(0)   = sY(s).\n$$\n\nSo, the differential equation transforms to\n\n$$\nsY(s) + 4Y(s)   = 2\\mathcal{L}\\{t\\}-\\mathcal{L}\\{1\\}.\n$$\n\nNow, we make use of the following result:\n\n$$\n\\mathcal L\\{t^n\\} = \\dfrac{n!}{s^{n+1}}, \\qquad s > 0, \\quad n\\in \\mathbb{N}_0.\n$$\n\nThis gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{t} & =\\frac{1!}{{s}^{1+1}}=\\frac{1}{{s}^{2}}, \\\\ L{1} & =\\frac{0!}{{s}^{0+1}}=\\frac{1}{s}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting these results into the transformed equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}sY(s)+4Y(s) & =2\\cdot \\frac{1}{{s}^{2}}-\\frac{1}{s} \\\\ (s+4)Y(s) & =\\frac{2}{{s}^{2}}-\\frac{1}{s} \\\\ (s+4)Y(s) & =\\frac{2-s}{{s}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, dividing by $(s+4),$ we obtain\n\n$$\nY(s) =\\dfrac{2-s}{s^2(s + 4)}.\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "fc740a47-119f-5fa2-bac2-018828c79406" :tutorial/math-academy-id 14890 :tutorial/title "Finding the Laplace Transform of the Unknown Function Satisfying the IVP"} :step/id #uuid "8da8db44-ae26-54f5-ba7f-f8256253813f" :step/math-academy-id 612634 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148458}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148458 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147527 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147362 :question/id #uuid "700f5e8f-33f6-568a-ad11-d2664336725d" :question/math-academy-id "e-6657"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b1417a42-f47a-5611-84be-f331c75287a9" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048580} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049301} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050608}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148146} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148153} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317269} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317332} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317357} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186335707} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186335708}] :knowledge-point/title "Laplace Transforms of First-Order Linear IVPs"} :step/id #uuid "451b2242-11ee-5088-a876-11e2c54ac097" :step/math-academy-id 612635 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148459}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148459 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148665 :tutorial/content "Once we have solved for $Y(s)$, the final step is to compute the solution .\n\nThe main idea is to rewrite $Y(s)$ as a sum of simpler terms whose inverse transforms are known from a table.\n\n**Step 1:** Look for standard inverse-transform patterns.\n\nThe goal is to match terms in $Y(s)$ to known transform pairs. Common forms include:\n\n- $\\mathcal{L}^{-1}\\left\\{\\dfrac{1}{s-a}\\right\\} = e^{at}$\n\n- $\\mathcal{L}^{-1}\\left\\{\\dfrac{1}{(s-a)^n}\\right\\} = \\dfrac{t^{n-1}e^{at}}{(n-1)!}$ for integer $n \\ge 1$\n\n- $\\mathcal{L}^{-1}\\left\\{\\dfrac{s}{s^2+\\omega^2}\\right\\} = \\cos(\\omega t)$\n\n- $\\mathcal{L}^{-1}\\left\\{\\dfrac{\\omega}{s^2+\\omega^2}\\right\\} = \\sin(\\omega t)$\n\n**Step 2:** If $Y(s)$ is a rational function, use **partial fraction decomposition**.\n\nIf the denominator has distinct linear factors, we write:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{P(s)}{(s-a)(s-b)} = \\dfrac{A}{s-a} + \\dfrac{B}{s-b}\n$$\n\nwhere $P(s)$ is a polynomial with $\\deg (P) < 2$.\n\nIf there is a repeated linear factor, we include a term for each power:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{P(s)}{(s-a)^2} = \\dfrac{A}{s-a} + \\dfrac{B}{(s-a)^2}\n$$\n\nwhere $P(s)$ is a polynomial with $\\deg (P) < 2$.\n\n**Step 3:** Apply the inverse transform term-by-term.\n\nBecause the inverse Laplace transform is **linear**, we can apply it to each term in the sum separately to find the final solution $y(t)$.\n\nNext, we will apply this method to a specific example." :tutorial/id #uuid "785dff4c-cd6a-5cdd-bbcf-7c6b0c82c077" :tutorial/math-academy-id 14742 :tutorial/title "Getting Ready for the Inverse Laplace Transform"} :step/id #uuid "d4f76515-92a2-55e5-b0c3-2c8b3edd59d2" :step/math-academy-id 612638 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148460}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148460 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147528 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147363 :question/id #uuid "409c5aa3-5c53-5ab4-b2f7-bcfe01a14914" :question/math-academy-id "e-6661"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "5eae6b5d-adff-53b2-970d-f0dcee58365f" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050050} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050768} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050964}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147787} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147788} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317272} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317338} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317340} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186391797} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186391831}] :knowledge-point/title "Solving First-Order Initial Value Problems Using Laplace Transforms"} :step/id #uuid "71fe0cf6-2b8b-5473-b14f-c3ddf9ebb1d5" :step/math-academy-id 612639 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148461}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148461 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148666 :tutorial/content "When $Y(s)$ is a rational function, we often use partial fractions to rewrite it in a form where we can apply $\\mathcal L^{-1}.$\n\nSometimes the denominator contains an *irreducible quadratic factor*, such as $s^2+\\omega^2.$\nIn that case, the partial fraction term must have a linear numerator.\n\n**Step 1:** Write the correct decomposition form.\n\nFor example, if\n\n$$\nY(s)=\\dfrac{P(s)}{(s-a)(s^2+\\omega^2)},\n$$\n\nthen we decompose it as\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{P(s)}{(s-a)(s^2+\\omega^2)}\n= \\dfrac{A}{s-a} + \\dfrac{Bs+C}{s^2+\\omega^2}.\n$$\n\n**Step 2:** Multiply both sides by the denominator.\n\nClearing denominators gives an identity in $s{:}$\n\n$$\nP(s)=A(s^2+\\omega^2) + (Bs+C)(s-a).\n$$\n\n**Step 3:** Solve for the constants.\n\nWe can find $A,$ $B,$ and $C$ by expanding the right-hand side and matching coefficients of powers of $s.$\n\n**Step 4:** Take the inverse Laplace transform.\n\nWe split the quadratic term to match the standard forms:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{Bs+C}{s^2+\\omega^2} = B\\left(\\dfrac{s}{s^2+\\omega^2}\\right) + \\dfrac{C}{\\omega}\\left(\\dfrac{\\omega}{s^2+\\omega^2}\\right).\n$$\n\nThen we apply the inverse transforms:\n\n$$\n\\mathcal L^{-1}\\left\\{\\dfrac{s}{s^2+\\omega^2}\\right\\}=\\cos(\\omega t),\n\\qquad\n\\mathcal L^{-1}\\left\\{\\dfrac{\\omega}{s^2+\\omega^2}\\right\\}=\\sin(\\omega t).\n$$\n\nLet's see an example." :tutorial/id #uuid "0db35afe-1ae8-50e9-a89d-2c65a3bc7956" :tutorial/math-academy-id 14743 :tutorial/title "Partial Fractions With Irreducible Quadratic Factors"} :step/id #uuid "7fe41923-9e55-52ed-b19d-70a8608c1ef8" :step/math-academy-id 612643 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148462}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148462 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147529 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147233 :question/id #uuid "abd7efc4-f397-564d-a471-17b1ffc93fb3" :question/math-academy-id "e-21862"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "06fedec8-5aed-52f9-bd4f-aa81b9180ad4" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050213}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147789} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147790} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317344} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317345} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317351} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186385259} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186391848} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186397600}] :knowledge-point/title "Solving First-Order Initial Value Problems With Sinusoidal Forcing"} :step/id #uuid "650337b0-ab16-5d31-b033-88945afb53bf" :step/math-academy-id 612644}] :activity/title "Solving First-Order ODEs Using Laplace Transforms" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186147710}] [{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148288} :activity/id #uuid "ddf081b5-4271-58f1-ac92-6ac95f2e57d1" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048622 :topic/id #uuid "af6ffcd9-235a-539a-acab-3fa95c86d5a2" :topic/math-academy-id 2526} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148288 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148747 :tutorial/content "A harmonic oscillator is said to be **forced** if it is subject to a nonzero external force $f(t)\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\ny'' + by' + \\omega^2 y = f(t)\n$$\n\nIf the external force $f(t)$ is periodic and its frequency is equal to the frequency of the complementary solution, then the system experiences **resonance** and the amplitude of the oscillations increases without bound over time.\n\nFor example, consider the following differential equation which represents a harmonic oscillator subject to a forcing function $\\cos(2t)\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\ny''+4y=\\cos(2t), \\qquad y(0) =0, \\qquad y'(0) = 0\n$$\n\nThe complementary solution $y_c$ here represents simple harmonic motion,\n\n$$\ny_c=A\\cos(2 t) + B\\sin(2 t).\n$$\n\nThe forcing function $f(t) = \\cos(2t)$ has the same frequency as the complementary solution above (both have a frequency of $\\Omega = 2 \\, \\textrm{s}^{-1}$), so the system experiences *resonance*. As a result,  the particular solution\n\n$$\ny_p = \\dfrac{t}{4}\\sin(2t)\n$$\n\nhas an amplitude that is proportional to $t,$ which means that the amplitude will increase over time.\n\nThe general solution in this case is\n\n$$\ny(t) = A\\cos(2 t) + B\\sin(2 t) +\\dfrac{t}{4}\\sin(2t),\n$$\n\nand taking into account the initial conditions, we get\n\n$$\ny(t)=\\dfrac{t}{4}\\sin(2t).\n$$\n\nNote that the amplitude of his function grows linearly. The graph is shown below.\n\n![](images/90/9032f93f083a100b14f732ba114e306fd0c9c5e0576ce218c9248c0e140854de.png)\n\n**Important:** Not all forced oscillators experience resonance. In the above example, if we change the forcing function to $f(t) = \\cos(t),$ resulting in the equation\n\n$$\ny''+4y=\\cos(t), \\qquad y(0) =0, \\qquad y'(0) = 0,\n$$\n\n then the amplitude of the particular solution is constant:\n\n$$\ny_p = \\dfrac{1}{3}\\cos(t)\n$$\n\nThere is no resonance in this case since the frequency of the external periodic force $f(t)=\\cos(t)$ does not coincide with the frequency of the complementary solution, which is $\\Omega = 2.$\n\nThe general solution in this case is\n\n$$\ny(t) = A\\cos(2 t) + B\\sin(2 t) +\\dfrac{1}{3}\\cos(t),\n$$\n\nand taking into account the initial conditions, we get\n\n$$\ny(t)=\\dfrac{1}{3}\\left(\\cos(t)-\\cos(2t)\\right).\n$$\n\nAs we can see in the graph below, the amplitude of the solution does not grow.\n\n![](images/0e/0eac5b659d3ea9712a3672eb443227d7c8cee5377ff52d7646ca263dc5e11007.png)" :tutorial/id #uuid "77b16ea3-5ad1-5108-9c77-d314e6e9ddf3" :tutorial/math-academy-id 4069 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "e32b8ecd-fee2-56e7-867d-2ac1f9f9b872" :step/math-academy-id 24279 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148307}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148307 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147448 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147376 :question/id #uuid "bd1d2ef2-9472-5e57-b071-fbbaf80023da" :question/math-academy-id "e-6731"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d29e7ead-5eae-5769-9502-7953d7018782" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048291}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148167} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148178} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186335720} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186357695} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186357696} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186357697} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186357698}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Equations that Represent Forced Harmonic Motion"} :step/id #uuid "39e8e7e0-57a8-511a-aef2-6b002039cb19" :step/math-academy-id 24721 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148317}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148308 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147449 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147377 :question/id #uuid "11a44451-5b9f-5892-b321-925b2a393551" :question/math-academy-id "e-6732"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "76e86899-2abb-5cca-9585-4a97e4630264" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148169} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148170} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186335721} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186335722} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186335723} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186335724} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186357699} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186357700}] :knowledge-point/title "Constructing a Differential Equation Governing a Forced Harmonic Motion"} :step/id #uuid "0dd1a25e-e81d-5bf2-9e52-1d14c32c9689" :step/math-academy-id 24722 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148318}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148309 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147450 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147378 :question/id #uuid "aa77f4ef-67b6-5041-9a3b-1a36ffd5582d" :question/math-academy-id "e-6733"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "813ec1d4-93ec-5a9e-996f-b2e672714927" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048130} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048289} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050378} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050805}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148168} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148204} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348241} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348244} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186357736} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186357737} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186357739} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186357740}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Solutions to Forced Harmonic Motion Equations"} :step/id #uuid "f42f709a-7c4f-5416-bf43-29a32c645bd5" :step/math-academy-id 24723 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148310}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148310 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147451 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147379 :question/id #uuid "20b53dff-43b9-5256-ab7d-67aff56db408" :question/math-academy-id "e-6734"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "5266e491-2718-56a9-b5a7-f4a86a8a01c7" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049099}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148174} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148181} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186335727} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186335728} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348245} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186404686}] :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Position of a Particle Oscillating With Forced Harmonic Motion at a Moment"} :step/id #uuid "dbf9225d-b92c-5540-96eb-190970598cab" :step/math-academy-id 24724} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148317 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148766 :tutorial/content "Remember that, if an object of mass $m$ is attached to a spring with spring constant $k,$ and the damping coefficient is $c,$ then the equation of motion is\n\n$$\nmy'' = -cy'-ky.\n$$\n\nIf there is an external force $F(t),$ then the equation of motion is\n\n$$\nm y'' =-cy' -k y + F(t) .\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "ab390ee6-c457-55d4-a265-9d62a1955ab8" :tutorial/math-academy-id 4169 :tutorial/title "The Equation of Motion for a Forced Spring-Mass System"} :step/id #uuid "62673a11-379c-5680-872b-45a2328762ae" :step/math-academy-id 25340 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148308}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148318 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148767 :tutorial/content "Given a forced harmonic motion equation *without* damping,\n\n$$\ny'' + \\omega^2 y = f(t),\n$$\n\nthe complementary solution can be written in three different forms:\n\n- $y_c = R\\cos{(\\omega t + \\phi_1)}$\n\n- $y_c = R\\sin{(\\omega t + \\phi_2)}$\n\n- $y_c =A\\cos{\\omega t} + B\\sin{\\omega t}$\n\nAlternatively, given a forced harmonic motion equation *with* damping,\n\n$$\ny'' + by' + \\omega^2 y = f(t),\n$$\n\nif the roots of the characteristic equation are $\\lambda = p \\pm \\textrm{i}q,$ then the complementary solution can be written in three different forms:\n\n- $y_c = R e^{p t} \\cos{(q t + \\phi_1)}$\n\n- $y_c = R e^{p t} \\sin{(q t + \\phi_2)}$\n\n- $y_c =e^{p t} \\left( A\\cos{q t} + B\\sin{q t} \\right)$" :tutorial/id #uuid "b4683094-19f8-557e-b0c7-7aad98cab72c" :tutorial/math-academy-id 4170 :tutorial/title "General Solutions to Forced Harmonic Motion Equations"} :step/id #uuid "4be5106f-eac3-59d0-8ca0-5316fe821456" :step/math-academy-id 25341 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148309}}] :activity/title "Forced Oscillators" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186147705}] [{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186153061} :activity/id #uuid "610c3ee9-b5f3-57d8-b82e-cac1f007574a" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048694 :topic/id #uuid "b52b86c6-5073-5d60-a17c-32a308489156" :topic/math-academy-id 2638} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186153061 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186153355 :tutorial/content "Suppose that we want to evaluate the line integral with respect to arc length\n\n$$\n\\int\\limits_Cf(x,y)\\:\\textrm{d}s\n$$\n\nfor some function $f(x,y),$ where $C$ is a curve in the $xy$-plane defined using plane polar coordinates as\n\n$$\nr = r(\\theta),\\qquad \\theta_1 \\leq \\theta \\leq \\theta_2.\n$$\n\nTo calculate this line integral, we need to recall the following facts:\n\n- A polar curve is just a special type of parametric curve in the parameter $\\theta,$ where\n\n$$\nx = r(\\theta)\\cos\\theta, \\quad y = r(\\theta)\\sin\\theta, \\qquad \\theta_1 \\leq \\theta \\leq \\theta_2.\n$$\n\n- The arc length element $\\textrm d s$ in plane polar coordinates is given by\n\n$$\n\\textrm ds = \\sqrt{r^2 + \\left(\\dfrac{\\textrm d r}{\\textrm d \\theta}\\right)^2}\\,\\textrm d \\theta\\,.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above formulas into our line integral, we get the following result:\n\n$$\n\\int\\limits_C f(x,y) \\, \\textrm{d}s  = \\int_{\\theta_1}^{\\theta_2}  f(r\\cos\\theta,r\\sin\\theta) \\,\\sqrt{r^2 + \\left(\\dfrac{\\textrm d r}{\\textrm d \\theta}\\right)^2}\\,\\textrm d \\theta\\,.\n$$\n\n \n\nLet's go through an example in which we apply this result." :tutorial/id #uuid "f970309b-6c25-53f1-a3b5-b094ebe87109" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3987 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "1d46b30d-17ce-5fe6-a7be-dcb1352a6151" :step/math-academy-id 23719 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186153062}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186153062 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152160 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186151949 :question/id #uuid "287a493f-e988-5cef-b0d0-4205d1eaf74e" :question/math-academy-id "e-6313"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "7e68ae67-2175-55a3-ac4a-117a340a32a4" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049041}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152740} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152743} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284544} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284545} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345237}] :knowledge-point/title "Reducing the Line Integral of a Scalar Function Along a Curve to a Definite Integral"} :step/id #uuid "70b202ae-ec41-5eae-8ae1-a2c1f7937280" :step/math-academy-id 23720 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186153063}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186153063 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152161 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186151950 :question/id #uuid "63aa09fb-9839-5f73-9f98-2eb9784ead43" :question/math-academy-id "e-6314"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9964be68-9e22-5d98-b0f8-ae19b6e94c1a" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152599} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152742} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345238} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345242} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345245} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345246} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186364399} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186364406}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Line Integral of a Scalar Function Along a Curve Given in Polar Form"} :step/id #uuid "f2968451-7379-5754-b957-4337d8ab3c16" :step/math-academy-id 23721 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186153064}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186153064 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152162 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186151951 :question/id #uuid "00b29cf2-6a73-5fd6-b8bd-04ba8c5f1a46" :question/math-academy-id "e-6315"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ee6e8ead-8186-5afe-bd66-d11c55b65dd4" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050489} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050929}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152739} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152741} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345247} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345250} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345253} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345254} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186362989} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186363534} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186364413}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Line Integral of a Scalar Function Along a Curve Using Substitution"} :step/id #uuid "15d98898-5aa7-5f1a-9c11-0b4e8416b8d1" :step/math-academy-id 23722}] :activity/title "Line Integrals of Scalar Functions Over Polar Curves" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186152335}] [{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186163036} :activity/id #uuid "fcbebdc5-6295-5f6d-84c8-dd3b196003b8" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049282 :topic/id #uuid "e9af0219-7eb8-5b10-84c1-b7b5e8e30ae2" :topic/math-academy-id 3135} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186163036 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186163308 :tutorial/content "Let's consider the least-squares problem $A\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{b},$ where \n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nRecall that the corresponding least-squares solution is a vector ${\\hat{\\mathbf{x}}}$ such that \n\n$$\n\\Vert  A{\\hat{\\mathbf{x}}} - \\mathbf{b} \\Vert\n$$\n\nis as small as possible.\n\nIn real-world problems, the coefficients stored in $A$ might come from sources that are not equally reliable. For example, the coefficients might be collected from population surveys with different sample sizes. As a result, we might want to assign a greater \"weight\" to the more reliable data.\n\nFor example, suppose we require the weight of the coefficients in the first row to be twice the weights of the other rows. Then, we can re-state our problem by saying that, for the matrix of weights $W,$ given by\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nwe wish to find vectors ${\\hat{\\mathbf{x}}}$ such that \n\n$$\n\\Vert  W (A{\\hat{\\mathbf{x}}} - \\mathbf{b}) \\Vert\n$$\n\nis as small as possible.\n\nTherefore, instead of solving $A\\mathbf x = \\mathbf{b},$ we now need to solve the least-squares problem \n\n$$\nW\\!A\\mathbf x = W\\mathbf{b}.\n$$\n\nTo get the normal equation corresponding to this least-squares problem, we pre-multiply both sides of the equation by $(W\\!A)^T{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(W\\,A)^{T}\\cdot W\\,Ax & =(W\\,A)^{T}\\cdot Wb \\\\ {A}^{T}({W}^{T}W)Ax & ={A}^{T}({W}^{T}W)b\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}{2}^{2} & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & {1}^{2} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & {1}^{2}\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nthe normal equation can be written as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 2 \\\\ -1 & 0 & -1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}4 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 2 \\\\ -1 & 0 & -1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}4 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}9 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 18\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 2 \\\\ -1 & 0 & -1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}4 & -4 \\\\ 1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 2 \\\\ -1 & 0 & -1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}36 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 18\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}9 & -6 \\\\ -6 & 5\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}72 \\\\ -54\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving the final equation, we obtain the least-squares solution $[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ -6\\end{aligned}]$" :tutorial/id #uuid "92c4b629-686d-5377-b646-ffe0dd0d636a" :tutorial/math-academy-id 6076 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "163538b7-47fd-5723-b154-a2b53db447fa" :step/math-academy-id 36972 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186163037}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186163037 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162092 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186161905 :question/id #uuid "2acc65a4-62df-5e61-a3c9-cb8a08e1ff8c" :question/math-academy-id "e-9831"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "af51b0b6-4ae7-5b6a-b151-7f8cb88fa186" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048284}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162349} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162356} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186269151} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186269152} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186269153} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186269154} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186269155} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186363134} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186363412} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186381076} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186381167} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186412659}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Normal Equation for a Weighted Least-Squares Problem"} :step/id #uuid "285c2b46-e74a-5ef0-8ec1-c86989af141c" :step/math-academy-id 36973 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186163038}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186163038 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162093 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186161906 :question/id #uuid "22ff2997-c3c3-560e-afdc-52da342dcc10" :question/math-academy-id "e-9832"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b21c27bd-5c7a-5645-93b5-0af73c59f685" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049557}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162350} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162351} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186269156} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186269157} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186269158} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278195} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186363141} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186363398} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186381083} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186381090} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186381160} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186388511} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186389009} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186411867} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186412658}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Solution to a Weighted Least-Squares Problem"} :step/id #uuid "7800afe1-dfc0-5278-8b64-b02ebb92f823" :step/math-academy-id 36975 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186163045}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186163045 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186163312 :tutorial/content "Recall that if the solution ${\\hat{\\mathbf{x}}}$ of the least-squares problem $A\\mathbf x=\\mathbf{b}$ is unique, then $A{\\hat{\\mathbf{x}}}$ lies in $\\textrm{Col}(A).$ Furthermore, $A{\\hat{\\mathbf{x}}}$ is the vector in $\\textrm{Col}(A)$ that's closest to $\\mathbf b.$ \n\nIn terms of vector spaces, the vector $A{\\hat{\\mathbf{x}}}$ is the orthogonal projection of $\\mathbf{b}$ onto the subspace spanned by the columns of $A.$ Therefore, the error vector $A{\\hat{\\mathbf{x}}} - \\mathbf{b}$ is orthogonal to $\\textrm{Col}(A).$\n\n![](images/3a/3a1c79036954f0e04212892b7e3d79c75f8cd7b81d7785441f090f89a4eb6faf.png)\n\nA similar property holds for weighted least-squares solutions:\n\n> *If the solution ${\\hat{\\mathbf{x}}}$ of the least-squares problem $W\\!A\\mathbf x=W\\mathbf{b}$ is unique, then\n> $A{\\hat{\\mathbf{x}}} - \\mathbf{b}$ is orthogonal to $\\textrm{Col}(A)$ with respect to the weighted dot product defined as \n> \n> \n> $$\n> \\langle \\mathbf{x}, \\mathbf{y} \\rangle_W = \\sum_{i=1}^n w_i^2 x_i y_i,\n> $$\n> \n>  \n> where the $w_i$'s are the weights from the main diagonal of the weight matrix $W.$*\n\nWe previously considered the weighted least-squares problem $W\\!Ax=W\\mathbf{b},$ where\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nIt's easy to show (using the usual techniques) that the unique weighted least-squares solution is given by \n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ -6\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nComputing our error vector $\\boldsymbol{\\varepsilon} = A{\\hat{\\mathbf{x}}} - \\mathbf{b},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}ε & =A\\overset{^}{x}-b \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ -6\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}9 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 18\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}10 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 14\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}9 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 18\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 4 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let $\\mathbf a_1$ and $\\mathbf a_2$ denote the columns of $A\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}| & | \\\\ {a}_{1} & {a}_{2} \\\\ | & |\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nNotice that the error vector $\\boldsymbol{\\varepsilon}$ is *not* orthogonal to the columns of $A\\mathbin{:}$\n\n- Computing $\\boldsymbol{\\varepsilon} \\cdot \\mathbf a_1,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}ε\\cdot {a}_{1} & =⟨[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 4 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]⟩ \\\\  & =(1)(1)+(4)(1)+(-4)(2) \\\\  & =-3 \\\\  & \\ne 0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Computing $\\boldsymbol{\\varepsilon} \\cdot \\mathbf a_2,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}ε\\cdot {a}_{2} & =⟨[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 4 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]⟩ \\\\  & =(1)(-1)+(4)(0)+(-4)(-1) \\\\  & =3 \\\\  & \\ne 0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHowever, if we check the orthogonality with respect to the *weighted* dot product \n\n$$\n\\langle \\mathbf{x}, \\mathbf{y} \\rangle_W = \\sum_{i=1}^n w_i^2 x_i y_i = 4x_1y_1 + x_2y_2 + x_3y_3,\n\\quad\n$$\n\n \n\nwe obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{⟨ε,{a}_{1}⟩}_{W} & ={⟨[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 4 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]⟩}_{W} \\\\  & =4(1)(1)+(4)(1)+(-4)(2) \\\\  & =0\\,✓ \\\\ {⟨ε,{a}_{2}⟩}_{W} & ={⟨[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 4 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]⟩}_{W} \\\\  & =4(1)(-1)+(4)(0)+(-4)(-1) \\\\  & =0\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis means that $A{\\hat{\\mathbf{x}}} - \\mathbf{b}$ is orthogonal to $\\textrm{Col}(A)$ with respect to the weighted dot product. We can write this as\n\n$$\nA{\\hat{\\mathbf{x}}} - \\mathbf{b} \\perp_W \\textrm{Col}(A).\n$$\n\nIn terms of vector spaces, the vector $A{\\hat{\\mathbf{x}}}$ is the orthogonal projection with respect to $\\langle \\mathbf{x}, \\mathbf{y} \\rangle_W$ of the vector $\\mathbf{b}$ onto the subspace spanned by the columns of $A.$ \n\nFurthermore, the weighted least-squares problem with weight matrix $W$ is equivalent to finding a vector ${\\hat{\\mathbf{x}}}$ such that\n\n$$\n\\Vert  A{\\hat{\\mathbf{x}}} - \\mathbf{b} \\Vert_W\n$$\n\nis as small as possible." :tutorial/id #uuid "91e0f414-7785-5748-bba9-04278c6519ad" :tutorial/math-academy-id 6084 :tutorial/title "A Geometric Interpretation of the Weighted Least-Squares Solution"} :step/id #uuid "373b333f-64bb-5518-823a-a53a7c7b2614" :step/math-academy-id 37006}] :activity/title "Weighted Least-Squares" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186162229}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046673} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186412663} :activity/id #uuid "5ae33ea6-f725-5358-bc56-131c7aa58050" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049282 :topic/id #uuid "e9af0219-7eb8-5b10-84c1-b7b5e8e30ae2" :topic/math-academy-id 3135} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186412660 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162350 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "088981d3-114e-574b-937a-924f96c1a577" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{5}{3} \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186412634} {:answer/id #uuid "495b45ed-6e05-5844-806a-6ba5b784755f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{5}{3} \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186412639} {:answer/id #uuid "552d678b-530b-5d1a-bf8b-91ab4eb12691" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186412640} {:answer/id #uuid "e4052908-8f95-52dd-aefc-cb82ac135212" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{4}{3} \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186412649} {:answer/id #uuid "fb119cac-1cdf-5bd7-b4cf-2acfdf9b0565" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186412653}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186412634} :answer-field/id #uuid "fea215fb-04f0-534e-86ff-be0201f49602" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186412657}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "f6bb363c-48b6-5047-89db-8e3fcc537bc7" :question/math-academy-id "q-137102" :question/problem "Find the solution for the weighted least-squares problem $W\\,Ax=Wb,$ where\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{aligned}],\\,b=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,W=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$" :question/worked-solution "To get the normal equation corresponding to $W\\,Ax=Wb,$ we pre-multiply both sides of the equation by $(W\\,A)^{T}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(W\\,A)^{T}\\cdot W\\,Ax & =(W\\,A)^{T}\\cdot Wb \\\\ {A}^{T}({W}^{T}W)Ax & ={A}^{T}({W}^{T}W)b\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since\n\n$$\n{W}^{T}W=[\\begin{aligned}{2}^{2} & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & {2}^{2} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & {1}^{2}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}4 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nour normal equation can be written as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 2 \\\\ -1 & 0 & -1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}4 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 2 \\\\ -1 & 0 & -1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}4 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 2 \\\\ -1 & 0 & -1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}4 & -4 \\\\ 4 & 0 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 2 \\\\ -1 & 0 & -1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 8 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}12 & -6 \\\\ -6 & 5\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}14 \\\\ -5\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since the matrix on the left-hand side is invertible, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & ={[\\begin{aligned}12 & -6 \\\\ -6 & 5\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}[\\begin{aligned}14 \\\\ -5\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{12\\cdot 5-(-6)\\cdot (-6)}[\\begin{aligned}5 & 6 \\\\ 6 & 12\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}14 \\\\ -5\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{24}[\\begin{aligned}40 \\\\ 24\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{5}{3} \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "41c0539c-002c-5259-832d-9bb45ac8b928" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186412662}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186412661 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186412658 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "466cb1d5-bbaa-5bcf-a4b9-8e35b9769bc6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "15" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186412638} {:answer/id #uuid "7390aa1b-1e0b-5b8f-8041-bba7a08ed6ca" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186412642} {:answer/id #uuid "b8d7ad48-ee12-53ed-ae80-3bfaa814df4f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186412647} {:answer/id #uuid "cb0cdbb1-4af3-53da-b876-22b6640991ee" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186412648} {:answer/id #uuid "f47c99c4-37e6-574e-a7cb-b1c519e96e66" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186412650}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186412642} :answer-field/id #uuid "68d36053-187e-5834-b46e-6140de6fde31" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186412655}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "7e019bd1-c120-5401-ab70-85681df29503" :question/math-academy-id "q-137930" :question/problem "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 6 \\\\ -1 & -3 \\\\ 1 & 3\\end{aligned}],\\,b=[\\begin{aligned}-22 \\\\ 11 \\\\ 7\\end{aligned}],\\,W=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nConsider the matrices $A$ and $W,$ and the vector $b$ shown above. If the general solution for the weighted least-squares problem $W\\,Ax=Wb$ is \n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+{x}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}b \\\\ c\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}\\in R,\n$$\n\nwhat is the value of  $a+\\frac{b}{c}?$" :question/worked-solution "To get the normal equation corresponding to $W\\,Ax=Wb,$ we pre-multiply both sides of the equation by $(W\\,A)^{T}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(W\\,A)^{T}\\cdot W\\,Ax & =(W\\,A)^{T}\\cdot Wb \\\\ {A}^{T}({W}^{T}W)Ax & ={A}^{T}({W}^{T}W)b\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since\n\n$$\n{W}^{T}W=[\\begin{aligned}{1}^{2} & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & {2}^{2} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & {1}^{2}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nour normal equation can be written as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 & 1 \\\\ 6 & -3 & 3\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 & 6 \\\\ -1 & -3 \\\\ 1 & 3\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 & 1 \\\\ 6 & -3 & 3\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-22 \\\\ 11 \\\\ 7\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 & 1 \\\\ 6 & -3 & 3\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 & 6 \\\\ -4 & -12 \\\\ 1 & 3\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 & 1 \\\\ 6 & -3 & 3\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-22 \\\\ 44 \\\\ 7\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}9 & 27 \\\\ 27 & 81\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}-81 \\\\ -243\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve the system corresponding to the above matrix equation using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}9 & 27 & -81 \\\\ 27 & 81 & -243\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2}:={R}_{2}-3{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}9 & 27 & -81 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. From the first equation, we have \n\n$$\n{x}_{1}=-9-3{x}_{2}.\n$$\n\nHence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}-9-3{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-9 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+{x}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\na+\\frac{b}{c}=-9+\\frac{(-3)}{1}=-12.\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "4cdf2d74-0bb9-5a46-a586-06014aa796e7"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186412662 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186412659 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "598cbe9b-f2eb-52d5-9e41-b48441ed64d8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "56" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186412641} {:answer/id #uuid "84e93a6b-0108-570f-8119-23d513088357" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "53" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186412643} {:answer/id #uuid "8e9cc8d3-0106-5ea8-86d6-66b4ea64a0cb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "68" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186412644} {:answer/id #uuid "934431cc-66ef-56d1-a66f-d360d9139b16" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "36" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186412645} {:answer/id #uuid "f9cbe89f-eecc-5c0e-b93a-5822e57acd6f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "33" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186412651}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186412641} :answer-field/id #uuid "b982590b-c792-58f1-b493-14196b43933c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186412656}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "8d91806b-81b0-566e-90e0-48dc8925cff9" :question/math-academy-id "q-137886" :question/problem "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}],\\,b=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}],\\,W=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the matrices $A$ and $W,$ and the vector $b$ above. If the normal equation for the corresponding weighted least-squares problem $W\\,Ax=Wb$ is given by\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}a & 20 \\\\ 20 & b\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ c\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nwhat is the value of $a+b+c?$" :question/worked-solution "To get the normal equation corresponding to $W\\,Ax=Wb,$ we pre-multiply both sides of the equation by $(W\\,A)^{T}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(W\\,A)^{T}\\cdot W\\,Ax & =(W\\,A)^{T}\\cdot Wb \\\\ {A}^{T}({W}^{T}W)Ax & ={A}^{T}({W}^{T}W)b\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since\n\n$$\n{W}^{T}W=[\\begin{aligned}{1}^{2} & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & {1}^{2} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & {2}^{2}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 4\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nour normal equation can be written as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 6 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 4\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 6 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 4\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 6 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 6 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 8\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}10 & 20 \\\\ 20 & 40\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\na+b+c=10+40+6=56.\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "887b3834-be22-5f6c-8700-a163d7c04bba" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186412664}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186412663 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162349 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1f5c53a9-a2e1-5c97-ae8a-eca40c100135" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186412635} {:answer/id #uuid "2c6fec5d-06d4-5c30-a152-268334590e76" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "42" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186412636} {:answer/id #uuid "465218a2-69bf-5497-8469-e2312eb7efd0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-19" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186412637} {:answer/id #uuid "9ff085af-9e73-5865-91af-322d3998891a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-27" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186412646} {:answer/id #uuid "fa9d5a16-3904-5615-8861-702cbf9dba03" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "59" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186412652}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186412652} :answer-field/id #uuid "0676c358-31ac-5dc1-99e9-f46cd0df84d6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186412654}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "363b8573-3582-5360-9547-0ffac7abdb28" :question/math-academy-id "q-137101" :question/problem "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 4 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}],\\,b=[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}],\\,W=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the matrices $A$ and $W,$ and the vector $b$ above. If the normal equation for the corresponding weighted least-squares problem $W\\,A=Wb$ is given by\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}a & 18 \\\\ 18 & b\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}c \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nwhat is the value of $a+b+c?$" :question/worked-solution "To get the normal equation corresponding to $W\\,Ax=Wb,$ we pre-multiply both sides of the equation by $(W\\,A)^{T}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(W\\,A)^{T}\\cdot W\\,Ax & =(W\\,A)^{T}\\cdot Wb \\\\ {A}^{T}({W}^{T}W)Ax & ={A}^{T}({W}^{T}W)b\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since\n\n$$\n{W}^{T}W=[\\begin{aligned}{2}^{2} & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & {1}^{2} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & {1}^{2}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}4 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nour normal equation can be written as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 4 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}4 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 4 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 4 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}4 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}1 & 4 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}4 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 4 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 4 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}16 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}24 & 18 \\\\ 18 & 17\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}18 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\na+b+c=24+17+18=59.\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "9b0b78c1-38dc-5b63-a705-26a38e682db9" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186412660}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186412664 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186411867 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "118a9ee1-aa44-5777-9118-5227ee29fe39" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411690} {:answer/id #uuid "343f47c9-bb05-5183-9f14-e8b4fa694501" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411704} {:answer/id #uuid "4d9f0f42-a4f1-5465-a3a2-7a60cda35ef3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411719} {:answer/id #uuid "8cb4a560-b34c-5ecb-b912-d45e89d2bc34" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411753} {:answer/id #uuid "fd063dda-be76-5f0e-93fe-6561f604b438" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411817}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186411719} :answer-field/id #uuid "4272bc52-3700-5a4c-920e-c054cf89452b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411825}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "9a9c63b9-55aa-5f88-8ff0-b14dd8c3b850" :question/math-academy-id "q-137889" :question/problem "Find the solution for the weighted least-squares problem $W\\,Ax=Wb,$ where\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 0\\end{aligned}],\\,b=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}],\\,W=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2\\end{aligned}].\n$$" :question/worked-solution "To get the normal equation corresponding to $W\\,Ax=Wb,$ we pre-multiply both sides of the equation by $(W\\,A)^{T}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(W\\,A)^{T}\\cdot W\\,Ax & =(W\\,A)^{T}\\cdot Wb \\\\ {A}^{T}({W}^{T}W)Ax & ={A}^{T}({W}^{T}W)b\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since\n\n$$\n{W}^{T}W=[\\begin{aligned}{1}^{2} & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & {1}^{2} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & {2}^{2}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 4\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nour normal equation can be written as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 4\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 4\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1 \\\\ 8 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -8\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}17 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 5\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}-15 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since the matrix on the left-hand side is invertible, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & ={[\\begin{aligned}17 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 5\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}[\\begin{aligned}-15 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{17\\cdot 5-2\\cdot 2}[\\begin{aligned}5 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 17\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-15 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{81}[\\begin{aligned}-81 \\\\ 81\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "bf465e2e-a5d2-5c64-9820-18efd555ee8e" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186412661}}] :activity/title "Weighted Least-Squares" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186412633}] [{:activity/expected-seconds 420.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134901} :activity/id #uuid "fc007430-4cc9-562b-9192-4d3c9dccd500" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049312 :topic/id #uuid "eb826c2f-ae04-5de5-b6a6-7efcf7e974ae" :topic/math-academy-id 2201} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134901 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186135151 :tutorial/content "If an $n\\!\\times \\!n$ matrix $A$ has an entire row or column of zeros, then its determinant is equal to zero.\n\nFor example, consider the matrix $A$ given below:\n\n![](images/6b/6bb8b64f7ed8730e641cdf88fc6fe7add40bc15061095c6b1f57c1b764b28feb.svg)\n\nThe second row consists of all zeros, and the asterisks ($\\ast$) may represent any number.\n\nLet's compute the determinant using the Laplace expansion across the second row:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A) & =|\\begin{aligned}∗ & ∗ & ∗ \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ ∗ & ∗ & ∗\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =(-1)^{2+1}(0)|\\begin{aligned}∗ & ∗ \\\\ ∗ & ∗\\end{aligned}|+(-1)^{2+2}(0)|\\begin{aligned}∗ & ∗ \\\\ ∗ & ∗\\end{aligned}|+(-1)^{2+3}(0)|\\begin{aligned}∗ & ∗ \\\\ ∗ & ∗\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =0+0+0 \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that it doesn't matter what numbers are represented by the asterisks ($\\ast$). Each cofactor is multiplied by zero, so the final result must be zero." :tutorial/id #uuid "cee0a552-60f2-5785-a506-63bd053364c7" :tutorial/math-academy-id 6888 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "b68428f5-f5bb-5090-b7f9-4971dc931914" :step/math-academy-id 41745 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134902}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134902 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186135152 :tutorial/content "Now, suppose a matrix has no zero rows but it has two identical rows:\n\n![](images/74/7477781b618ec384b1638d01707acf545e7d9882ffca5bbc0efde4e224b04308.svg)\n\nWe can subtract the $1$st row from the $3$rd row, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+(-1){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =B\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince this operation does not change the determinant, we get $\\det(A) = \\det(B).$ Furthermore, as $B$ contains a zero row, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\det(A) = \\det(B) = 0.\n$$\n\nNotice that we can use this method with columns too. Therefore, we have the following property:\n\n*If a square matrix has at least two identical rows/columns, its determinant equals zero.*" :tutorial/id #uuid "6813d49e-3908-5735-8196-8e1aad5a14e6" :tutorial/math-academy-id 6889 :tutorial/title "Determinants with Identical Rows or Identical Columns"} :step/id #uuid "e105e6ae-64b8-5812-85e8-b5c3ef7b6c7d" :step/math-academy-id 41746 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134903}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134903 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133911 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133466 :question/id #uuid "bb2795c3-6359-50d8-9531-1e9dfae7a2ce" :question/math-academy-id "e-10994"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "28142e26-f6c7-54c9-9c16-035c75f7659f" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048462} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049159}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134395} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134408} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272009} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186329700} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351408} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186400867} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186420351} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186420352}] :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Determinant of a Matrix With A Zero Row/Column or Two Identical Rows/Columns"} :step/id #uuid "f2a27f52-c605-5d01-84c1-49531f59d922" :step/math-academy-id 41747 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134904}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134904 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186135153 :tutorial/content "Now, suppose a matrix has no zero rows and no identical rows but it has two proportional rows:\n\n![](images/81/8181d36ff1378310744b0d74f42f2934eb1e41053f45ecfeacf0c813abe994df.svg)\n\nWe can add three times the $1$st row to the $3$rd row, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 3 \\\\ -3 & -6 & -3\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+3{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =B\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince this operation does not change the determinant, we get $\\det(A) = \\det(B).$ Furthermore, as $B$ contains a zero row, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\det(A) = \\det(B) = 0.\n$$\n\nNotice that we can use this method with columns too. Therefore, we have the following property:\n\n*If a square matrix has at least two proportional rows/columns, its determinant equals zero.*" :tutorial/id #uuid "fe83e8c9-948a-596f-a2d9-c2ae2fe4f335" :tutorial/math-academy-id 6890 :tutorial/title "Determinants with Proportional Rows/Columns"} :step/id #uuid "23e26677-bdeb-5a45-a6d3-4a8d02a72732" :step/math-academy-id 41750 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134905}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134905 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133912 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133467 :question/id #uuid "dc780837-b763-5ba9-981a-2373afcb524a" :question/math-academy-id "e-10995"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "8aef330a-81b3-56c4-9559-67df673e8687" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134394} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134407} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272011} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186329654} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351409} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186402861} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186402868} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186411852} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186415475} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186415477} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186415478}] :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Determinant of a Matrix With Two Proportional Rows/Columns"} :step/id #uuid "93111f5e-5fd2-5a5b-81b1-27969a9b5d1d" :step/math-academy-id 41751 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134906}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134906 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186135154 :tutorial/content "In conclusion, if an $n\\!\\times \\!n$ matrix $A$ satisfies any one of the following conditions, then $\\det(A)=0.$\n\n- $A$ has a zero row/column\n\n- $A$ has two identical rows/columns\n\n- $A$ has two proportional rows/columns\n\n- $A$ has a row/column that is a linear combination of other rows/columns\n\nNotice that condition IV encompasses each of the conditions I, II, and III. If we have a zero row/column, two identical rows/columns, or proportional rows/columns, then the rows/columns form a linearly dependent set of vectors. \nIn turn, this means that one of the rows/columns is a linear combination of other rows/columns.\n\n**Watch out!** While all of the above conditions are *sufficient* to conclude that $\\det(A)=0,$ only condition IV is *necessary* to conclude that $\\det(A)=0.$ \nIn other words, if we have a matrix $A$ with $\\det(A)=0,$ then conditions I, II, and III may or may not be satisfied, while condition IV *must* be satisfied. \n\nSo, we have the following theorem:\n\n*$\\det(A)=0$ if and only if one of the rows/columns of $A$ is a linear combination of other rows/columns of $A.$*\n\nFinally, note also that in the case of a $2\\times 2$ matrix *only*, statements III and IV mean the same thing." :tutorial/id #uuid "9d8b0323-170c-5a60-b372-1a258c4848cc" :tutorial/math-academy-id 6891 :tutorial/title "Conditions When a Determinant Equals Zero"} :step/id #uuid "4aec093b-050b-5263-9ef2-c875455b8c07" :step/math-academy-id 41754 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134908}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134907 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133913 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133468 :question/id #uuid "33ca37e5-c598-52a2-861f-2d5bfa6fd571" :question/math-academy-id "e-10996"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "abaeea03-6a2a-5d37-a2af-a9ea109175ad" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134393} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134414} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272012} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272013} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186329652} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186329653} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186329699} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351410} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186415476} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186415479} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186420353}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Reasons Why a Determinant Equals Zero"} :step/id #uuid "81571aa7-ce3d-5713-bf67-a49538fee0ef" :step/math-academy-id 41755} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134908 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186135155 :tutorial/content "Geometrically, the absolute value of an $n \\times n$ determinant can be interpreted as the volume of the $n$-dimensional parallelepiped spanned by the columns of the determinant. \n\nFor example:\n\n- Suppose the columns of a $3\\times 3$ matrix $A$ are ${\\color{black}\\mathbf{a}}, {\\color{black}\\mathbf{b}},$ and ${\\color{black}\\mathbf{c}}.$\n![](images/6e/6e046341b7a1b8f6760797f7db89a7e021a6ace6b5cdf28bd5217998cb450837.svg)\nThe $3$-dimensional parallelepiped spanned by the columns of $A$ is depicted below:\n\n![](images/25/2597b48a010607fca71c551dbce8e9e2bbeda47d0892a582347ae1cdf19f1bf6.png)\n\nThe volume $V$ of this parallelepiped equals the absolute value of the determinant of $A{:}$\n\n$$\nV = \\left|\\det(A)\\right|\n$$\n\n- Now suppose the columns of a $2\\times 2$ matrix $B$ are ${\\color{black}\\mathbf{p}}$ and ${\\color{black}\\mathbf{q}}.$\n![](images/f5/f593c123eb081da0399e74c665325774758b48c5f086a7475b959905e1de2763.svg)\nThe $2$-dimensional parallelepiped (i.e., the parallelogram) spanned by vectors $\\mathbf{p}$ and $\\mathbf{q}$ is shown below:\n\n![](images/4a/4af1971404b188df75bb3d62d47ac95340c5e1791066e5358370d743230baa45.png)\n\nThe two-dimensional volume $V$ (i.e., the area) of this parallelepiped equals the absolute value of the determinant of $B{:}$\n\n$$\nV = \\left|\\det(B)\\right|\n$$\n\n- In general, the absolute value of the determinant of an $n\\times n$ matrix $M$ gives the $n$-dimensional volume of the parallelepiped spanned by the columns of $M.$\n\nIn this lesson, we studied the following property:\n\n*$\\det(A) = 0$ if and only if one of the columns of $A$ is a linear combination of other columns of $A$.*\n\nWhat does this property mean in geometric terms?\n\nThe component $\\det(A) = 0$ implies that the $n$-dimensional parallelepiped formed by the columns of $A$ has zero volume. Conversely, when one of the columns of $A$ is a linear combination of other columns, it indicates that the corresponding parallelepiped possesses at most $n-1$ distinct dimensions. \n\nConsider the following examples:\n\n- In the case of a $3$-dimensional parallelepiped, if the vector $\\mathbf{c}$ is a linear combination of the vectors $\\mathbf{a}$ and $\\mathbf{b}$, then $\\mathbf{c}$ lies within the plane containing $\\mathbf{a}$ and $\\mathbf{b}$. In other words, the vectors $\\mathbf{a}$, $\\mathbf{b}$, and $\\mathbf{c}$ are coplanar. Consequently, we obtain a \"flat\" ($2$-dimensional) figure with a volume of $0$.\n\n- For the parallelogram example, if the vector $\\mathbf{q}$ is a linear combination of the vector $\\mathbf{p}$ (i.e., $\\mathbf{p}$ and $\\mathbf{q}$ are proportional), then $\\mathbf{q}$ resides on the line containing vector $\\mathbf{p}$. In other words, the vectors $\\mathbf{p}$ and $\\mathbf{q}$ are collinear. This results in a $1$-dimensional object with a $2$-dimensional volume (i.e., area) of $0$." :tutorial/id #uuid "1f7191c6-60ca-5c44-befc-8dbad7779b08" :tutorial/math-academy-id 6893 :tutorial/title "Geometric Interpretation of a Zero Determinant"} :step/id #uuid "8c5dbeea-b2e4-5fe9-9d8c-98750164b70c" :step/math-academy-id 41766 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134907}}] :activity/title "Conditions When a Determinant Equals Zero" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186134042}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046673} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186415480} :activity/id #uuid "d3285aa6-5f09-548a-886c-a1acc2f7b8d6" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049312 :topic/id #uuid "eb826c2f-ae04-5de5-b6a6-7efcf7e974ae" :topic/math-academy-id 2201} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186415480 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186415477 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "022ff781-0bb6-5222-8917-bb9fe088d40f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415445} {:answer/id #uuid "68acb0f6-b335-5e47-b870-8fcc301ed02a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415455} {:answer/id #uuid "a31adf10-60d1-51b5-9022-8cf1940c42be" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415461} {:answer/id #uuid "ebaffa84-7e8a-5edc-8432-6adf9e5b2b42" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415465} {:answer/id #uuid "fae22a7a-c8b8-5a0b-bb2c-5bc815b3b48d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415467}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186415445} :answer-field/id #uuid "d9ec239f-8a57-5ead-b4ff-042aa539477e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415472}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "d4554f29-f5fb-5556-852c-2f9d95422a4d" :question/math-academy-id "q-42464" :question/problem "Find $5det(A)+7det(B)$, where \n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & \\sqrt{3} & 1 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 4 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 0 & 3\\sqrt{3} & 3 \\\\ 2 & 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}],\\,B=[\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{3} & \\sqrt{2} & 0 & 3 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 0 & 3 \\\\ \\sqrt{3} & 1 & 0 & \\sqrt{2} \\\\ 4 & 3 & 0 & 2\\end{aligned}].\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Let's examine each matrix in turn.\n\n- Since $A$ has proportional rows, we obtain\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & \\sqrt{3} & 1 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 4 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 0 & 3\\sqrt{3} & 3 \\\\ 2 & 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\,⟹\\,det(A)=0.\n$$\n\n- Since $B$ has a zero column, we obtain\n\n$$\nB=[\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{3} & \\sqrt{2} & 0 & 3 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 0 & 3 \\\\ \\sqrt{3} & 1 & 0 & \\sqrt{2} \\\\ 4 & 3 & 0 & 2\\end{aligned}]\\,⟹\\,det(B)=0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $5det(A)+7det(B)=5\\cdot 0+7\\cdot 0=0.$"} :step/id #uuid "0922e6fb-1dc5-5a88-a985-bdf2643cb465" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186415484}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186415481 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186415479 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1d00532a-c12b-50b9-ad0b-b9f1fa474794" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415449} {:answer/id #uuid "44173bde-0fab-57de-b1b3-22d94262de8d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415452} {:answer/id #uuid "6b9c1118-9a19-54c3-9683-cf1248dd342b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415456} {:answer/id #uuid "7e862f84-0fe3-5f4e-8829-b54fb65649ca" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415457} {:answer/id #uuid "e9cd19d7-7290-5199-aa60-b1bc0b2d73b0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415464}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186415464} :answer-field/id #uuid "e80361a1-690f-54bf-a979-055f8bec64f8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415473}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "f8a11ae3-b740-5975-a251-a4796d69b742" :question/math-academy-id "q-43307" :question/problem "Let $A$ be a $3\\times 3$ matrix such that $|A|=0.$ Which of the following statements must be true?\n\n- $A$ has a zero column\n- $A$ has two proportional rows\n- One of the rows/columns of $A$ is a linear combination of other rows/columns of $A$" :question/worked-solution "Let's consider our statements in turn.\n\n- Statements I and II are false. For example, consider the matrix \n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 3\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nExpending across the $1$st column, we have that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|A| & =1\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 3\\end{aligned}|+1|\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =(3-2)+(1-2) \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nbut $A$ has neither a zero column nor two proportional rows.\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, we can make use of the following fact:\n\n$|A|=0$ *if and only if one of the rows/columns of $A$ is a linear combination of other rows/columns of $A.$*\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only\"."} :step/id #uuid "73a434b3-1e72-5102-a3b4-f3e485e5088a" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186415482}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186415482 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186415475 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "045918a8-9cd8-5a07-b550-ae46024d9091" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$|A|$ and $|C|$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415446} {:answer/id #uuid "402ee237-56af-5ed0-b68b-15a4eb93a4c5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$|B|$ and $|C|$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415451} {:answer/id #uuid "82c9a685-f3be-5652-9e92-444a23e21632" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$|A|$ and $|B|$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415458} {:answer/id #uuid "f5705fa2-288d-561a-9ae0-435e05884593" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$|A|,$ $|B|,$ and $|C|$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415466} {:answer/id #uuid "fff98d8e-e1e9-59ce-9f45-62d4096f2b19" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$|C|$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415469}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186415466} :answer-field/id #uuid "3898e8c8-305f-5107-8e55-3fa0057b225c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415470}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "135c5d03-e391-5a5b-8227-7d54c7203318" :question/math-academy-id "q-140048" :question/problem "Which of the following determinants are equal to $0?$\n\n$$\n|A|=|\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 2 & 1 & 0\\end{aligned}|,\\,|B|=|\\begin{aligned}1 & \\sqrt{2} & 1 \\\\ \\sqrt{2} & 2 & \\sqrt{2} \\\\ -1 & \\sqrt{2} & 1\\end{aligned}|,\\,|C|=|\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 5 \\\\ -1 & 0 & 3 \\\\ 2 & 0 & 4\\end{aligned}|\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Let's examine each of the given determinants in turn.\n\n- Since $A$ is a triangular matrix, its determinant is given by the product of the elements of the main diagonal:\n\n$$\n|A|=0\\cdot 0\\cdot 0\\cdot 0=0\n$$\n\n- The matrix $B$ has proportional rows: \n\n$$\nB=[\\begin{aligned}1 & \\sqrt{2} & 1 \\\\ \\sqrt{2} & 2 & \\sqrt{2} \\\\ -1 & \\sqrt{2} & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\n \nAs a result, $|B|=0.$\n\n- The matrix $C$ has a zero column: \n\n$$\nC=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 5 \\\\ -1 & 0 & 3 \\\\ 2 & 0 & 4\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nAs a result, $|C|=0.$\n\nTherefore, $|A|,$ $|B|,$ and $|C|$ are equal to $0.$"} :step/id #uuid "7fde0eea-1a95-5e36-bc1d-53171442da99"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186415483 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186415478 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1859d7c5-0d55-5273-a004-f8368886466b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415448} {:answer/id #uuid "4a61a420-d52d-5f1a-b48b-ade03dffdca3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415453} {:answer/id #uuid "5fcc61bd-b9ee-5f9e-8558-651f8ea9c858" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415454} {:answer/id #uuid "851dc3ec-467b-568b-a964-3b5e34b3527e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415459} {:answer/id #uuid "e2e4e722-717d-508c-a98d-112f70b09d9f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "18" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415463}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186415463} :answer-field/id #uuid "f920ea7f-06a7-52e9-8586-d0e6b44af6b3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415474}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "e897ce2c-45dd-5cf3-9d25-ca84c0dd6ea8" :question/math-academy-id "q-42881" :question/problem "Find $3det(A)-det(B)$, where \n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3\\end{aligned}],\\,B=[\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{3} & 2 & 4 \\\\ 0.57 & 3 & 6 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 2\\end{aligned}].\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Let's examine each matrix in turn.\n\n- Since $A$ is a triangular matrix, we have\n\n$$\ndet(A)=2\\cdot 1\\cdot 3=6.\n$$\n\n- Since $B$ has proportional columns, we obtain\n\n$$\nB=[\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{3} & 2 & 4 \\\\ 0.57 & 3 & 6 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 2\\end{aligned}]\\,⟹\\,det(B)=0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $3det(A)-det(B)=3\\cdot 6-0=18.$"} :step/id #uuid "8a73fe86-1dcc-5158-b5d0-cfa5bf0ca01e" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186415481}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186415484 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186415476 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "143f48c0-a939-5a4c-b987-afdbbefec79a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415447} {:answer/id #uuid "22066df8-6293-5194-a873-db4b694c6674" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415450} {:answer/id #uuid "8e60b08a-cd17-575f-8815-735e23870208" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415460} {:answer/id #uuid "b446fc5b-8c89-5d67-acf5-bbcf1be8249d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415462} {:answer/id #uuid "fe594f39-ea49-5697-a493-c1ebeaf625b4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415468}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186415450} :answer-field/id #uuid "44a7cadd-9704-50b9-80ee-df6bc66a727b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415471}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "8d883981-f066-5218-a1bf-dd76574de46e" :question/math-academy-id "q-42469" :question/problem "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}3 & -2.05 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 6 & \\sqrt{7} & 2\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the matrix given above. Identify **all** of the reasons why one can conclude that $|A|=0?$\n\n- $A$ has proportional columns\n- $A$ has identical rows\n- $A$ has a zero row" :question/worked-solution "Let's examine each of the statements in turn.\n\n- $A$ has proportional columns: \n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}3 & -2.05 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 6 & \\sqrt{7} & 2\\end{aligned}]\\,✓\n$$\n\n- $A$ does *not* have identical rows.\n\n- $A$ has a zero row: \n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}3 & -2.05 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 6 & \\sqrt{7} & 2\\end{aligned}]\\,✓\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\""} :step/id #uuid "a995ede1-d1f3-53d5-97cb-aed29dce50f7" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186415483}}] :activity/title "Conditions When a Determinant Equals Zero" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415444}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046660} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186416116} :activity/id #uuid "9cb6054b-7ed5-5992-92b3-318764a5074c" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049312 :topic/id #uuid "eb826c2f-ae04-5de5-b6a6-7efcf7e974ae" :topic/math-academy-id 2201} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186416114 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186415477 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "022ff781-0bb6-5222-8917-bb9fe088d40f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415445} {:answer/id #uuid "68acb0f6-b335-5e47-b870-8fcc301ed02a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415455} {:answer/id #uuid "a31adf10-60d1-51b5-9022-8cf1940c42be" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415461} {:answer/id #uuid "ebaffa84-7e8a-5edc-8432-6adf9e5b2b42" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415465} {:answer/id #uuid "fae22a7a-c8b8-5a0b-bb2c-5bc815b3b48d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415467}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186415445} :answer-field/id #uuid "d9ec239f-8a57-5ead-b4ff-042aa539477e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415472}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "d4554f29-f5fb-5556-852c-2f9d95422a4d" :question/math-academy-id "q-42464" :question/problem "Find $5det(A)+7det(B)$, where \n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & \\sqrt{3} & 1 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 4 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 0 & 3\\sqrt{3} & 3 \\\\ 2 & 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}],\\,B=[\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{3} & \\sqrt{2} & 0 & 3 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 0 & 3 \\\\ \\sqrt{3} & 1 & 0 & \\sqrt{2} \\\\ 4 & 3 & 0 & 2\\end{aligned}].\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Let's examine each matrix in turn.\n\n- Since $A$ has proportional rows, we obtain\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & \\sqrt{3} & 1 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 4 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 0 & 3\\sqrt{3} & 3 \\\\ 2 & 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\,⟹\\,det(A)=0.\n$$\n\n- Since $B$ has a zero column, we obtain\n\n$$\nB=[\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{3} & \\sqrt{2} & 0 & 3 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 0 & 3 \\\\ \\sqrt{3} & 1 & 0 & \\sqrt{2} \\\\ 4 & 3 & 0 & 2\\end{aligned}]\\,⟹\\,det(B)=0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $5det(A)+7det(B)=5\\cdot 0+7\\cdot 0=0.$"} :step/id #uuid "21677cec-5329-5c17-ae68-e4ce9c1eced7" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186416118}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186416115 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272012 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3dc653f0-653a-51c8-9dde-134e1ea1131e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269831} {:answer/id #uuid "63282b85-24f9-5737-88f6-e544d18bead1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "None of the statements are true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270146} {:answer/id #uuid "d283f88f-ed57-5c59-963f-f49777d28ae0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271122} {:answer/id #uuid "db49a675-ce1e-57fa-a849-aff9de416a49" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271188} {:answer/id #uuid "ecb23ab9-9422-51b8-8613-a343d4227bf0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271348}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186271348} :answer-field/id #uuid "cfceea30-a8b2-5f6c-aa6e-a6e0d02cdbfc" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271881}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "f60fdb9d-cf89-59bd-ad65-e9ec096b82df" :question/math-academy-id "q-140049" :question/problem "Let $A$ be a $2\\times 2$ matrix such that $|A|\\ne 0.$ Which of the following statements must be true?\n\n- $A$ is a triangular matrix\n- $A$ does not have identical rows\n- $A$ does not have proportional columns" :question/worked-solution "Let's consider our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. For example, consider the matrix \n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nWe have that \n\n$$\n|A|=1\\cdot 1-(-2)\\cdot 2=5\\ne 0\n$$\n\nbut $A$ is not a triangular matrix.\n\n- Statements II and III are true. Indeed, we can make use of the following fact:\n\n$|A|=0$ *if and only if one of the rows/columns of $A$ is a linear combination of other rows/columns of $A.$*\n\nIn the case of a $2\\times 2$ matrix, this means that one of the rows/columns must be proportional to another row/column.\nHence, if $|A|\\ne 0$ then $A$ doesn't have proportional nor identical rows/columns.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only\"."} :step/id #uuid "5d4a636e-e59e-599e-aff6-d3384708bbf3"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186416116 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186329652 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "005e27bf-cb54-5586-9f97-7b38aad175ed" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326938} {:answer/id #uuid "2bd6a87d-fb02-5806-ae65-64162e9cf218" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327321} {:answer/id #uuid "8de5c3ae-cce3-5e55-9356-16f0393d6b32" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328163} {:answer/id #uuid "df2048ab-aecc-5672-ad82-8817a5b81317" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328888} {:answer/id #uuid "f32e69b1-2499-5293-92c5-f46f9bacf57b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329059}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186327321} :answer-field/id #uuid "49c4b9a1-7b38-5148-a1f8-07d819c935b0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329323}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "fffc6699-6d6e-50bb-8cc3-7d4f0e3f1d17" :question/math-academy-id "q-42460" :question/problem "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{5} & 1 & 5 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 2 & 2 & 4 \\\\ \\sqrt{5} & 1 & 5 & 2 \\\\ -1 & 4 & -1 & 8\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\n \nConsider the matrix given above. Identify **all** of the reasons why one can conclude that $|A|=0?$\n\n- $A$ has a zero row\n- $A$ has two proportional columns\n- $A$ has two identical rows" :question/worked-solution "Let's examine each of the statements in turn.\n\n- $A$ does *not* have a zero row.\n\n- $A$ has two proportional columns:\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{5} & 1 & 5 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 2 & 2 & 4 \\\\ \\sqrt{5} & 1 & 5 & 2 \\\\ -1 & 4 & -1 & 8\\end{aligned}]\\,✓\n$$\n\n- $A$ has two identical rows: \n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{5} & 1 & 5 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 2 & 2 & 4 \\\\ \\sqrt{5} & 1 & 5 & 2 \\\\ -1 & 4 & -1 & 8\\end{aligned}]\\,✓\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\""} :step/id #uuid "72236d0c-8f54-552b-be75-f74f2ec50cbd" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186416114}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186416117 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186415479 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1d00532a-c12b-50b9-ad0b-b9f1fa474794" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415449} {:answer/id #uuid "44173bde-0fab-57de-b1b3-22d94262de8d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415452} {:answer/id #uuid "6b9c1118-9a19-54c3-9683-cf1248dd342b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415456} {:answer/id #uuid "7e862f84-0fe3-5f4e-8829-b54fb65649ca" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415457} {:answer/id #uuid "e9cd19d7-7290-5199-aa60-b1bc0b2d73b0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415464}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186415464} :answer-field/id #uuid "e80361a1-690f-54bf-a979-055f8bec64f8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415473}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "f8a11ae3-b740-5975-a251-a4796d69b742" :question/math-academy-id "q-43307" :question/problem "Let $A$ be a $3\\times 3$ matrix such that $|A|=0.$ Which of the following statements must be true?\n\n- $A$ has a zero column\n- $A$ has two proportional rows\n- One of the rows/columns of $A$ is a linear combination of other rows/columns of $A$" :question/worked-solution "Let's consider our statements in turn.\n\n- Statements I and II are false. For example, consider the matrix \n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 3\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nExpending across the $1$st column, we have that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|A| & =1\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 3\\end{aligned}|+1|\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =(3-2)+(1-2) \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nbut $A$ has neither a zero column nor two proportional rows.\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, we can make use of the following fact:\n\n$|A|=0$ *if and only if one of the rows/columns of $A$ is a linear combination of other rows/columns of $A.$*\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only\"."} :step/id #uuid "cd1aa3f6-63e2-5747-ab7a-9aca6a4ae1ae" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186416115}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186416118 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186402861 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "78b84216-c188-5769-a0cf-2f3c4b5d4fe1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$|B|$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402863} {:answer/id #uuid "47a2ba38-2156-5dd9-922d-70e7a9112796" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$|A|$ and $|C|$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402864} {:answer/id #uuid "bf232768-62b0-5a99-b6ae-c2f1d1ac4e96" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$|C|$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402865} {:answer/id #uuid "5c171a58-43ca-5ac8-896f-be2e38df60c5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$|A|$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402866} {:answer/id #uuid "75bb085b-9153-5df0-9e32-3ce23b5de8fb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$|A|$ and $|B|$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402867}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186402863} :answer-field/id #uuid "a4619bd4-0fd6-5b0e-85d8-4d713cc4997e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402862}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "b44f28c6-ca93-5246-be2b-1a2725d36e07" :question/math-academy-id "q-43057" :question/problem "Which of the following determinants are equal to $0?$\n\n$$\n|A|=|\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 & 0 \\\\ \\sqrt{3} & -6 & 0 \\\\ \\sqrt{5} & 8 & 2\\end{aligned}|,\\,|B|=|\\begin{aligned}1 & \\sqrt{2} & 4 \\\\ 2 & 2\\sqrt{2} & 8 \\\\ 0 & -\\sqrt{3} & 4\\end{aligned}|,\\,|C|=|\\begin{aligned}-1 & \\sqrt{3} & 4 \\\\ 0 & 3 & \\sqrt{3} \\\\ 0 & 0 & 4\\end{aligned}|.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Let's examine each of the given determinants in turn.\n\n- The matrix $A$ is a triangular matrix, hence \n\n$$\n|A|=3\\cdot (-6)\\cdot 2=-36\\ne 0.\n$$\n\n- The matrix $B$ has proportional rows: \n\n$$\nB=[\\begin{aligned}1 & \\sqrt{2} & 4 \\\\ 2 & 2\\sqrt{2} & 8 \\\\ 0 & -\\sqrt{3} & 4\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nAs a result, $|B|=0.$\n\n- The matrix $C$ is a triangular matrix, hence \n\n$$\n|C|=(-1)\\cdot 3\\cdot 4=-12\\ne 0\n$$\n\nTherefore, only $|B|$ is equal to $0.$"} :step/id #uuid "dd405466-fc10-5821-904a-fe28c7027da8" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186416117}}] :activity/title "Conditions When a Determinant Equals Zero" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416113}] [{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186158385} :activity/id #uuid "1d574af2-7331-5f7c-b580-2b1b6004da27" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049435 :topic/id #uuid "f51bc61f-8fa1-5d38-b9a3-c8521db4447e" :topic/math-academy-id 2856} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186158385 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186158506 :tutorial/content "When we have a cube root in an inequality, we can cube both sides of the inequality.\n\nFor example, to solve the inequality\n\n$$\n\\sqrt[3]{x-2} \\leq 4,\n$$\n\nwe can cube both sides and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(\\sqrt[3]{x-2})}^{3} & \\le {4}^{3} \\\\ x-2 & \\le 64 \\\\ x & \\le 66.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reason why we're allowed to cube both sides of an inequality is that cubing preserves the order of numbers. That is to say, if $a < b,$ then $a^3 < b^3.$\n\nTo see this concretely, consider the following order of numbers:\n\n$$\n-2 < -1 < 0 < 0.5 < 1 < 2 < 3\n$$\n\nIf we take the cubes of all the numbers above, they stay in the same order:\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{-8}_{ (-2)^3 } < \\underbrace{-1}_{ (-1)^3 } < \\underbrace{0}_{ 0^3 } < \\underbrace{0.125}_{ (0.5)^3 } < \\underbrace{1}_{ 1^3 } < \\underbrace{8}_{ 2^3 } < \\underbrace{27}_{ 3^3 }\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "372baf4e-1214-5c31-9cee-48762b863105" :tutorial/math-academy-id 4653 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "d46966b9-f04d-56e3-9df8-7ed2c4d8af6b" :step/math-academy-id 468025 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186158386}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186158386 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157442 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157132 :question/id #uuid "813b7b5f-0da8-5a92-b4ff-f7208c5f059f" :question/math-academy-id "e-7654"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "bf6a3dbb-52b3-5384-b640-0fbf29a6cc98" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048784} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049986}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157891} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157894} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186250333} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186294915} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186294921} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186294922} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186354585}] :knowledge-point/title "Solving an Inequality Involving a Cube Root"} :step/id #uuid "fd5a0c08-cb93-5a8d-9608-af5e2a089f47" :step/math-academy-id 468026 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186158387}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186158387 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186158504 :tutorial/content "When we have a square root in an inequality, we *can't* always square both sides. To see why not, consider the following inequality:\n\n$$\n-4 \\lt 3\n$$\n\nIf we square both sides, then we get a false statement:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(-4)^{2} & ≮{3}^{2} \\\\ 16 & ≮9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHowever, if we're comparing a square root to a negative number, there's no need to take the square root. The key is to remember that the square root of a number is always positive.\n\n- If a square root is less than (or equal to) a negative number, then there is no solution.\n\nFor example, if\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{x+1} < -3,\n$$\n\nthen there is no solution because the square root $\\sqrt{x+1}$ can never be negative.\n\n- If a square root is greater than (or equal to) a negative number, then any number we can substitute into the square root will satisfy the inequality. So, the solution is the domain of the square root.\n\nFor example, if\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{x+1} > -3,\n$$\n\nthen the solution consists of all $x$ in the domain of $\\sqrt{x+1}$ because $\\sqrt{x+1}$ is zero or positive for all $x$ in its domain. To find the domain, we recall that the inside of the square root must be greater than or equal to $0{:}$\n\n$$\nx+1 \\geq 0 \\quad \\Rightarrow \\quad x \\geq -1\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "21c64dd2-31f7-5c73-a9b1-a82a67d65615" :tutorial/math-academy-id 4651 :tutorial/title "Comparing a Square Root to a Negative Number"} :step/id #uuid "a7b132c4-8966-5562-a68f-5bcc071c84e8" :step/math-academy-id 468029 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186158388}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186158388 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157443 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157133 :question/id #uuid "bc44bc68-a615-5f25-b686-8a775fcac64c" :question/math-academy-id "e-7655"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "24f2c5a5-dc56-5f78-8138-8f7b3a6280e2" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157890} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157892} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186250334} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186250335} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186294865} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186294923} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186294924} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186294959} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186354587} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186354588} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186354589}] :knowledge-point/title "Solving an Inequality Involving a Square Root Compared to a Negative Number"} :step/id #uuid "1c597805-9cb4-559b-bb63-8db421b0801d" :step/math-academy-id 468030 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186158389}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186158389 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186158505 :tutorial/content "If we have an inequality that compares a square root to a non-negative number, we can square both sides of the equation.\n\nHowever, there is a catch: when we solve the resulting inequality, we must remember to remove extraneous solutions. We can do this by keeping only the solutions that are in the domain of the square root.\n\nFor example, consider the following inequality:\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{x+1} < 3\n$$\n\nIn this inequality, the square root is being compared to a non-negative number $(3).$ So, we can square both sides of the inequality:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(\\sqrt{x+1})}^{2} & <{3}^{2} \\\\ x+1 & <9 \\\\ x & <8\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we must only keep the solutions that are in the domain of the square root. To find the domain of the square root, we recall that the argument of the square root must be greater than or equal to zero:\n\n$$\nx+1 \\geq 0 \\quad \\Rightarrow \\quad x \\geq -1\n$$\n\nOur solution is $x < 8,$ which can be expressed as $(-\\infty, 8).$ But the domain of the square root is $x \\geq -1,$ which can be expressed as $[-1, \\infty).$ To find the solutions that are in the domain of the square root, we take the intersection:\n\n$$\n(-\\infty, 8) \\cap [-1, \\infty) = [-1, 8)\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is $x \\in [-1,8),$ which can also be written as $-1 \\leq x < 8.$" :tutorial/id #uuid "17128b58-d757-54db-8cf9-d8f7b07a2f4b" :tutorial/math-academy-id 4652 :tutorial/title "Comparing a Square Root to a Positive Number"} :step/id #uuid "3f826d84-2a18-5b72-abc0-6924c5df05de" :step/math-academy-id 468034 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186158390}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186158390 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157444 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157134 :question/id #uuid "bca45b70-4e79-5baf-a4be-186366cebdd5" :question/math-academy-id "e-7656"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b9003081-7cf6-5a99-accf-6a69b65db47c" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049597}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157893} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157895} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186234468} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186250336} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186294866} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186294970} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186294972} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186354591} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186354592}] :knowledge-point/title "Solving an Inequality Involving a Square Root Compared to a Positive Number"} :step/id #uuid "db7196bf-9800-52ad-a813-ef174eb1383c" :step/math-academy-id 468035}] :activity/title "Solving Radical Inequalities" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186157501}] [{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167585} :activity/id #uuid "c294b9cd-2169-5715-a0ea-b56c96da8d7d" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049652 :topic/id #uuid "08673d5e-730a-5a5d-82e0-95906c87a855" :topic/math-academy-id 3214} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167585 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186168066 :tutorial/content "When working with normally distributed random variables, we often want to find a value of $z$ that gives rise to a specific probability. \n\nFor example, suppose that $Z\\sim N(0,1),$ and we wish to find the value of $z$ such that\n\n$$\nP(Z\\leq z) = 0.85.\n$$\n\nTherefore, our problem is to find a value of $z$ such that $\\Phi(z) = 0.85.$\n\n![](images/dc/dc2006ce351b075c735f9fc1ace31d5688cd39639a6c526810011b44e631be44.png)\n\nA good place to start is the CDF table for $\\Phi(z)$ around the $z$-values where $\\Phi(z)\\approx 0.85.$ This part of the table is shown below.\n\n| $z$ | $\\, 0.03 \\,$ | $\\, 0.04 \\,$ | $\\, 0.05 \\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\, 1.0 \\,$ | $\\, 0.8485 \\,$ | $\\, 0.8508 \\,$ | $\\, 0.8531 \\,$ |\n| $\\, 1.1 \\,$ | $\\, 0.8708 \\,$ | $\\, 0.8729 \\,$ | $\\, 0.8749 \\,$ |\n| $\\, 1.2 \\,$ | $\\, 0.8907 \\,$ | $\\, 0.8925 \\,$ | $\\, 0.8944 \\,$ |\n\nThere is no listed value of $z$ where $\\Phi(z) = 0.85$ exactly. However, notice that there are values where $\\Phi(z)$ is slightly below and slightly above $0.85{:}$\n\n- $\\Phi(1.03) = 0.8485 < 0.85$\n\n- $\\Phi(1.04) = 0.8508 > 0.85$\n\nTherefore, the required value of $z$ must lie between $1.03$ and $1.04.$ To approximate this value of $z,$ we take the average of these two numbers:\n\n$$\nz = \\dfrac{1.03 + 1.04}{2} = 1.035\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\Phi(1.035) \\approx 0.85.\n$$\n\nTaking the average of two numbers on an interval to find an improved approximation is known as **interval bisection**." :tutorial/id #uuid "2d87d053-8fff-571d-bf83-ee48ea3f1ab9" :tutorial/math-academy-id 4992 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "f9c15c99-f0b6-53f9-baac-f0a2a6d399f6" :step/math-academy-id 31092 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167665}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167665 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166825 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166663 :question/id #uuid "a2b50673-6a4f-5e64-ae16-fdce0819637e" :question/math-academy-id "e-8526"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "99f2c276-2eb0-5fe9-b46d-6cf30547a96e" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050116}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167469} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167485} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284452} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302420} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302422} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360723}] :knowledge-point/title "Estimating the Z-Score for a Given Probability Using Interval Bisection"} :step/id #uuid "84bdbe07-98a7-5c11-87f9-778014cacb5b" :step/math-academy-id 31695 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167692}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167666 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166826 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166664 :question/id #uuid "d1e64fae-04ef-5c7d-9bb0-e81cf06bb672" :question/math-academy-id "e-8528"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a54afd65-a69e-52dd-84eb-7cf936773678" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050867}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167470} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167495} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186304773} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186304774} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186304775} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360724} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360725}] :knowledge-point/title "Using a Percentage Points Table To Find the Z-Score for a Given Probability"} :step/id #uuid "e0a49c13-605f-56c4-93a7-388766c320b3" :step/math-academy-id 31697 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167672}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167672 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166831 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166673 :question/id #uuid "a29f1f2c-104a-53aa-ad55-0c6abde0a359" :question/math-academy-id "e-8590"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "8742ad90-1d33-5b56-a83f-8edd7f2f5aef" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167471} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167472} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284474} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360726} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360727} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360728} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360729} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186411872}] :knowledge-point/title "Using a Percentage Points Table and Symmetry To Find the Z-Score for a Given Probability"} :step/id #uuid "4ec11483-e06e-598e-9c74-73dd2d8f63d0" :step/math-academy-id 31898} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167692 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186168083 :tutorial/content "One final way to find the $z$-score for a given probability is to simply look it up in a **percentage points table**, such as the one below.\n\n| $\\quad p \\quad$ | $0.55$ | $0.60$ | $0.65$ | $0.70$ | $0.75$ | $0.80$ | $0.85$ | $0.90$ | $0.95$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\Phi^{-1}(p)$ | $\\,0.1257\\,$ | $\\,0.2533\\,$ | $\\,0.3853\\,$ | $\\,0.5244\\,$ | $\\,0.6745\\,$ | $\\,0.8416\\,$ | $\\,1.0364\\,$ | $\\,1.2816\\,$ | $\\,1.6449\\,$ |\n\nThis table gives the values of $\\Phi^{-1}(p)$ for different values of $p,$ where $p = \\Phi(z)$ is the cumulative distribution function for the standard normal distribution.\n\nLet's use the table above to find $z$ such that $P(Z \\leq z) = 0.75.$\nFirst, we express the given information in terms of $\\Phi{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(Z\\le z) & =0.75 \\\\ Φ(z) & =0.75\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we are looking for the value of $z$ such that\n\n$$\nz = \\Phi^{-1}(0.75).\n$$\n\nWe look for the column in the table that corresponds to $p=0.75,$ and we find that\n\n$$\np = 0.75 \\quad \\Rightarrow \\quad \\Phi^{-1}(p) = 0.6745.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\nz = \\Phi^{-1}(0.75) = 0.6745.\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "4ca3b764-afc6-5490-a58b-a1932c8fc0d0" :tutorial/math-academy-id 5168 :tutorial/title "Using a Percentage Table To Find the Z-Score for a Given Probability"} :step/id #uuid "c9e425e7-ceba-5289-97b0-ead6c5ca0320" :step/math-academy-id 32468 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167666}}] :activity/title "Percentage Points of the Standard Normal Distribution" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186166998}] [{:activity/expected-seconds 1020.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186163068} :activity/id #uuid "fdff7ccc-5a47-5748-b28e-bcf8de0682a8" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049674 :topic/id #uuid "09e82368-7656-5709-b72f-b4db605d6406" :topic/math-academy-id 3013} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186163068 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186163326 :tutorial/content "Recall that if $X_1, X_2, \\ldots, X_{n}$ is a random sample of size $n$ drawn from a population with mean $\\mu$ and variance $\\sigma^2,$ then the sample mean, denoted $\\overline{X},$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\overline{X} = \\dfrac1n\\sum_{i=1}^n X_i.\n$$\n\nThe sample mean is a random variable whose probability distribution (also called the **sampling distribution**) has an expected value of $\\mu\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\textrm{E}[\\overline{X}] = \\mu\n$$\n\n \n\nIn other words, the sample mean $\\overline{X}$ is an unbiased estimate of the population mean $\\mu.$\n\nLet's now find the variance of the sampling distribution of $\\overline{X}.$ Using the properties of variance for sums of independent random variables, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[\\overset{―}{X}] & =\\text{Var}[\\frac{1}{n}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{X}_{i}] \\\\  & ={(\\frac{1}{n})}^{2}\\cdot \\text{Var}[{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{X}_{i}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{n}^{2}}\\cdot \\text{Var}[{X}_{1}+{X}_{2}+⋯+{X}_{n}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{n}^{2}}\\cdot (\\text{Var}[{X}_{1}]+\\text{Var}[{X}_{2}]+⋯+\\text{Var}[{X}_{n}]) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{n}^{2}}\\cdot ({\\sigma }^{2}+{\\sigma }^{2}+⋯+{\\sigma }^{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{n}^{2}}\\cdot n{\\sigma }^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{{\\sigma }^{2}}{n}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo summarize, we have the following important result:\n\n$$\n\\textrm{Var}[\\overline{X}] = \\dfrac{\\sigma^2}{n}\n$$\n\nIt should be noted that we don't yet know the sampling distribution of $\\overline{X}.$ What we *do* know is that whatever the sampling distribution of $\\overline{X}$ is, it has mean $\\mu$ and variance $\\dfrac{\\sigma^2}{n}.$" :tutorial/id #uuid "58763c8a-23b3-55bb-9b5c-4781a3c271b4" :tutorial/math-academy-id 6276 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "0fb9bf51-5dff-5207-8c2c-54d18ba6afd9" :step/math-academy-id 38034 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186163069}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186163069 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162106 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186161672 :question/id #uuid "486f4292-2f50-5d89-be1a-b3c054b449e2" :question/math-academy-id "e-10082"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "bebcdc0f-dc9b-54ec-9cc5-49b2c046e8b7" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048914} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050327} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050606}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162379} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162380} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186418456} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186418457}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Variance of a Sample Mean"} :step/id #uuid "0828c240-f63f-5c50-a2e8-eba11979ee4c" :step/math-academy-id 38036 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186163086}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186163070 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162107 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186161673 :question/id #uuid "41713dda-2195-5bd5-b0be-941b8188cb25" :question/math-academy-id "e-10083"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "6eac4a2e-cfa8-5476-919e-fd3c26d01d75" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162381} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162382} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186418458} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186418459}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Standard Error"} :step/id #uuid "ae3ab16a-d830-544c-8331-c62d1505d7dd" :step/math-academy-id 38037 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186163087}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186163071 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162108 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186161674 :question/id #uuid "0f59a118-79ce-5746-8917-6f4092dcbc6a" :question/math-academy-id "e-10084"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9453a0f1-029b-5436-94c1-47ee311f6e4d" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049435} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050315} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050383}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162383} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162394} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186410430} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186418454} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186418455}] :knowledge-point/title "Calculating an Appropriate Sample Size"} :step/id #uuid "6c760552-8a9d-54b8-962b-87e6f849c3da" :step/math-academy-id 38040} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186163086 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186163333 :tutorial/content "The standard deviation of the sample mean is called the **standard error** of the sample mean and is denoted $\\textrm{SE}[\\overline{X}]\\mathbin{:}$ \n\n$$\n\\textrm{SE}[\\overline{X}] = \\sqrt{\\textrm{Var}[\\overline{X}]}\n$$\n\nSo, if we have a random sample of size $n$ drawn from a population with mean $\\mu$ and variance $\\sigma^2,$ then the  standard error is given by\n\n$$\n\\textrm{SE}[\\overline{X}] = \\sqrt{\\dfrac{\\sigma^2}{n}} = \\dfrac{\\sigma}{\\sqrt n}.\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "c2217692-f38f-597c-9295-cd25f1d4d026" :tutorial/math-academy-id 6300 :tutorial/title "The Standard Error"} :step/id #uuid "2f745e7f-72c3-508a-96ad-ef71ea659d96" :step/math-academy-id 38149 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186163070}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186163087 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186163334 :tutorial/content "Suppose we have a random sample ![](images/ff/ff40ddf134336ef15bcaf81b5b23aa8fb7c5ea13276e0f86fa6dde15841e0c5d.svg) of size ![](images/fb/fb2a8560bbc7122a4dd4f1a0bb9862d4368a63f4b03968884a3a60e7423a852c.svg) from a population with mean ![](images/75/75d2faaecd7646e08d87276399a471f8f713fbeda95e358e94410ac2e23b42a5.svg) and variance ![](images/1a/1af459c3f0307649888db8b148eef968a9c5da6a77788c4ab7fe32d6ea30717e.svg) We know that the sample mean \n\n![](images/51/513b88bd8fa93336cc949758da79cac238f871b477d1682ab6b20635a4149361.svg)\n\nis an unbiased estimator of ![](images/98/9878f65a2daf16e1fbd2b0c4929e4407c4ddc406c147dab8a5f6b1a8ee25af33.svg) \n\nHowever, it should be noted that selecting a single observation ![](images/3c/3c8b064868e0622effd6b83427f453ecdb3e033319c9e02969bb988b22ec7cb6.svg) to represent the whole could *also* be considered an unbiased estimator of the population mean. Recall that, by assumption\n\n![](images/e7/e7cc0631f5e6b060f0b9fc77dcac428c2b853d0e19be2e9b20e6e9f8d5d7aaad.svg)\n\nIn what sense is ![](images/84/84269b1ed65c2a169ac4246065d27cbba714010309ddb03a65f7fa878b7b65ea.svg) a \"better\" estimator of ![](images/75/75d2faaecd7646e08d87276399a471f8f713fbeda95e358e94410ac2e23b42a5.svg) than, say, ![](images/c3/c3bb2b6f3769acd8b9c9ce9f929b5bc6c7ec840ab18503cf4c878a1fe8f8f41d.svg) The answer lies in their respective variances. \n\nAs the sample size ![](images/fb/fb2a8560bbc7122a4dd4f1a0bb9862d4368a63f4b03968884a3a60e7423a852c.svg) becomes larger and larger, the variance and standard error of ![](images/84/84269b1ed65c2a169ac4246065d27cbba714010309ddb03a65f7fa878b7b65ea.svg) become smaller and smaller. It is easy to see that, as ![](images/f4/f4d85c2aac179db9322aef2478cfa080e9d602f8aeb8c0436f646f0e2321c139.svg)\n we have\n![](images/d5/d5676c70b4fcb7f68704ba0bb46fc262c7d8f764748e252aa989d6e82e88283b.svg)\n\nSo, when the sample size ![](images/fb/fb2a8560bbc7122a4dd4f1a0bb9862d4368a63f4b03968884a3a60e7423a852c.svg) is large, there is less variance in the sampling distribution of ![](images/dc/dc01b2699b68bb8be585039efc249eaeeba5e4ad17a5c687a8811bf266cabc87.svg) Therefore, the larger the sample, the more likely it is that ![](images/84/84269b1ed65c2a169ac4246065d27cbba714010309ddb03a65f7fa878b7b65ea.svg) will give good estimates for ![](images/98/9878f65a2daf16e1fbd2b0c4929e4407c4ddc406c147dab8a5f6b1a8ee25af33.svg) The individual sample elements do not have this property since ![](images/63/631e2156cfdd9541694e1238fb8c717e721e0ef452de4bb898beb2c4509abc6b.svg)\n\nThe key takeaway is that the sample mean ![](images/84/84269b1ed65c2a169ac4246065d27cbba714010309ddb03a65f7fa878b7b65ea.svg) becomes a better estimator for the corresponding population mean ![](images/75/75d2faaecd7646e08d87276399a471f8f713fbeda95e358e94410ac2e23b42a5.svg) as the sample size ![](images/fb/fb2a8560bbc7122a4dd4f1a0bb9862d4368a63f4b03968884a3a60e7423a852c.svg) increases." :tutorial/id #uuid "2fc54f89-7780-558c-8193-28d92a025bc8" :tutorial/math-academy-id 6301 :tutorial/title "The Sample Mean and Large Samples"} :step/id #uuid "c79d4341-23ea-55d9-84aa-e073291790d1" :step/math-academy-id 38150 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186163071}}] :activity/title "Variance of Sample Means" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186162169}] [{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148386} :activity/id #uuid "e3ff63f4-b713-5986-8c6d-ad2b66c650aa" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049741 :topic/id #uuid "10441679-94f4-5ae1-8bff-8e5945144d9c" :topic/math-academy-id 2532} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148386 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148769 :tutorial/content "The **phase line** of an autonomous differential equation\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x} = f(y)\n$$\n\nis a vertical $y$-axis marked with equilibrium values and arrows indicating whether solutions increase or decrease in each interval.\n\nSince the rate of change depends only on $y$ (and not on $x$), we can sketch a one-dimensional diagram that describes how the system behaves in different intervals of $y.$\n\nLet's see a concrete example and construct the phase line for the autonomous differential equation\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x} = (y+1)(y-1).\n$$\n\nTo draw the phase line of the differential equation, we proceed using the following three steps:\n\n**Step 1**: Find the equilibrium solutions of the differential equation. \n\nIn this case, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(y+1)(y-1) & =0 \\\\ y & =-1,1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equilibrium solutions are $y=-1$ and $y=1.$\n\n**Step 2**: Find the intervals of $y$ for which $f(y)>0$ and $f(y) < 0.$ \n\nConstructing a sign table, we get the following:\n\n|  | $(-\\infty,-1)$ | $(-1,1)$ | $(1,\\infty)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $y+1$ | ${\\color{red}\\boldsymbol{-}}$ | ${\\color{blue}\\boldsymbol{+}}$ | ${\\color{blue}\\boldsymbol{+}}$ |\n| $y-1$ | ${\\color{red}\\boldsymbol{-}}$ | ${\\color{red}\\boldsymbol{-}}$ | ${\\color{blue}\\boldsymbol{+}}$ |\n| $f(y)$ | ${\\color{blue}\\boldsymbol{+}}$ | ${\\color{red}\\boldsymbol{-}}$ | ${\\color{blue}\\boldsymbol{+}}$ |\n\nSo, we have that $f(y)$ is positive for $y\\in (-\\infty, -1) \\cup (1, \\infty),$ and negative for $y\\in (-1,1).$\n\n**Step 3**: Draw the phase line using the following recipe:\n\n- Draw a vertical $y$-axis\n\n- Mark the equilibrium solutions\n\n- Draw arrows pointing up in the intervals where $f(y) > 0$\n\n- Draw arrows pointing down in the intervals where $f(y) < 0$\n\nApplying the above recipe, we get the following phase line for our differential equation.\n\n![](images/98/9861bea513671d39d85e1f0bafc975118613daeb7dd399e1cf9108a078062f4b.png)" :tutorial/id #uuid "228d941e-82e9-5159-b563-975a96888cd6" :tutorial/math-academy-id 5569 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "d34ff0d2-946a-52ae-a32a-d005e7eb06c4" :step/math-academy-id 593578 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148388}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148387 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147490 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147384 :question/id #uuid "adebd9bd-412d-5e0e-ab21-0087a5c35d9c" :question/math-academy-id "e-8535"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "36070a0f-0b40-5fa6-9357-9f0c754b00a4" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049905}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148219} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148224} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186311622} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186362246} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186362247} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186368168} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186368934} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186395343} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186395350}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Phase Lines From Graphs"} :step/id #uuid "43a23b93-83e7-52e1-89aa-90625941dc2e" :step/math-academy-id 593579 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148390}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148388 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147491 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147382 :question/id #uuid "842d6fb8-d895-584f-9a49-5d90409f1ba2" :question/math-academy-id "e-8533"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "66924d52-fd39-5308-b78e-611e3d3129ad" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049863} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186051068}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148215} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148225} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186362298} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186362299} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186362302} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186395521} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186395528}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Phase Line That Corresponds to a Differential Equation"} :step/id #uuid "5e6a17ba-d700-510e-b2d5-945ee4fa25e5" :step/math-academy-id 593582 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148439}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148389 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147492 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147388 :question/id #uuid "fba1d686-de7e-5e8e-ae0b-1f4f04fa79ba" :question/math-academy-id "e-8586"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "6e71c0c9-7dd1-5f09-a55c-96b5bed1ee9a" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148216} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148226} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186237797} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186311626} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186362303} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186362304} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186362305} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186362306} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186368948} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186368955}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying a Differential Equation Corresponding to a Phase Line"} :step/id #uuid "4de9a841-9218-5356-bf72-17107cf67bd9" :step/math-academy-id 593585 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148387}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148390 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148770 :tutorial/content "Below is the slope field for the autonomous differential equation\n$\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x} = (y+1)(y-1).$\n\n![](images/a5/a51eadb566c1d01ea3973914decec266e7253797c0308e7a6c0a2ddf3baa5bbd.png)\n\nNotice that for autonomous differential equations, the slope marks are invariant horizontally; that is, any two points with the same $y$-coordinate have the same slope.\n\nTherefore, drawing more than one vertical line of slope marks is redundant, since each vertical line is identical.\n\nInstead of drawing the full slope field, we can represent all the necessary information on a single vertical line. This is the **phase line**.\n\nThe diagram below shows how the slope field for\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x} = \\underbrace{(y+1)(y-1)}_{\\large f(y)}\n$$\n\ncorresponds to its phase line.\n\n![](images/4c/4cc5ca68d8e7588b809c0e87b24915669fb0d62c333a9ea75d62098f097de972.png)\n\nThe direction of the arrows on the phase line is determined by the sign of $f(y)$:\n\n- If $f(y) > 0$ on an interval, the slopes are positive ($\\dfrac{dy}{dx} > 0$), and solutions $y(x)$ increase. The arrow on the phase line points **up**.\n\n- If $f(y) < 0$ on an interval, the slopes are negative ($\\dfrac{dy}{dx} < 0$), and solutions $y(x)$ decrease. The arrow on the phase line points **down**." :tutorial/id #uuid "fc44d777-d50e-5f1a-85d7-d7994ec043fc" :tutorial/math-academy-id 5570 :tutorial/title "Connection Between Phase Lines and Slope Fields"} :step/id #uuid "287daa69-be4c-5b66-8276-bbc42b6247a1" :step/math-academy-id 593588 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148391}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148391 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147493 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147383 :question/id #uuid "45254b75-3db0-5c50-970a-4eea7db883f6" :question/math-academy-id "e-8534"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c72cc6e0-deae-51fc-a5e4-b27fcb6f7892" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049722}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148217} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148218} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186311647} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186362265} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186362266} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186368941} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186395396} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186395403} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186395410}] :knowledge-point/title "Phase Lines and Slope Fields"} :step/id #uuid "6a27dd7b-c25f-53bc-bd43-6ca382e43421" :step/math-academy-id 593589} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148439 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148669 :tutorial/content "Suppose that we know that the autonomous differential equation\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x} = f(y)\n$$\n\nhas the following phase line:\n\n![](images/83/83d1a80bef714ffe5806d0247c7f5c1aebe0b5c2aeb8270baf730b190080446a.png)\n\nWhat does this phase line tell us about the function $f(y)$?\n\nWe can interpret the key features of the phase line as follows:\n\n- The solid dots are the **equilibrium solutions**, where \n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x} = f(y) = 0.\n$$\n\nFrom the diagram, the equilibrium solutions are $y=-1$ and $y=2$.\n\n- The arrows show the sign of $\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x} = f(y)$ on the intervals between the equilibrium solutions:\n\n- On the interval $(-\\infty, -1),$ the arrow points down, so the derivative $f(y)$ is *negative*.\n\n- On the interval $(-1, 2),$ the arrow points up, so the derivative $f(y)$ is *positive*.\n\n- On the interval $(2, \\infty),$ the arrow points up, so the derivative $f(y)$ is *positive*.\n\nThe graph of a *possible* derivative function $f(y)$ looks as follows.\n\n![](images/06/06e2c08b823a49d069e4eaa23b8642eb8c17f08fea437ebf213abc6fe737134d.png)\n\nOn the next slide, we will see how we can use the given phase line to identify the corresponding differential equation." :tutorial/id #uuid "c7520b35-b0a6-5676-898b-1b8dd7163a6f" :tutorial/math-academy-id 14933 :tutorial/title "Analyzing Properties of a Differential Equation Given Its Phase Line"} :step/id #uuid "485ce960-e9f1-5aa4-8ec8-b8aa608d874b" :step/math-academy-id 610176 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148389}}] :activity/title "Phase Lines of First-Order ODEs" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186147711}] [{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167900} :activity/id #uuid "eed4b5fc-95ba-5979-9241-b92cdec87053" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049830 :topic/id #uuid "1725b401-08f0-5bd3-9105-3249e65bebf9" :topic/math-academy-id 3211} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167900 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186168037 :tutorial/content "In a homogeneous boundary value problem (BVP) that depends on a parameter $\\lambda$, most values of $\\lambda$ lead only to the trivial solution $y\\equiv 0$.  \n\nThe special values of $\\lambda$ that produce *nontrivial* solutions are called **eigenvalues**, and the corresponding nontrivial solutions are called **eigenfunctions**.\n\nFor example, consider the boundary value problem\n\n$$\ny'' - 18\\lambda y' + 81\\lambda^2y  = 0, \\quad y(0) = 0, \\quad 4y(3) + 5y'(3) = 0, \\quad 0 \\leq x \\leq 3, \\quad \\lambda \\in\\mathbb R.\n$$\n\nLet's find the eigenvalues and eigenfunctions of this problem. \n\nThe method for solving the differential equation depends on whether $\\lambda = 0$ or $\\lambda \\neq 0.$ To find the eigenvalues, we must therefore investigate these two possibilities as separate cases.\n\nOn the next slide, we will analyze the case where $\\lambda = 0.$" :tutorial/id #uuid "1378993d-8bac-50d2-b1ea-4f7c3a1f5ec2" :tutorial/math-academy-id 14646 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "887046eb-da38-509e-bc00-d45adf4aa497" :step/math-academy-id 592744 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167959}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167901 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166939 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166566 :question/id #uuid "877e1518-96ed-5b31-9e05-22990ce1cb72" :question/math-academy-id "e-21808"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ba4d320f-517e-5dc6-9c6d-02d1c2488472" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049937} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050433} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050906}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167268} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167269} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186314743} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186314744} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186365845} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186365858} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186397210} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186397223} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186397252}] :knowledge-point/title "Exponential Eigenfunctions"} :step/id #uuid "4251a9bf-5cd3-5cfc-ae56-c1aa466b5998" :step/math-academy-id 592745 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167903}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167902 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166940 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166565 :question/id #uuid "738bd502-dec6-589b-b198-5040fe7e3906" :question/math-academy-id "e-21806"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "472cbb79-e22b-50d2-a997-93130ff66c22" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048289} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049936} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049937}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167266} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167267} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186314745} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186314746} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186365869} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186391695} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186391709} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186391720} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186397263} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186413439}] :knowledge-point/title "Trigonometric Eigenfunctions"} :step/id #uuid "dc1ec685-f38d-51a2-9240-4f3a1f1346e1" :step/math-academy-id 592748} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167903 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186168038 :tutorial/content "When the auxiliary equation has purely imaginary roots, the solutions are trigonometric, and the boundary conditions usually force $\\lambda$ to take on a discrete set of values.\nSo, we again look for values of $\\lambda$ that allow a nontrivial solution.\n\nWe will now find the **eigenvalues** and **eigenfunctions** for the boundary value problem:\n\n$$\ny'' + 9\\lambda^2 y = 0, \\qquad y'(0) = 0, \\quad y(4) = 0, \\quad 0 \\leq x \\leq 4, \\quad \\lambda \\geq 0.\n$$\n\nWe consider two cases based on the value of $\\lambda,$ since they correspond to different forms of the general solution.\n\n**Case 1: $\\lambda = 0$**\n\nSetting $\\lambda = 0$ gives the equation $y'' = 0.$\nIntegrating this equation twice gives the general solution:\n\n$$\ny = Ax + B.\n$$\n\nTo apply the boundary conditions, we first find the derivative, $y' = A.$\n\n- Applying $y'(0) = 0$ gives $A = 0.$ The solution becomes $y = B.$\n\n- Applying $y(4) = 0$ then gives $B = 0.$\n\nThis yields the trivial solution $y \\equiv 0.$ Since an eigenfunction must be nontrivial, $\\lambda = 0$ is *not* an eigenvalue.\n\n**Case 2: $\\lambda > 0$**\n\nThe auxiliary equation is $m^2 + 9\\lambda^2 = 0,$ which has roots $m = \\pm 3i\\lambda.$\nThe general solution is therefore\n\n$$\ny = A\\cos (3\\lambda x) + B\\sin (3\\lambda x).\n$$\n\nNow we apply the boundary conditions. First, we find the derivative:\n\n$$\ny' = -3A\\lambda\\sin(3\\lambda x) + 3B\\lambda\\cos(3\\lambda x).\n$$\n\n- Applying the condition $y'(0) = 0$ yields:\n\n$$\n-3A\\lambda\\sin(0) + 3B\\lambda\\cos(0) = 0 \\quad\\implies\\quad 3B\\lambda = 0.\n$$\n\nSince we are in the case where $\\lambda > 0,$ this requires $B = 0.$ The solution simplifies to:\n\n$$\ny = A\\cos (3\\lambda x).\n$$\n\n- Next, applying the condition $y(4) = 0$ yields:\n \n\n$$\nA\\cos (12\\lambda) = 0.\n$$\n\n For a nontrivial solution, we must have $A \\neq 0,$ which means we require\n \n\n$$\n\\cos (12\\lambda) = 0.\n$$\n\n This condition is met when $12\\lambda$ is an odd multiple of $\\pi/2:$\n\n$$\n12\\lambda = \\dfrac{(2n+1)\\pi}{2}, \\quad \\text{for } n = 0, 1, 2, \\dots\n$$\n\nSolving for $\\lambda$ gives the set of **eigenvalues**:\n\n$$\n\\lambda_n = \\dfrac{(2n+1)\\pi}{24}.\n$$\n\nThe corresponding **eigenfunctions** are \n\n$$\ny_n(x) = A_n \\cos(3\\lambda_n x) , \\quad A_n \\in \\mathbb{R}.\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "2e52b01a-4f53-5d27-9984-dd731308d87d" :tutorial/math-academy-id 14650 :tutorial/title "Trigonometric Eigenfunctions"} :step/id #uuid "3e7ba88a-596c-53db-b70b-6b89ee975318" :step/math-academy-id 592751 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167902}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167958 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186168055 :tutorial/content "Next, we consider the case $\\lambda \\neq 0.$\n\nTo find the fundamental solutions of \n\n$$\ny'' - 18\\lambda y' + 81\\lambda^2y  = 0,\n$$\n\nfor $\\lambda \\neq 0,$ we set $y = e^{m x}.$ The auxiliary equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{m}^{2}-18\\lambda m+81{\\lambda }^{2} & =0 \\\\ (m-9\\lambda )(m-9\\lambda ) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives a repeated root $m = 9\\lambda.$ The general solution is\n\n$$\ny = Ae^{9\\lambda x} + Bxe^{9\\lambda x}.\n$$\n\nNow we apply the boundary conditions.\n\n- Applying the first boundary condition, $y(0) = 0,$ yields\n\n$$\ny(0) = Ae^{0} + B(0)e^{0} = 0 \\quad \\implies \\quad A = 0.\n$$\n\nSo, the solution simplifies to $y = Bxe^{9\\lambda x}.$ Its derivative is\n\n$$\ny' = Be^{9\\lambda x} + 9B\\lambda xe^{9\\lambda x}.\n$$\n\n- Applying the second boundary condition, $4y(3) + 5y'(3) = 0,$ yields\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4(3B{e}^{27\\lambda })+5(B{e}^{27\\lambda }+27B\\lambda {e}^{27\\lambda }) & =0 \\\\ 12B{e}^{27\\lambda }+5B{e}^{27\\lambda }+135B\\lambda {e}^{27\\lambda } & =0 \\\\ B{e}^{27\\lambda }(17+135\\lambda ) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor a nontrivial solution (an eigenfunction), we require $B\\neq 0.$ Since $e^{27\\lambda}$ is never zero, we must have\n\n$$\n17+135\\lambda = 0 \\quad \\implies \\quad \\lambda = -\\dfrac{17}{135}.\n$$\n\nThis is our **eigenvalue**. The corresponding **eigenfunction** is\n\n$$\ny(x) = Bxe^{9(-17/135) x} = Bxe^{-17x/15}.\n$$\n\nNote that any nonzero constant multiple of this function is also an eigenfunction." :tutorial/id #uuid "f4d9fb35-7991-5b49-bfd6-c82ad2ef1c69" :tutorial/math-academy-id 14892 :tutorial/title "Non-Trivial Solution of the Eigenvalue Problem"} :step/id #uuid "81f0071e-ac02-5b8f-ba91-88209136e903" :step/math-academy-id 608067 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167901}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167959 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186168056 :tutorial/content "We begin by considering  the case $\\lambda = 0.$\n\nSetting $\\lambda = 0$ in the original equation, $y'' - 18\\lambda y' + 81\\lambda^2y  = 0,$ gives a much simpler equation:\n\n$$\ny'' = 0.\n$$\n\nIntegrating this equation twice with respect to $x$ gives the general solution\n\n$$\ny = Ax + B.\n$$\n\nNow we apply the boundary conditions. \n\n- The first condition, $y(0) = 0,$ implies:\n\n$$\ny(0) = A(0) + B = 0 \\quad \\implies \\quad B=0.\n$$\n\nSo the solution must be of the form $y = Ax.$\n\n- For the second condition, we first find the derivative, $y' = A.$ Applying the condition $4y(3) + 5y'(3) = 0$ yields\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4(A\\cdot 3)+5(A) & =0 \\\\ 12A+5A & =0 \\\\ 17A & =0\\,\\,⟹\\,\\,A=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince both $A=0$ and $B=0,$ we are left with the trivial solution $y \\equiv 0.$ By definition, an eigenfunction must be a *nontrivial* solution.\n\nTherefore, $\\lambda = 0$ is *not* an eigenvalue. \n\nNext, we will check the case where $\\lambda \\neq 0.$" :tutorial/id #uuid "b3458843-3f77-548b-b991-69c3d163d75d" :tutorial/math-academy-id 14893 :tutorial/title "Trivial Solution of the Eigenvalue Problem"} :step/id #uuid "d95ffe14-fb14-518a-ac26-6970a9d57acf" :step/math-academy-id 608068 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167958}}] :activity/title "Eigenvalues and Eigenfunctions of Homogeneous BVPs" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186166995}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046660} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186418250} :activity/id #uuid "eea1e25f-6efb-5cef-9851-420bd75de37e" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049830 :topic/id #uuid "1725b401-08f0-5bd3-9105-3249e65bebf9" :topic/math-academy-id 3211} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186418250 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186365858 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0ee92df8-a96b-5333-ae8d-b489df88c366" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\pm \\frac{1}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365860} {:answer/id #uuid "6f3b7016-4934-590e-b25b-b91ebf2125b9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$-\\frac{1}{5}$ and $\\frac{1}{10}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365861} {:answer/id #uuid "acc5b921-33a3-53bb-931e-2dc15cff77d8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$-\\frac{1}{10}$ and $\\frac{1}{5}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365862} {:answer/id #uuid "3b8a4734-03dd-59ba-8bf2-3aa5143e433c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\pm \\frac{1}{10}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365863}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186365861} :answer-field/id #uuid "76d0668d-3ca7-569b-925d-2e956888c08a" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365859} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8f73ef24-52a4-5a30-9cf7-ddacd6a35251" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$Bx{e}^{-x}$ and $C{e}^{x/2}(x-3)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365865} {:answer/id #uuid "7a1b1386-fefe-522a-9724-c7282fdf6830" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$Bx{e}^{x}$ and $C{e}^{-x/2}(x+4)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365866} {:answer/id #uuid "e9883a8c-7a90-5bdd-a094-5cb18a2dfc22" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$Bx{e}^{-x}$ and $C{e}^{x/2}(x+\\frac{3}{2})$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365867} {:answer/id #uuid "8e3aea8a-e9e7-5796-bf9a-ff68da034ea5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$Bx{e}^{-2x}$ and $C{e}^{x}(x-4)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365868}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186365865} :answer-field/id #uuid "4984fab2-6b76-5c85-a24b-7e17e049b22f" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365864}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "c6e87982-ab6f-5e96-94e7-996ae7f3b605" :question/math-academy-id "q-338845" :question/problem "Consider the boundary value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}-10\\lambda {y}^{′}+25{\\lambda }^{2}y=0,\\,{y}^{′}(1)=0,\\,y(2)+2{y}^{′}(2)=0,\\,1\\le x\\le 2,\\,\\lambda \\in R.\n$$\n\nFind the eigenvalues and eigenfunctions of this problem. Assume that $B$ and $C$ are constants.\n\n$\\,$ The eigenvalues are $\\lambda =$ {{field-1}}.\n\n$\\,$ The eigenfunctions are $y=$ {{field-2}}." :question/worked-solution "We consider the cases $\\lambda =0$ and $\\lambda \\ne 0$ separately, since they correspond to different solutions. \n\n**Case 1**: First, we consider $\\lambda =0.$ \n\nSetting $\\lambda =0$ gives the equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}=0.\n$$\n\nIntegrating this equation gives\n\n$$\ny=Ax+B.\n$$\n\nApplying the conditions ${y}^{′}(1)=0$ and $y(2)+2{y}^{′}(2)=0$ yields the trivial solution $y≡0.$ Since the zero solution is not an eigenfunction, $\\lambda =0$ is not an eigenvalue.\n\n**Case 2**: Next, we consider $\\lambda \\ne 0.$\n\nTo find the fundamental solutions, we set $y={e}^{mx}.$ The auxiliary equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{m}^{2}-10\\lambda m+25{\\lambda }^{2} & =0 \\\\ (m-5\\lambda )(m-5\\lambda ) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the double root $m=5\\lambda .$ So, the fundamental solutions are \n\n$$\n{y}_{1}(x)={e}^{5\\lambda x},\\,{y}_{2}(x)=x{e}^{5\\lambda x}\n$$\n\nand the general solution is\n\n$$\ny=A{e}^{5\\lambda x}+Bx{e}^{5\\lambda x}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =5A\\lambda {e}^{5\\lambda x}+B{e}^{5\\lambda x}+5B\\lambda x{e}^{5\\lambda x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the boundary condition ${y}^{′}(1)=0$ yields\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5A\\lambda {e}^{5\\lambda }+B{e}^{5\\lambda }+5B\\lambda {e}^{5\\lambda } & =0 \\\\ 5A\\lambda +B+5B\\lambda  & =0 \\\\ (5\\lambda )A+(1+5\\lambda )B & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the boundary condition $y(2)+2{y}^{′}(2)=0$ yields\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A{e}^{10\\lambda }+2B{e}^{10\\lambda }+2(5A\\lambda {e}^{10\\lambda }+B{e}^{10\\lambda }+10B\\lambda {e}^{10\\lambda }) & =0 \\\\ A+2B+2(5A\\lambda +B+10B\\lambda ) & =0 \\\\ A+2B+10A\\lambda +2B+20B\\lambda  & =0 \\\\ (1+10\\lambda )A+(4+20\\lambda )B & =0 \\\\ (1+10\\lambda )A+4(1+5\\lambda )B & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the following systems of equations for $A$ and $B:$\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}(5\\lambda )A+(1+5\\lambda )B=0 \\\\ (1+10\\lambda )A+4(1+5\\lambda )B=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis homogeneous linear system has a nontrivial solution if and only if the determinant is zero. This gives\n\n$$\n|\\begin{aligned}5\\lambda  & 1+5\\lambda  \\\\ 1+10\\lambda  & 4(1+5\\lambda )\\end{aligned}|=0.\n$$\n\nSo, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5\\lambda \\cdot 4(1+5\\lambda )-(1+5\\lambda )(1+10\\lambda ) & =0 \\\\ (1+5\\lambda )[20\\lambda -(1+10\\lambda )] & =0 \\\\ (1+5\\lambda )[20\\lambda -1-10\\lambda ] & =0 \\\\ (1+5\\lambda )(10\\lambda -1) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, we have the eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1}=-\\frac{1}{5}\\text{ and }{\\lambda }_{2}=\\frac{1}{10}.$\n\nFinally, we find the eigenfunction corresponding to each eigenvalue:\n\n- Consider ${\\lambda }_{1}=-\\frac{1}{5}.$ Then, we have the solution\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1}(x) & =A{e}^{5{\\lambda }_{1}x}+Bx{e}^{5{\\lambda }_{1}x} \\\\  & =A{e}^{-x}+Bx{e}^{-x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand substituting ${\\lambda }_{1}$ into\n\n$$\n(5\\lambda )A+(1+5\\lambda )B=0\n$$\n\n \nwe get \n\n$$\n-A+(1-1)B=0\n$$\n\n \nwhich gives $A=0,$ and where $B$ is a free parameter. Thus, the eigenfunction corresponding to this eigenvalue is\n\n$$\n{y}_{1}(x)=Bx{e}^{-x}.\n$$\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{2}=\\frac{1}{10},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{2}(x) & =A{e}^{5{\\lambda }_{2}x}+Bx{e}^{5{\\lambda }_{2}x} \\\\  & =A{e}^{x/2}+Bx{e}^{x/2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand substituting ${\\lambda }_{2}$ into\n\n$$\n(5\\lambda )A+(1+5\\lambda )B=0\n$$\n\n \nwe get \n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{2}A+\\frac{3}{2}B=0\\,⇒\\,A=-3B\n$$\n\nwhere $B$ is a free parameter. Thus, the eigenfunction corresponding to this eigenvalue is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{2}(x) & =-3B{e}^{x/2}+Bx{e}^{x/2} \\\\  & =B{e}^{x/2}(x-3) \\\\  & =C{e}^{x/2}(x-3)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere we have set $C=B.$\n\nSo, the eigenfunctions corresponding to ${\\lambda }_{1}$ and ${\\lambda }_{2},$ respectively, are $Bx{e}^{-x}\\text{ and }C{e}^{x/2}(x-3).$"} :step/id #uuid "10ec5288-96c7-54f4-a406-4dc3fa372454" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186418254}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186418251 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186413439 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "333487d5-2b4f-53f7-a1f4-c935ce9920e2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{{n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}}{36}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413422} {:answer/id #uuid "3a127422-744b-580f-8766-eedd4918927a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{{n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}}{81}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413424} {:answer/id #uuid "3d8a953c-29a1-5c31-b0ad-482482bf1033" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{{n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413426} {:answer/id #uuid "9863903c-b7b0-5c31-8d4c-bcb8da7c8123" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{{n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}}{27}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413430}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186413424} :answer-field/id #uuid "314046e0-b3cb-5409-b06d-bb0e8f9f90c3" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413435} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0e54c974-6252-5708-b98d-d9bcad440480" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{B}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x\\sqrt{{\\lambda }_{n}})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413419} {:answer/id #uuid "74d7d903-23a2-5c49-b052-79f08cba23e5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{B}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x\\sqrt{{\\lambda }_{n}})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413428} {:answer/id #uuid "a7739e90-7698-5078-912c-cda3c5ade6a3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{B}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x\\sqrt{{\\lambda }_{n}})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413432} {:answer/id #uuid "f8cb5d6d-8680-580b-b06c-9309df73f9bc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{B}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x\\sqrt{{\\lambda }_{n}})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413434}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186413428} :answer-field/id #uuid "6c50717b-94b2-5e1c-9cdd-e60b58c14e94" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413436}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "827b7203-4bc8-5ef4-ada5-12343dfe7439" :question/math-academy-id "q-338917" :question/problem "Consider the boundary value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+9\\lambda y=0,\\,{y}^{′}(0)={y}^{′}(3)=0,\\,0\\le x\\le 3,\\,\\lambda \\ge 0.\n$$\n\nFind the parameter values corresponding to the eigenvalues and the associated eigenfunctions of this problem.\n\n$\\,$ The eigenvalues correspond to the parameter values ${\\lambda }_{n}=$ {{field-1}}, where $n\\in N.$\n\n$\\,$ The eigenfunctions are ${y}_{n}(x)=$ {{field-2}}, where ${B}_{n}\\in R.$" :question/worked-solution "We consider the cases $\\lambda =0$ and $\\lambda >0$ separately, since they correspond to different solutions. \n\n**Case 1**: First, we consider $\\lambda =0.$ \n\nSetting $\\lambda =0$ gives the equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}=0.\n$$\n\nIntegrating this equation gives\n\n$$\ny=Ax+B.\n$$\n\nApplying the conditions ${y}^{′}(0)={y}^{′}(3)=0$ yields $A=0.$ Thus, we have a nontrivial solution, so $\\lambda =0$ is a parameter corresponding to eigenvalue.\n\n**Case 2**: Next, we consider $\\lambda >0.$\n\nTo find the fundamental solutions, we set $y={e}^{mx}.$ The auxiliary equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{m}^{2}+9\\lambda  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have $m=\\pm 3\\text{i}\\sqrt{\\lambda }.$ So, the fundamental solutions are \n\n$$\n{y}_{1}(x)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x\\sqrt{\\lambda }),\\,{y}_{2}(x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x\\sqrt{\\lambda })\n$$\n\nand the general solution is\n\n$$\ny=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x\\sqrt{\\lambda })+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x\\sqrt{\\lambda }).\n$$\n\nDifferentiating, we get\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=-3A\\sqrt{\\lambda }\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x\\sqrt{\\lambda })+3B\\sqrt{\\lambda }\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x\\sqrt{\\lambda }).\n$$\n\nApplying the boundary condition ${y}^{′}(0)=0$ yields\n\n$$\n-3A\\sqrt{\\lambda }\\operatorname{sin}⁡(0)+3B\\sqrt{\\lambda }\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0)=0\n$$\n\nwhich gives $B=0.$ So, we have\n\n$$\ny=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x\\sqrt{\\lambda }).\n$$\n\nApplying the boundary condition ${y}^{′}(3)=0$ yields\n\n$$\n-3A\\sqrt{\\lambda }\\operatorname{sin}⁡(9\\sqrt{\\lambda })=0.\n$$\n\nFor nontrivial solutions, we require \n\n$$\n9\\sqrt{\\lambda }=n\\pi \n$$\n\nfor $n\\in N.$ This gives the parameter values corresponding to the eigenvalues:\n\n$$\n{\\lambda }_{n}=\\frac{{n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}}{81}.\n$$\n\nThe corresponding eigenfunctions are \n\n$$\n{y}_{n}(x)={B}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x\\sqrt{{\\lambda }_{n}}),\\,{B}_{n}\\in R.\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "35485800-4576-505b-b01c-a3a46dd2634c" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186418252}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186418252 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186391720 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d299aab6-7d45-5daf-aeed-dbcaf20eb01f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{{n}^{2}}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391722} {:answer/id #uuid "50cf2632-5224-53c3-9780-700b593c95e4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{{n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391723} {:answer/id #uuid "dad65025-c4f6-50bb-bfc0-81fdab4d27f0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{{n}^{2}}{16}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391724} {:answer/id #uuid "78ab4f16-e632-5d7c-a0ed-fca61eb6d86b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{{n}^{2}\\pi }{16}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391725}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186391724} :answer-field/id #uuid "c3e4d0a8-336d-51eb-9840-10ac5a3bc5ff" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391721} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "950d017d-4409-5a7d-93e6-82c04aebf61f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{B}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x\\sqrt{{\\lambda }_{n}})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391727} {:answer/id #uuid "a76caf84-a8f6-5ed7-b166-b53efe8b63f5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{B}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4x\\sqrt{{\\lambda }_{n}})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391728} {:answer/id #uuid "7e019b9a-dbcd-5aaa-9d4a-e9839bb29f61" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{B}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x\\sqrt{{\\lambda }_{n}})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391729} {:answer/id #uuid "3ae13067-ee42-5ad2-b168-11769b5a9ed5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{B}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x\\sqrt{{\\lambda }_{n}})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391730}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186391729} :answer-field/id #uuid "bf084e4c-8bde-52e0-8415-918ddbdb9317" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391726}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "55c03286-efa5-5f72-9411-1febab9b4b0f" :question/math-academy-id "q-338920" :question/problem "Consider the boundary value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+16\\lambda y=0,\\,y(0)=y(\\pi )=0,\\,0\\le x\\le \\pi ,\\,\\lambda \\ge 0.\n$$\n\nFind the parameter values corresponding to the eigenvalues and the associated eigenfunctions of this problem.\n\n$\\,$ The eigenvalues correspond to the parameter values ${\\lambda }_{n}=$ {{field-1}}, where $n\\in N.$\n\n$\\,$ The eigenfunctions are ${y}_{n}(x)=$ {{field-2}}, where ${B}_{n}\\in R.$" :question/worked-solution "We consider the cases $\\lambda =0$ and $\\lambda >0$ separately, since they correspond to different solutions. \n\n**Case 1**: First, we consider $\\lambda =0.$ \n\nSetting $\\lambda =0$ gives the equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}=0.\n$$\n\nIntegrating this equation gives\n\n$$\ny=Ax+B.\n$$\n\nApplying the conditions $y(0)=y(\\pi )=0$ yields the trivial solution $y≡0.$ Since the zero solution is not an eigenfunction, $\\lambda =0$ does not correspond to an eigenvalue.\n\n**Case 2**: Next, we consider $\\lambda >0.$\n\nTo find the fundamental solutions, we set $y={e}^{mx}.$ The auxiliary equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{m}^{2}+16\\lambda  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have $m=\\pm 4\\text{i}\\sqrt{\\lambda }.$ So, the fundamental solutions are \n\n$$\n{y}_{1}(x)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4x\\sqrt{\\lambda }),\\,{y}_{2}(x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x\\sqrt{\\lambda })\n$$\n\nand the general solution is\n\n$$\ny=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4x\\sqrt{\\lambda })+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x\\sqrt{\\lambda }).\n$$\n\nApplying the boundary condition $y(0)=0$ yields\n\n$$\nA\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0)+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡(0)=0\n$$\n\nwhich gives $A=0.$ So, we have\n\n$$\ny=B\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x\\sqrt{\\lambda }).\n$$\n\nApplying the boundary condition $y(\\pi )=0$ yields\n\n$$\nB\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4\\pi \\sqrt{\\lambda })=0.\n$$\n\nFor nontrivial solutions, we require \n\n$$\n4\\pi \\sqrt{\\lambda }=n\\pi \n$$\n\nfor $n\\in N.$ This gives the parameter values corresponding to the eigenvalues:\n\n$$\n{\\lambda }_{n}=\\frac{{n}^{2}}{16}.\n$$\n\nThe corresponding eigenfunctions are \n\n$$\n{y}_{n}(x)={B}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x\\sqrt{{\\lambda }_{n}}),\\,{B}_{n}\\in R.\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "742e6a8d-8e75-5c43-a20e-2b9e2df49b4e"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186418253 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186391695 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "80717e13-aa04-534e-b7cb-b9dd198b1171" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{n}{16}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391697} {:answer/id #uuid "ab9a1003-518c-5978-82a3-b12124ff2cae" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{n\\pi }{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391698} {:answer/id #uuid "cb175292-1d19-53ba-a268-6e1a4ae2b1d6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{n\\pi }{16}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391699} {:answer/id #uuid "ab342eb6-9f44-5413-9868-ba9ddfbb18a7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4n" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391700} {:answer/id #uuid "94b96273-f78c-566b-9d25-97a799b4f55c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{n}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391701}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186391701} :answer-field/id #uuid "b89af3df-4153-5000-b2fe-f6e14e30ad1b" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391696} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a3af5ddc-3890-5c59-8803-f398173deb54" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{B}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4{\\lambda }_{n}x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391703} {:answer/id #uuid "0044f4b1-e6d3-557b-b79a-d102edcc257a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{B}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡({\\lambda }_{n}x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391704} {:answer/id #uuid "4961ff2e-c297-5af0-a99e-d7db5b609a6a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{B}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{4{\\lambda }_{n}x}{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391705} {:answer/id #uuid "40f7e995-e510-5677-a3ee-2e9610868cad" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{B}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡({\\lambda }_{n}x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391706} {:answer/id #uuid "d6c87594-7fd9-5262-b977-690b21e75561" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{B}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4{\\lambda }_{n}x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391707} {:answer/id #uuid "56ed424b-8ca9-59d2-8528-1ebfe5b468f4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{B}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{{\\lambda }_{n}x}{4})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391708}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186391707} :answer-field/id #uuid "d664b659-e57c-53ea-8b4c-aa060927c521" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391702}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "2b03c659-cc6c-5259-9cfb-c53b896cc809" :question/math-academy-id "q-338909" :question/problem "Consider the boundary value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+16{\\lambda }^{2}y=0,\\,{y}^{′}(0)={y}^{′}(\\pi )=0,\\,0\\le x\\le \\pi ,\\,\\lambda \\ge 0.\n$$\n\nFind the parameter values corresponding to the eigenvalues and the associated eigenfunctions of this problem.\n\n$\\,$ The eigenvalues correspond to the parameter values ${\\lambda }_{n}=$ {{field-1}}, where $n\\in N.$\n\n$\\,$ The eigenfunctions are ${y}_{n}(x)=$ {{field-2}}, where ${B}_{n}\\in R.$" :question/worked-solution "We consider the cases $\\lambda =0$ and $\\lambda \\ne 0$ separately, since they correspond to different solutions. \n\n**Case 1**: First, we consider $\\lambda =0.$ \n\nSetting $\\lambda =0$ gives the equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}=0.\n$$\n\nIntegrating this equation gives\n\n$$\ny=Ax+B.\n$$\n\nApplying the conditions ${y}^{′}(0)={y}^{′}(\\pi )=0$ yields $A=0.$ Thus, we have a nontrivial solution, so $\\lambda =0$ is a parameter value corresponding to the eigenvalue.\n\n**Case 2**: Next, we consider $\\lambda >0.$\n\nTo find the fundamental solutions, we set $y={e}^{mx}.$ The auxiliary equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{m}^{2}+16{\\lambda }^{2} & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have $m=\\pm 4\\text{i}\\lambda .$ So, the fundamental solutions are \n\n$$\n{y}_{1}(x)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4\\lambda x),\\,{y}_{2}(x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4\\lambda x)\n$$\n\nand the general solution is\n\n$$\ny=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4\\lambda x)+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4\\lambda x).\n$$\n\nDifferentiating gives\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=-4A\\lambda \\operatorname{sin}⁡(4\\lambda x)+4B\\lambda \\operatorname{cos}⁡(4\\lambda x).\n$$\n\nApplying the boundary condition ${y}^{′}(0)=0$ yields\n\n$$\n-4A\\lambda \\operatorname{sin}⁡(0)+4B\\lambda \\operatorname{cos}⁡(0)=0\n$$\n\nwhich gives $B=0.$ So, we have\n\n$$\ny=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4\\lambda x).\n$$\n\nApplying the boundary condition ${y}^{′}(\\pi )=0$ yields\n\n$$\n-4A\\lambda \\operatorname{sin}⁡(4\\lambda \\pi )=0.\n$$\n\nFor nontrivial solutions, we require \n\n$$\n4\\lambda \\pi =n\\pi \n$$\n\nfor $n\\in N.$ This gives the parameter values corresponding to the eigenvalues:\n\n$$\n{\\lambda }_{n}=\\frac{n}{4}.\n$$\n\nThe corresponding eigenfunctions are \n\n$$\n{y}_{n}(x)={B}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4{\\lambda }_{n}x),\\,{B}_{n}\\in R.\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "bab35c78-7682-5ede-95d9-455d7d62484b" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186418251}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186418254 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186314744 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "28512bc4-3366-5851-ab15-e9ddf7bdc6ed" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\pm \\frac{1}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413420} {:answer/id #uuid "3b6a8205-b73b-5687-a756-4e7c12880207" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\pm \\frac{1}{12}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413425} {:answer/id #uuid "900413a5-3275-52e8-aad8-708d3d20a529" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$-\\frac{1}{6}$ and $\\frac{1}{12}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413429} {:answer/id #uuid "9df526c7-25b8-5eaf-9168-18db96d42a4e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$-\\frac{1}{12}$ and $\\frac{1}{6}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413431}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186413429} :answer-field/id #uuid "8dd2d3fa-c650-5e37-9b97-e79bceb3c6ba" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413437} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2ed3b012-ea23-5630-95d6-c16372fcd823" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$Bx{e}^{-x}$ and $C{e}^{x}(x-1)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413421} {:answer/id #uuid "33bd6051-1ce7-59a0-a89b-5f289fa49519" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$Bx{e}^{-2x}$ and $C{e}^{x}(x-3)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413423} {:answer/id #uuid "408a492f-a860-5962-a306-84c6d1f01f6b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$B{e}^{-x}(x-1)$ and $C{e}^{x/2}(x-2)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413427} {:answer/id #uuid "f6353e39-4783-54e8-b20e-f618126b98a2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$B{e}^{-x}(x+1)$ and $C{e}^{x/2}(x-2)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413433}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186413433} :answer-field/id #uuid "9b93007b-725a-5c21-aef1-4108c8c123fb" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413438}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "b2709e91-93e6-520b-8d50-587e1e5c8629" :question/math-academy-id "q-338904" :question/problem "Consider the boundary value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}-12\\lambda {y}^{′}+36{\\lambda }^{2}y=0,\\,{y}^{′}(0)=0,\\,y(1)+2{y}^{′}(1)=0,\\,0\\le x\\le 1,\\,\\lambda \\in R.\n$$\n\nFind the eigenvalues and eigenfunctions of this problem. Assume that $B$ and $C$ are constants.\n\n$\\,$ The eigenvalues are $\\lambda =$ {{field-1}}.\n\n$\\,$ The eigenfunctions are $y=$ {{field-2}}." :question/worked-solution "We consider the cases $\\lambda =0$ and $\\lambda \\ne 0$ separately, since they correspond to different solutions. \n\n**Case 1**: First, we consider $\\lambda =0.$ \n\nSetting $\\lambda =0$ gives the equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}=0.\n$$\n\nIntegrating this equation gives\n\n$$\ny=Ax+B.\n$$\n\nApplying the conditions ${y}^{′}(0)=0$ and $y(1)+2{y}^{′}(1)=0$ yields the trivial solution $y≡0.$ Since the zero solution is not an eigenfunction, $\\lambda =0$ is not an eigenvalue.\n\n**Case 2**: Next, we consider $\\lambda \\ne 0.$\n\nTo find the fundamental solutions, we set $y={e}^{mx}.$ The auxiliary equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{m}^{2}-12\\lambda m+36{\\lambda }^{2} & =0 \\\\ (m-6\\lambda )(m-6\\lambda ) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the double root $m=6\\lambda .$ So, the fundamental solutions are \n\n$$\n{y}_{1}(x)={e}^{6\\lambda x},\\,{y}_{2}(x)=x{e}^{6\\lambda x}\n$$\n\nand the general solution is\n\n$$\ny=A{e}^{6\\lambda x}+Bx{e}^{6\\lambda x}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =6A\\lambda {e}^{6\\lambda x}+B{e}^{6\\lambda x}+6B\\lambda x{e}^{6\\lambda x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the boundary condition ${y}^{′}(0)=0$ yields\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6A\\lambda +B & =0 \\\\ (6\\lambda )A+B & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the boundary condition $y(1)+2{y}^{′}(1)=0$ yields\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A{e}^{6\\lambda }+B{e}^{6\\lambda }+2(6A\\lambda {e}^{6\\lambda }+B{e}^{6\\lambda }+6B\\lambda {e}^{6\\lambda }) & =0 \\\\ A+B+2(6A\\lambda +B+6B\\lambda ) & =0 \\\\ A+B+12A\\lambda +2B+12B\\lambda  & =0 \\\\ (1+12\\lambda )A+(3+12\\lambda )B & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the following systems of equations for $A$ and $B:$\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}(6\\lambda )A+B=0 \\\\ (1+12\\lambda )A+(3+12\\lambda )B=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis homogeneous linear system has a nontrivial solution if and only if the determinant is zero. This gives\n\n$$\n|\\begin{aligned}6\\lambda  & 1 \\\\ 1+12\\lambda  & 3+12\\lambda \\end{aligned}|=0.\n$$\n\nSo, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6\\lambda (3+12\\lambda )-(1)(1+12\\lambda ) & =0 \\\\ 18\\lambda +72{\\lambda }^{2}-1-12\\lambda  & =0 \\\\ 72{\\lambda }^{2}+6\\lambda -1 & =0 \\\\ (12\\lambda -1)(6\\lambda +1) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, we have the eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1}=-\\frac{1}{6}\\text{ and }{\\lambda }_{2}=\\frac{1}{12}.$\n\nFinally, we find the eigenfunction corresponding to each eigenvalue:\n\n- Consider ${\\lambda }_{1}=-\\frac{1}{6}.$ Then, we have the solution\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1}(x) & =A{e}^{6{\\lambda }_{1}x}+Bx{e}^{6{\\lambda }_{1}x} \\\\  & =A{e}^{-x}+Bx{e}^{-x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand substituting ${\\lambda }_{1}$ into\n\n$$\n(6\\lambda )A+B=0\n$$\n\n \nwe get \n\n$$\n-A+B=0\\,⇒\\,A=B\n$$\n\nwhere $B$ is a free parameter. Thus, the eigenfunction corresponding to this eigenvalue is\n\n$$\n{y}_{1}(x)=B{e}^{-x}(x+1).\n$$\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{2}=\\frac{1}{12},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{2}(x) & =A{e}^{6{\\lambda }_{2}x}+Bx{e}^{6{\\lambda }_{2}x} \\\\  & =A{e}^{x/2}+Bx{e}^{x/2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand substituting ${\\lambda }_{2}$ into\n\n$$\n(6\\lambda )A+B=0\n$$\n\n \nwe get \n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{2}A+B=0\\,⇒\\,A=-2B\n$$\n\nwhere $B$ is a free parameter. Thus, the eigenfunction corresponding to this eigenvalue is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{2}(x) & =-2B{e}^{x/2}+Bx{e}^{x/2} \\\\  & =B{e}^{x/2}(x-2) \\\\  & =C{e}^{x/2}(x-2)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere we have set $C=B.$\n\nSo, the eigenfunctions corresponding to ${\\lambda }_{1}$ and ${\\lambda }_{2},$ respectively, are $B{e}^{-x}(x+1)\\text{ and }C{e}^{x/2}(x-2).$"} :step/id #uuid "db32b2ae-bde3-5b8b-9a23-8d2e69b2eb90" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186418253}}] :activity/title "Eigenvalues and Eigenfunctions of Homogeneous BVPs" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186418249}] [{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162849} :activity/id #uuid "22c8da45-56d1-5645-85df-aa94cccb29d8" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049849 :topic/id #uuid "1887f465-a1b8-5370-82f6-80e02b9d153a" :topic/math-academy-id 3120} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162849 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186163224 :tutorial/content "Suppose that $A$ is an $n\\times n$ symmetric matrix over $\\mathbb{R}.$ The **spectral theorem** states the following:\n\n- The eigenvalues of $A$ are all real.\n- The eigenvectors corresponding to *different* eigenvalues are orthogonal.\n- The dimension of the eigenspace for each eigenvalue equals the multiplicity of that eigenvalue as a root of the characteristic polynomial.\n- $A$ is orthogonally diagonalizable." :tutorial/id #uuid "5ecbbdd0-25a0-52ee-8f0c-987dcd8ba584" :tutorial/math-academy-id 4882 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "84b2f14e-f6c7-5b6c-b6d7-19d4d7d71f71" :step/math-academy-id 30533 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162902}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162902 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162013 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186161805 :question/id #uuid "6ae71f41-133f-50b6-a286-20ae7e2c3a9d" :question/math-academy-id "e-8291"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4c67d532-acd9-5231-a27f-d39084863e01" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048493}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162645} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162659} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186365052} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186366636} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186366643} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186366650} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186366657} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186411856}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements Regarding the Spectral Theorem"} :step/id #uuid "95f72e96-f84b-5b21-a070-253638be57a8" :step/math-academy-id 30970 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162903}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162903 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186163243 :tutorial/content "We can write an $n\\times n$ symmetric matrix $A$ in terms of a sum of products of its eigenvalues and eigenvectors.\n\nSpecifically, if $\\mathbf{u}_i$ denotes an orthonormal eigenvector of $A$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda_i$ for $i=1,\\ldots, n$, then\n\n$$A=\\sum_{i=1}^n \\lambda_i \\mathbf{u}_i \\mathbf{u}_i^T.$$\n\nExpressing a symmetric matrix $A$ in this way is called the **spectral decomposition** of $A.$\n\nTo demonstrate, let's find the spectral decomposition of the $2\\times 2$ symmetric matrix $A,$ given by\n\n$$A = [\\begin{aligned}-3 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 5\\end{aligned}].$$\n\nThe eigenvalues of $A$ are $\\lambda_1 = 6$ and $\\lambda_2 = -4,$ and the corresponding eigenvectors are\n\n$$\\mathbf{v}_1 = [\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}] \\quad \\textrm{and} \\quad \\mathbf{v}_2 = [\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}].$$\n\nThe spectral decomposition of $A$ is given by\n\n$$A =\\sum_{i=1}^2 \\lambda_i \\mathbf{u}_i \\mathbf{u}_i^T = \\lambda_1 \\mathbf u_1 \\mathbf u_1^T + \\lambda_2 \\mathbf u_2 \\mathbf u_2^T,$$\n\nwhere $\\mathbf u_1$ and $\\mathbf u_2$ are orthonormal eigenvectors of $A$ corresponding to $\\lambda_1$ and $\\lambda_2,$ respectively.\n\nFirst, let's normalize our eigenvectors:\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1} & =\\frac{{v}_{1}}{‖{v}_{1}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}] \\\\ {u}_{2} & =\\frac{{v}_{2}}{‖{v}_{2}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}$$\n\nTherefore, the spectral decomposition of $A$ is\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}A & ={\\lambda }_{1}{u}_{1}{u}_{1}^{T}+{\\lambda }_{2}{u}_{2}{u}_{2}^{T} \\\\ & =6\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3\\end{aligned}]+(-4)\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}[\\begin{aligned}3 & -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\ & =6\\cdot \\frac{1}{10}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 & 3\\end{aligned}]+(-4)\\cdot \\frac{1}{10}[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}3 & -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\ & ={{6}_{⏟}}_{{\\lambda }_{1}}\\cdot {{[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{10} & \\frac{3}{10} \\\\ \\frac{3}{10} & \\frac{9}{10}\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{{u}_{1}{u}_{1}^{T}}+{{(-4)}_{⏟}}_{{\\lambda }_{2}}\\cdot {{[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{9}{10} & -\\frac{3}{10} \\\\ -\\frac{3}{10} & \\frac{1}{10}\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{{u}_{2}{u}_{2}^{T}}.\\end{aligned}$$\n\nIn this decomposition, the matrix $\\mathbf{u}_i \\mathbf{u}_i^T$ is the orthogonal projection matrix that maps any $\\mathbf x \\in \\mathbb R^2$ onto the subspace spanned by $\\mathbf u_i.$" :tutorial/id #uuid "a90957e3-e4b9-548d-930b-e060c6b63859" :tutorial/math-academy-id 4973 :tutorial/title "The Spectral Decomposition of a Symmetric Matrix"} :step/id #uuid "8df507ec-4d53-59f1-b941-07907cd14b6f" :step/math-academy-id 30971 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162905}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162904 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162014 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186161806 :question/id #uuid "d185ef42-c177-5217-99b7-b42d800f3385" :question/math-academy-id "e-8292"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "84207049-12f7-514c-bfa2-51bce8631b41" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049489}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162646} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162662} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186365059} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186366670} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186366677} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186378058} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186395038} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186395045} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186395052} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186407552}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Projection Matrix That Orthogonally Projects a Vector to the Subspace Spanned by an Eigenvector"} :step/id #uuid "2a1d5d86-333c-5361-87ec-fd7766797988" :step/math-academy-id 30972} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162905 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162015 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186161807 :question/id #uuid "65b23c21-528f-5df7-a00c-8e2dce041276" :question/math-academy-id "e-8293"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "785b09aa-1b82-5021-8b10-271a063cb916" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162647} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162665} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186366684} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186375958} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186378065} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186395059} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186395066} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186395073} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186407550} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186407551} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186407553}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Spectral Decomposition of a Symmetric Matrix"} :step/id #uuid "86248bdd-6a75-54cd-8196-8b333a64cac5" :step/math-academy-id 30973 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162904}}] :activity/title "The Spectral Theorem" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186162216}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046673} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186407555} :activity/id #uuid "cb802846-7e96-5e29-a7fd-43a85cc938d8" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049849 :topic/id #uuid "1887f465-a1b8-5370-82f6-80e02b9d153a" :topic/math-academy-id 3120} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186407554 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186407550 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "237e9827-741e-554d-b4bd-a580c12832ad" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}5 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 5\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407524} {:answer/id #uuid "637ff16c-2a36-5623-9258-1f3130b80075" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}2 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 2\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407529} {:answer/id #uuid "6e8f4850-6613-5ef7-a355-f12825aba902" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}10 & 6 \\\\ 6 & 10\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407532} {:answer/id #uuid "bea7dec3-53c9-52fb-9b61-30210ef0b89a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407538} {:answer/id #uuid "d23ed5d0-4d06-561d-902d-1213f1013134" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-3 & -5 \\\\ -5 & -3\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407539}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186407524} :answer-field/id #uuid "3b7eee37-4841-5d7e-83f1-ad47a007de82" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407545}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "69f0b9bd-9336-5c1c-91e3-bf72b33b1333" :question/math-academy-id "q-140847" :question/problem "Suppose that\n\n$\\,$ ${v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\,$ and $\\,{v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$\n\nare eigenvectors of a $2\\times 2$ symmetric matrix $A$ that correspond to the eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1}=2$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=8,$ respectively. Find the matrix $A.$" :question/worked-solution "The spectral decomposition of a $2\\times 2$ symmetric matrix $A$ is given by\n\n$$\nA={\\lambda }_{1}{u}_{1}\\cdot {u}_{1}^{T}+{\\lambda }_{2}{u}_{2}\\cdot {u}_{2}^{T},\n$$\n\nwhere ${u}_{1}$ and ${u}_{2}$ are orthonormal eigenvectors of $A$ corresponding to the eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1}$ and ${\\lambda }_{2},$ respectively.\n\nFirst, let's normalize our eigenvectors:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1} & =\\frac{{v}_{1}}{‖{v}_{1}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\ {u}_{2} & =\\frac{{v}_{2}}{‖{v}_{2}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ={\\lambda }_{1}{u}_{1}{u}_{1}^{T}+{\\lambda }_{2}{u}_{2}{u}_{2}^{T} \\\\  & =2\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1\\end{aligned}]+8\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =2\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1\\end{aligned}]+8\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(2\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1\\end{aligned}]+8\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 1\\end{aligned}]) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(2\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}]+8\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}]) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}[\\begin{aligned}10 & 6 \\\\ 6 & 10\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}5 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 5\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "07386288-c5f3-55df-b73a-99cec25cb7d6"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186407555 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162646 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1556dc1b-0c8b-54e0-a340-18371f592ed3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}[\\begin{aligned}4 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407520} {:answer/id #uuid "286d06a8-1050-59ed-a665-558d840c2aae" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\sqrt{5}[\\begin{aligned}4 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407525} {:answer/id #uuid "d6effcc8-e643-5b20-9090-b3855faacab9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{5}[\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407541} {:answer/id #uuid "e9f4f75f-447a-5e0c-a1d6-feae985a30a3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407543} {:answer/id #uuid "f93a6cf9-78b6-546d-ba88-361fc48a056a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{5}[\\begin{aligned}4 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407544}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186407544} :answer-field/id #uuid "ebade115-5b0c-52d7-94cc-8ec9e5babad9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407549}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "11bdef24-ff68-513b-bfa6-5f2fb8c78b02" :question/math-academy-id "q-94768" :question/problem "Find the orthogonal projection matrix that maps $x\\in {R}^{2}$ onto the subspace spanned by an eigenvector corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =-4$ of the matrix $A=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 4 \\\\ 4 & 4\\end{aligned}].$" :question/worked-solution "The spectral decomposition of a $2\\times 2$ symmetric matrix $A$ is given by\n\n$$\nA={\\lambda }_{1}{u}_{1}\\cdot {u}_{1}^{T}+{\\lambda }_{2}{u}_{2}\\cdot {u}_{2}^{T},\n$$\n\nwhere ${u}_{1}$ and ${u}_{2}$ are orthonormal eigenvectors of $A$ corresponding to the eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1}$ and ${\\lambda }_{2},$ respectively.\n\nMoreover, ${u}_{i}\\cdot {u}_{i}^{T}$ is the projection matrix corresponding to ${u}_{i}.$\n\nFirst, we need to find an eigenvector that corresponds to $\\lambda =-4.$ Computing $A-(-4)I,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A+4I & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 4 \\\\ 4 & 4\\end{aligned}]+4[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 4 \\\\ 4 & 8\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a system of linear equations with the augmented matrix $M$ which we reduce to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 8 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-2){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}2 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. From the first equation, we obtain ${x}_{1}=-2{x}_{2}.$ Hence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}-2{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nTherefore, setting ${x}_{2}=1,$ we get the eigenvector \n\n$$\nv=[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nwhere $‖v‖=\\sqrt{5}.$ Dividing $v$ by its norm, we obtain the normalized eigenvector: \n\n$$\nu=\\frac{v}{‖v‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nFinally, the required orthogonal projection matrix is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u\\cdot {u}^{T} & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}-2 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}[\\begin{aligned}4 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "2d35b336-2654-53f7-8116-4689735d233e" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186407557}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186407556 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186407551 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "16877fa0-d3a3-5da8-ba77-c7e71244a7c0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}0 & -6 \\\\ -6 & 4\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407521} {:answer/id #uuid "6d264661-257d-5c8a-8684-122b5c0a6807" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-5 & 6 \\\\ 6 & 0\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407531} {:answer/id #uuid "9555f6b0-dd96-561e-b956-a28b10613e7e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-8 & 6 \\\\ 6 & -3\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407535} {:answer/id #uuid "ad0b779d-af50-58f8-97aa-dd75027108dd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}5 & -6 \\\\ -6 & -4\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407536} {:answer/id #uuid "d6231cbd-de79-555a-b056-b8008595ed25" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-5 & 6 \\\\ 6 & -3\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407540}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186407531} :answer-field/id #uuid "d1092e23-b9a3-5556-b0d1-0745f95be4b9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407548}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "7e3f233e-913d-50cf-8912-1c6832afca12" :question/math-academy-id "q-95725" :question/problem "Suppose that\n\n$\\,$ ${v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]\\,$ and $\\,{v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]$\n\nare eigenvectors of a $2\\times 2$ symmetric matrix $A$ that correspond to the eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1}=4$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=-9,$ respectively. Find the matrix $A.$" :question/worked-solution "The spectral decomposition of a $2\\times 2$ symmetric matrix $A$ is given by\n\n$$\nA={\\lambda }_{1}{u}_{1}\\cdot {u}_{1}^{T}+{\\lambda }_{2}{u}_{2}\\cdot {u}_{2}^{T},\n$$\n\nwhere ${u}_{1}$ and ${u}_{2}$ are orthonormal eigenvectors of $A$ corresponding to the eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1}$ and ${\\lambda }_{2},$ respectively.\n\nFirst, let's normalize our eigenvectors:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1} & =\\frac{{v}_{1}}{‖{v}_{1}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{13}}[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}] \\\\ {u}_{2} & =\\frac{{v}_{2}}{‖{v}_{2}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{13}}[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ={\\lambda }_{1}{u}_{1}{u}_{1}^{T}+{\\lambda }_{2}{u}_{2}{u}_{2}^{T} \\\\  & =4\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{13}}[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{13}}[\\begin{aligned}2 & 3\\end{aligned}]+(-9)\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{13}}[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{13}}[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =4\\cdot \\frac{1}{13}[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 & 3\\end{aligned}]+(-9)\\cdot \\frac{1}{13}[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-3 & 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{13}(4\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 & 3\\end{aligned}]+(-9)\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-3 & 2\\end{aligned}]) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{13}(4\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}4 & 6 \\\\ 6 & 9\\end{aligned}]+(-9)\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}9 & -6 \\\\ -6 & 4\\end{aligned}]) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{13}[\\begin{aligned}-65 & 78 \\\\ 78 & 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-5 & 6 \\\\ 6 & 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "5fc71f89-cc9a-549d-9f1f-b3a2081e4a63" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186407554}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186407557 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186407552 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1d327dec-d42a-581c-8707-926d0b6893a9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{17}[\\begin{aligned}1 & -4 \\\\ -4 & 16\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407522} {:answer/id #uuid "3c3ec327-8643-5dfe-8735-8c0774db03ee" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{17}}[\\begin{aligned}16 & 4 \\\\ 4 & 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407526} {:answer/id #uuid "799755e8-7ecf-5b56-8460-da2805b858a9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\sqrt{17}[\\begin{aligned}16 & 4 \\\\ 4 & 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407533} {:answer/id #uuid "95054c16-a500-55cc-9c6d-5dc257b93966" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{17}}[\\begin{aligned}1 & -4 \\\\ -4 & 16\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407534} {:answer/id #uuid "e4b2cd88-0c1c-5067-96b8-f34a70eda38d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{17}[\\begin{aligned}16 & 4 \\\\ 4 & 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407542}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186407542} :answer-field/id #uuid "cb85388f-97d4-5b7a-9384-bb45d3a9d42b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407547}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "9d231abd-db3d-5d11-9253-820e7eb51742" :question/math-academy-id "q-95712" :question/problem "Find the orthogonal projection matrix that maps $x\\in {R}^{2}$ onto the subspace spanned by an eigenvector corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =2$ of the matrix $A=[\\begin{aligned}3 & -4 \\\\ -4 & 18\\end{aligned}].$" :question/worked-solution "The spectral decomposition of a $2\\times 2$ symmetric matrix $A$ is given by\n\n$$\nA={\\lambda }_{1}{u}_{1}\\cdot {u}_{1}^{T}+{\\lambda }_{2}{u}_{2}\\cdot {u}_{2}^{T},\n$$\n\nwhere ${u}_{1}$ and ${u}_{2}$ are orthonormal eigenvectors of $A$ corresponding to the eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1}$ and ${\\lambda }_{2},$ respectively.\n\nMoreover, ${u}_{i}\\cdot {u}_{i}^{T}$ is the projection matrix corresponding to ${u}_{i}.$\n\nFirst, we need to find an eigenvector that corresponds to $\\lambda =2.$ Computing $A-2I,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-2I & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & -4 \\\\ -4 & 18\\end{aligned}]-2[\\begin{aligned}1 & -4 \\\\ -4 & 16\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -4 \\\\ -4 & 16\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a system of linear equations with the augmented matrix $M$ which we reduce to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & -4 & 0 \\\\ -4 & 16 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+4{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. From the first equation, we obtain ${x}_{1}=4{x}_{2}.$ Hence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}4{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nTherefore, setting ${x}_{2}=1,$ we get the eigenvector \n\n$$\nv=[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nwhere $‖v‖=\\sqrt{17}.$ Dividing $v$ by its norm, we obtain the normalized eigenvector: \n\n$$\nu=\\frac{v}{‖v‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{17}}[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nFinally, the required orthogonal projection matrix is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u\\cdot {u}^{T} & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{17}}[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{17}}[\\begin{aligned}4 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{17}[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}4 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{17}[\\begin{aligned}16 & 4 \\\\ 4 & 1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "61132d09-e84e-51b9-b83a-aedca27c1563" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186407558}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186407558 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186407553 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1f090535-e10a-5c59-9a51-2d2aae1cbc9b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-5 & 2 \\\\ 2 & -2\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407523} {:answer/id #uuid "3f43beb7-1a8e-5d63-bf78-07894e8c2c04" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}5 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407527} {:answer/id #uuid "5613d7c8-bedb-54d2-be91-7e80b596eb5c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-5 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 2\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407528} {:answer/id #uuid "694df5b3-0ed3-5486-8f0d-63bf6d2b9e75" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-5 & 4 \\\\ 4 & -3\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407530} {:answer/id #uuid "bc550768-52c7-5626-83cb-470760bd86c3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}5 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 3\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407537}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186407523} :answer-field/id #uuid "a49b921e-a620-50dc-993e-f6b1a37ca3f6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407546}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "b502656e-ef35-5f6c-921c-ae3a8ea1f2a2" :question/math-academy-id "q-95721" :question/problem "Suppose that\n\n$\\,$ ${v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]\\,$ and $\\,{v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$\n\nare eigenvectors of a $2\\times 2$ symmetric matrix $A$ that correspond to the eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1}=-1$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=-6,$ respectively. Find the matrix $A.$" :question/worked-solution "The spectral decomposition of a $2\\times 2$ symmetric matrix $A$ is given by\n\n$$\nA={\\lambda }_{1}{u}_{1}\\cdot {u}_{1}^{T}+{\\lambda }_{2}{u}_{2}\\cdot {u}_{2}^{T},\n$$\n\nwhere ${u}_{1}$ and ${u}_{2}$ are orthonormal eigenvectors of $A$ corresponding to the eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1}$ and ${\\lambda }_{2},$ respectively.\n\nFirst, let's normalize our eigenvectors:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1} & =\\frac{{v}_{1}}{‖{v}_{1}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\ {u}_{2} & =\\frac{{v}_{2}}{‖{v}_{2}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ={\\lambda }_{1}{u}_{1}{u}_{1}^{T}+{\\lambda }_{2}{u}_{2}{u}_{2}^{T} \\\\  & =(-1)\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2\\end{aligned}]+(-6)\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =(-1)\\cdot \\frac{1}{5}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 2\\end{aligned}]+(-6)\\cdot \\frac{1}{5}[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-2 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}((-1)\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 2\\end{aligned}]+(-6)\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-2 & 1\\end{aligned}]) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}((-1)\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 4\\end{aligned}]+(-6)\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}4 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{aligned}]) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}[\\begin{aligned}-25 & 10 \\\\ 10 & -10\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-5 & 2 \\\\ 2 & -2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "639ff62f-ac48-56c2-bf2b-580bf31ccf01" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186407556}}] :activity/title "The Spectral Theorem" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407519}] [{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134689} :activity/id #uuid "542ba334-8a63-5347-814a-76bcfa5bc249" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049864 :topic/id #uuid "1973989c-ebc8-5a88-b58e-c9b9de5f86ee" :topic/math-academy-id 2128} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134689 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186135056 :tutorial/content "Consider the subspace $V=\\textrm{Span}\\{\\mathbf{a}_1,\\mathbf{a}_2,\\mathbf{a}_3\\},$ where\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nWe already know how to find an orthogonal basis for $2$-dimensional subspaces. But, how can we do it for subspaces with higher dimensions, like $V?$\n\nGood news is that we can extend the **Gram-Schmidt process**. Since the first two vectors are already orthogonal, we proceed to find an orthogonal basis using the Gram-Schmidt process, as follows:\n\n**Step 1**: We set $[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]$\n\n**Step 2**: We set $[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]$\n\n**Step 3**. Next, let $S=\\textrm{Span}(\\mathbf{a}_1,\\mathbf{a}_2).$ We find ${\\color{blue}\\mathbf{v}_3}$ using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{3} & ={a}_{3}\\,-\\,{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,{a}_{3} \\\\  & ={a}_{3}\\,-\\,{\\text{proj}}_{{v}_{1}}\\,{a}_{3}\\,-\\,{\\text{proj}}_{{v}_{2}}\\,{a}_{3} \\\\  & ={a}_{3}-\\frac{{a}_{3}\\cdot {v}_{1}}{{v}_{1}\\cdot {v}_{1}}{v}_{1}-\\frac{{a}_{3}\\cdot {v}_{2}}{{v}_{2}\\cdot {v}_{2}}{v}_{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n![](images/5b/5b52c26738f2b4dd577ab4148ef7673ad4a1aa53c82a6a6c1e7bf46c0ff36df0.png)\n\nNotice that $\\color{blue}\\mathbf{v}_3$ is written as a linear combination of $\\mathbf{a}_1(={\\color{blue}\\mathbf{v}_1}),$ $\\mathbf{a}_2(={\\color{blue}\\mathbf{v}_2}),$ and $\\mathbf{a}_3.$ This means that ${\\color{blue}\\mathbf{v}_3} \\in V,$ and also ${\\color{blue}\\mathbf{v}_3} \\perp S.$ Moreover, \n\n$$\n\\textrm{Span}\\{\\mathbf{a}_1,\\mathbf{a}_2,\\mathbf{a}_3\\} = V = \\textrm{Span}\\{ {\\color{blue}\\mathbf{v}_1}, {\\color{blue}\\mathbf{v}_2}, {\\color{blue}\\mathbf{v}_3} \\}.\n$$\n\n \n\nApplying the formula for $\\mathbf{v}_3$ in our case gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{3} & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]-(\\frac{2}{2})[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]-(-\\frac{3}{3})[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, $[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]$ is an orthogonal basis for $V.$" :tutorial/id #uuid "066a02aa-4c3e-5e75-8147-7e723bac3b9e" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3633 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "9b8d3614-1e0d-5f26-94a2-81b1f24e5df1" :step/math-academy-id 20262 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134690}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134690 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133796 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133605 :question/id #uuid "683556db-c65d-5279-ac14-7dfaa496db0a" :question/math-academy-id "e-5584"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f585bd05-7dac-55b1-a0ab-f8320242a5df" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050578}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134314} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134360} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186326922} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360629} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360630}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding an Orthogonal Basis of a Span Given Some Orthogonal Vectors"} :step/id #uuid "9ca65108-e04c-5dd4-9c81-fa10af333e97" :step/math-academy-id 20263 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134797}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134691 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186135057 :tutorial/content "But what do we do if the original basis we are given doesn't contain any orthogonal vectors? Well, then we make them orthogonal using the Gram-Schmidt process!\n\nGiven a basis $\\,\\{ \\mathbf{a}_1,\\mathbf{a}_2,\\ldots,\\mathbf{a}_n\\}$ for a non zero subspace $V$, we can get an orthogonal basis for $V$ by defining the vectors\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{1} & ={a}_{1} \\\\ {v}_{2} & ={a}_{2}-{\\text{proj}}_{{v}_{1}}\\,{a}_{2} \\\\ {v}_{3} & ={a}_{3}-{\\text{proj}}_{{v}_{1}}\\,{a}_{3}-{\\text{proj}}_{{v}_{2}}\\,{a}_{3} \\\\ ⋮\\,\\, & =\\,⋮ \\\\ {v}_{n} & ={a}_{n}-{\\text{proj}}_{{v}_{1}}\\,{a}_{n}-{\\text{proj}}_{{v}_{2}}\\,{a}_{n}-⋯-{\\text{proj}}_{{v}_{n-1}}\\,{a}_{n}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe set of vectors $\\{\\mathbf{v}_1,\\mathbf{v}_2,\\ldots, \\mathbf{v}_n\\}$ will be an orthogonal basis for $V$.\n\nHere, we build up the orthogonal basis one vector at a time, by taking away from each basis vector its orthogonal projection onto each of the $1$-dimensional subspaces spanned by one of the orthogonal basis vectors found already.\n\nFurthermore, if we want to find an *orthonormal* basis, we just have to normalize the vectors $\\mathbf{v}_i$ for $i=1,2,\\dots,n.$" :tutorial/id #uuid "a3f649a6-0268-539c-a952-a33eeaa25744" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3634 :tutorial/title "Gram-Schmidt Process"} :step/id #uuid "3d2dec80-add8-5035-b3b2-de9867a75aad" :step/math-academy-id 20264 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134692}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134692 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133797 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133606 :question/id #uuid "87d88110-e10d-553c-983d-6c22d3f19895" :question/math-academy-id "e-5585"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9870ad37-3e54-5403-936c-09a321d4a0cf" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134318} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134361} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272155} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272169} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360641} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186366132} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186366697}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding an Orthogonal Basis Using the Gram-Schmidt Process"} :step/id #uuid "d25a0643-79f7-54c9-aee4-b85ff777d4ae" :step/math-academy-id 20265} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134797 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133861 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133677 :question/id #uuid "06f90a75-b288-5975-9e74-994c180be4d6" :question/math-academy-id "e-8321"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "866ab12b-7f51-55f8-9e7b-e39f9c3b4b44" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134313} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134317} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186326904} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186326905} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360633} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186365038} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186365045} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186392415}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding an Orthogonal Basis For the Column Space of a Matrix Given Some Orthogonal Columns"} :step/id #uuid "b6a4c4a6-8981-577f-95e3-60a561156de9" :step/math-academy-id 31080 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134691}}] :activity/title "The Gram-Schmidt Process in the General Case" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186134007}] [{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134834} :activity/id #uuid "6fb1a6a6-6d39-5ea2-af30-b6e13fa3c1ac" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049881 :topic/id #uuid "1aba1eae-fe85-559f-b4fb-b8896b64623f" :topic/math-academy-id 2200} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134834 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186135110 :tutorial/content "When considering an $m \\times n$ matrix $A$ as a linear transformation\n\n$$\\mathbb{R}^n \\xrightarrow{\\quad A \\quad} \\mathbb{R}^m,$$\n\nthere are **four fundamental subspaces** that naturally occur. The first two are as follows:\n\n- The **column space** of $A,$ denoted $\\text{Col}(A),$ is a subspace of $\\mathbb{R}^m.$ This is the set of all linear combinations of the columns of $A.$\n- The **null space** of $A,$ denoted $\\text{Null}(A),$ is a subspace of $\\mathbb{R}^n.$ This is the set of all vectors in $\\mathbb{R}^n$ that, when pre-multiplied by $A,$ result in the zero-vector $\\mathbf{0}.$\n\nNow, notice that the transpose $A^T$ is an $n \\times m$ matrix and can be considered as the transformation\n\n$$\\mathbb{R}^m \\xrightarrow{\\quad A^T \\quad} \\mathbb{R}^n.$$\n\nSo, we have similar subspaces for the transpose $A^T{:}$\n\n- The column space of $A^T,$ denoted $\\text{Col}(A^T),$ is a subspace of $\\mathbb{R}^n.$ We call this the **row space** of $A$ since the span of the columns of $A^T$ is equivalent to the span of the rows of $A.$\n- The null space of $A^T,$ denoted $\\text{Null}(A^T),$ is a subspace of $\\mathbb{R}^m.$ We call this the **left null space** of $A$ since it is equivalent to the set of all vectors $\\mathbf y\\in\\mathbb R^m$ such that $\\mathbf y^T A = \\mathbf{0}.$\n\nLet's discuss how these spaces relate to each other:\n\n- We have two subspaces in $\\mathbb{R}^n$ that are orthogonal complements of each other: $$\\text{Col}(A^T) = \\text{Null}(A)^\\perp$$\n- We have two subspaces in $\\mathbb{R}^m$ that are orthogonal complements of each other: $$\\text{Col}(A) = \\text{Null}(A^T)^\\perp$$\n\nThe four fundamental subspaces of a matrix $A$ can be schematically visualized, as shown below.\n\n![](images/ce/ced4191955b6886491bf612c62344d2faf6e7fe8d49884446de0eb86851d22e8.png)\n\nTo see why $\\text{Col}(A)$ is the orthogonal complement of $\\text{Null}(A^T),$ note the following:\n\n- When calculating the product $A^T \\mathbf x,$ we multiply each row of $A^T$ by $\\mathbf x.$\n- Suppose that $\\mathbf x\\in \\text{Null}(A^T).$ Then, by definition, $A^T \\mathbf x = \\mathbf 0.$\n- Thus, $\\mathbf x$ must be orthogonal to each row of $A^T.$ Equivalently, this means that $\\mathbf x$ is orthogonal to each *column* of $A.$\n- Hence, $\\text{Null}(A^T)$ is orthogonal to $\\text{Col}(A).$\n\nWe can deduce that $\\text{Null}(A)$ is the orthogonal complement of $\\text{Col}(A^T)$ by similar reasoning." :tutorial/id #uuid "2b6f45b1-7cfa-5c62-9928-caeff36d1cc0" :tutorial/math-academy-id 5443 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "f59f0795-5e5a-517e-8142-7251b3810d58" :step/math-academy-id 33903 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134835}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134835 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133885 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133701 :question/id #uuid "47945f1d-ab72-5fe8-bc2c-43a69480b323" :question/math-academy-id "e-9090"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "5ada5d20-89d8-5bc9-9b10-172356be0eb4" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048181} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048613} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050656} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050727}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134071} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134077} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186362710} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186362717} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186362757} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186380566} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186407246}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying an Orthogonal Fundamental Subspaces of a Matrix"} :step/id #uuid "a063cde8-8349-5bde-8239-ea3b6c3146eb" :step/math-academy-id 33904 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134839}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134837 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133887 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133703 :question/id #uuid "bb597435-f43e-54de-a219-1af646efcb82" :question/math-academy-id "e-9099"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "1a36c7f7-4287-562b-94ea-047b38ede70f" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050028} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050996}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134070} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134076} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186362730} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186362777} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186362784} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186380580} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186380587} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186407243} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186407244} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186411864}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Dimensions of the Four Fundamental Subspaces of a Matrix"} :step/id #uuid "f05c128d-c2b3-55a6-b5af-aa01107d4052" :step/math-academy-id 33918 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134841}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134838 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133888 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133704 :question/id #uuid "bd1f48b4-8bfc-536d-8145-0effcf44a788" :question/math-academy-id "e-9104"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "75e1da77-a5dc-5f78-977e-0d6dae99c97e" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049539}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134072} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134073} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186362743} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186362750} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186362770} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186380573} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186397950} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186407245} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186407247}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Images of the Four Fundamental Subspaces of the Matrix Under Its Action"} :step/id #uuid "f70134b1-0b93-5597-a6b6-16fdc7fb1bad" :step/math-academy-id 33937 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134840}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134839 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186135111 :tutorial/content "Given an $m \\times n$ matrix $A,$ what is the image of $\\mathbb{R}^n$ under $A?$\n\nNote that pre-multiplying a vector $\\mathbf{x} \\in \\mathbb{R}^n$ by $A$ gives a linear combination of the columns of $A{:}$ $$\\underbrace{[\\begin{aligned}| & | & ⋯ & | \\\\ {a}_{1} & {a}_{2} & ⋯ & {a}_{n} \\\\ | & | & ⋯ & |\\end{aligned}]}_{\\large A} \\cdot \\underbrace{[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ ⋮ \\\\ {x}_{n}\\end{aligned}]}_{\\large \\mathbf{x}} = x_1 [\\begin{aligned}| \\\\ {a}_{1} \\\\ |\\end{aligned}] + x_2 [\\begin{aligned}| \\\\ {a}_{2} \\\\ |\\end{aligned}] +\\cdots+ x_n [\\begin{aligned}| \\\\ {a}_{n} \\\\ |\\end{aligned}]$$\n\nTherefore, by definition, the image of $\\mathbb{R}^n$ under the action of $A$ is $\\text{Col}(A),$ the column space of $A.$\n\n![](images/db/dbefb436195af5ffa8841f4119054426db50ac2987138a5912991cf42321e0dc.png)\n\nSimilarly, pre-multiplying a vector $\\mathbf{x} \\in \\mathbb{R}^m$ by $A^T$ gives a linear combination of the columns of $A^T{:}$ $$\\underbrace{[\\begin{aligned}| & | & ⋯ & | \\\\ {a}_{1}^{T} & {a}_{2}^{T} & ⋯ & {a}_{m}^{T} \\\\ | & | & ⋯ & |\\end{aligned}]}_{\\large A^T} \\cdot \\underbrace{[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ ⋮ \\\\ {x}_{m}\\end{aligned}]}_{\\large \\mathbf{x}} = x_1 [\\begin{aligned}| \\\\ {a}_{1}^{T} \\\\ |\\end{aligned}] + x_2 [\\begin{aligned}| \\\\ {a}_{2}^{T} \\\\ |\\end{aligned}] +\\cdots+ x_m [\\begin{aligned}| \\\\ {a}_{m}^{T} \\\\ |\\end{aligned}]$$\n\nTherefore, the image of $\\mathbb{R}^m$ under the action of $A^T$ must be $\\text{Col}(A^T).$ This is also the row space of $A,$ since a linear combination of the columns of $A^T$ is equivalent to a linear combination of the rows of $A.$\n\n![](images/c8/c82ab88289fc2d35ce354933937d908c54a9839bfa0fd126187a9723c58eeb27.png)\n\nWe can use similar reasoning to find the image of the four fundamental subspaces under the appropriate matrix." :tutorial/id #uuid "915420e9-f241-5e99-8274-32ffdfb79b9a" :tutorial/math-academy-id 5454 :tutorial/title "Images Under the Action of a Matrix and Its Transpose"} :step/id #uuid "e8b74e81-7593-5f0a-9136-22383330a688" :step/math-academy-id 33950 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134838}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134840 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186135112 :tutorial/content "Consider an $m \\times n$ matrix $A$ with rank $r.$ The dimensions of the four fundamental subspaces of $A$ are as follows:\n\n- $\\text{dim}(\\text{Col}(A)) = r.$ Since the column space is the span of the columns of $A,$ its dimension is the number of linearly independent columns which, by Gaussian elimination, is $\\text{rank}(A)=r.$\n- $\\text{dim}(\\text{Col}(A^T)) = r.$ It can be shown that the dimension of the row space of $A$ also equals $r.$ Therefore, $\\text{rank}(A^T)=\\text{rank}(A)=r.$\n- $\\text{dim}(\\text{Null}(A)) = n-r.$ Since $\\text{Null}(A)$ is the orthogonal complement of $\\text{Col}(A^T)$ in $\\mathbb{R}^n,$ we have that $$\\text{dim}(\\text{Null}(A)) = \\underbrace{\\text{dim}(\\mathbb{R}^n)}_{n} - \\underbrace{\\text{dim}(\\text{Col}(A^T))}_{r} = n-r.$$\n- $\\text{dim}(\\text{Null}(A^T)) = m-r.$ Since $\\text{Null}(A^T)$ is the orthogonal complement of $\\text{Col}(A)$ in $\\mathbb{R}^m,$ we have that $$\\text{dim}(\\text{Null}(A^T)) = \\underbrace{\\text{dim}(\\mathbb{R}^m)}_{m} - \\underbrace{\\text{dim}(\\text{Col}(A))}_{r} = m-r.$$\n\nLet's add these dimensions to the diagram below.\n\n![](images/1c/1c550b87048bba6a7c362f00f09faca74ca9b056a93698436d0994c120971356.png)" :tutorial/id #uuid "40af35b2-abb6-59b8-999a-9976edbb9ce8" :tutorial/math-academy-id 5455 :tutorial/title "Dimensions of the Four Fundamental Subspaces"} :step/id #uuid "957cde37-32d4-515e-ad96-75c96f7bfa57" :step/math-academy-id 33951 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134837}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134841 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186135113 :tutorial/content "Let's review what we have learned in this lesson.\n\nIf we consider an $m \\times n$ matrix $A$ of rank $r$ as a transformation from $\\mathbb{R}^n$ to $\\mathbb{R}^m,$ then there are four fundamental subspaces of $A{:}$\n\n- $\\text{Col}(A)$ and $\\text{Null}(A^T)$ are subspaces in $\\mathbb{R}^m$ with dimensions $r$ and $m-r,$ respectively.\n- $\\text{Col}(A^T)$ and $\\text{Null}(A)$ are subspaces in $\\mathbb{R}^n$ with dimensions $r$ and $n-r,$ respectively.\n\nFurthermore,\n\n- $\\text{Col}(A)$ and $\\text{Null}(A^T)$ are orthogonal complements in $\\mathbb{R}^m,$ and\n- $\\text{Null}(A)$ and $\\text{Col}(A^T)$ are orthogonal complements in $\\mathbb{R}^n.$\n\nFinally, we have that\n\n- the image of $\\text{Col}(A^T)$ under the action of $A$ is $\\text{Col}(A),$ and\n- the image of $\\text{Col}(A)$ under the action of $A^T$ is $\\text{Col}(A^T).$\n\nThese facts about the four fundamental subspaces of a matrix $A$ can be schematically visualized, as shown below.\n\n![](images/37/37f633d0bca1a599f7d1658c051f802da5d607d420beeb4bddbef5cd98c366ef.png)" :tutorial/id #uuid "190e1777-4f27-5c94-88bd-21c9992eb024" :tutorial/math-academy-id 5456 :tutorial/title "The Big Picture of the Four Fundamental Subspaces"} :step/id #uuid "8eef352e-273a-5614-b97d-8c357807e9a0" :step/math-academy-id 33952}] :activity/title "The Four Fundamental Subspaces of a Matrix" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186134041}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046673} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186407248} :activity/id #uuid "d12b0c27-35c4-517e-8eb3-41dab352b8fa" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049881 :topic/id #uuid "1aba1eae-fe85-559f-b4fb-b8896b64623f" :topic/math-academy-id 2200} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186407248 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186407246 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "61b8af3e-3b47-5aa2-91bd-6b1c4c03c696" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407219} {:answer/id #uuid "a0b64355-3c4a-5468-b79c-60409857f476" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407223} {:answer/id #uuid "bc41a449-214b-5c1b-8a85-1c8dc86ad746" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407227} {:answer/id #uuid "f4e3cd61-e48f-5faf-ad9f-c294bdd8ce47" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407236} {:answer/id #uuid "f62c90f5-3a7e-558e-82d7-d3e2ac3e06e4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407237}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186407227} :answer-field/id #uuid "65c8f326-9093-58bd-a6e1-bf36f4cf4e21" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407240}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "b9f78c95-881f-537a-b5e6-305264553498" :question/math-academy-id "q-109777" :question/problem "Which of the following subspaces must be orthogonal to $\\text{Col}(A)$ for every matrix $A?$\n\n- $\\text{Col}({A}^{T})$\n- $\\text{Null}({A}^{T})$\n- $\\text{Null}(A)$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that $\\text{Col}(A)$ consists of all linear combinations of the columns of $A$, that is, the rows of ${A}^{T}.$\n\nIf a vector is orthogonal to every linear combination of the rows of ${A}^{T},$ it must be orthogonal to each row of ${A}^{T}.$\nBut notice that when pre-multiplying a vector by ${A}^{T},$ we actually multiply each row of ${A}^{T}$ by that vector.\nSo, $\\text{Col}(A)$ must be orthogonal to the left null space of $A,$ which is $\\text{Null}({A}^{T}).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\"\n\n**Note:** In general, the four fundamental subspaces of a matrix $A$ can be schematically visualized, as shown below.\n\n![](images/de/de22dd5b352e1d5be48f4b6ed3b2a4657ae623c1bd288d3464559758ea334af7.png)"} :step/id #uuid "453b68d4-1d9d-50c9-a90c-51c8b99946a5" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186407250}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186407249 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186407245 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2ad86372-161b-56b5-b9b3-67cb9855544f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407216} {:answer/id #uuid "3e4b4700-8836-57b3-a254-6685beac57ed" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\text{Null}({A}^{T})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407217} {:answer/id #uuid "65001b28-0435-5b9d-91ec-614c84c822e3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{R}^{n}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407221} {:answer/id #uuid "bf467989-30ff-5c80-94cc-972d1936e437" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\text{Col}({A}^{T})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407228} {:answer/id #uuid "d63461e1-95f1-5efd-aec4-a9f50c64386c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{R}^{m}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407232}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186407228} :answer-field/id #uuid "32cd5975-d7c1-5dd8-91de-4834a553c50c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407239}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "b9c49e80-5070-56aa-b070-99b1fa2fdff3" :question/math-academy-id "q-109848" :question/problem "Let $A$ be a $m\\times n$ matrix. What is the image of ${R}^{m}$ under the action of ${A}^{T}?$" :question/worked-solution "First, notice that ${A}^{T}$ is a $n\\times m$ matrix.\n\nPre-multiplying a vector $x\\in {R}^{m}$ by ${A}^{T}$ gives a linear combination of the columns of ${A}^{T}:$\n\n$$\n{{[\\begin{aligned}| & | & ⋯ & | \\\\ {a}_{1}^{T} & {a}_{2}^{T} & ⋯ & {a}_{m}^{T} \\\\ | & | & ⋯ & |\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{{A}^{T}}\\cdot {{[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ ⋮ \\\\ {x}_{m}\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{x}={x}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}| \\\\ {a}_{1}^{T} \\\\ |\\end{aligned}]+{x}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}| \\\\ {a}_{2}^{T} \\\\ |\\end{aligned}]+⋯+{x}_{m}[\\begin{aligned}| \\\\ {a}_{m}^{T} \\\\ |\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nTherefore, the image of ${R}^{m}$ under the action of ${A}^{T}$ must be $\\text{Col}({A}^{T}),$ the row space of $A.$"} :step/id #uuid "6ef8f97d-5b2a-5ab0-998f-92729ef9dc31" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186407252}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186407250 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186407243 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "166f24b3-2b33-5589-9ba5-efd73efdf696" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407215} {:answer/id #uuid "794d7c6a-4727-5c98-8506-efa641e24334" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407222} {:answer/id #uuid "c4f31e2f-2172-581c-b46f-338027d98c9a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407229} {:answer/id #uuid "de4a68e5-c938-5191-8ef4-522e158bedb4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407233} {:answer/id #uuid "f4d32fbe-6d2e-5aca-9070-ed1ab0efc4fa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407235}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186407229} :answer-field/id #uuid "077cd26a-5956-5d07-8539-e101ab53d7f4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407238}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "5d75070f-88dd-5b20-8fec-c1f3c6de013c" :question/math-academy-id "q-109846" :question/problem "Consider a $5\\times 4$ matrix $A.$ If $\\text{dim}(\\text{Col}({A}^{T}))=3,$ then $\\text{dim}(\\text{Null}(A))=$" :question/worked-solution "The four fundamental subspaces of a matrix $A$ can be schematically visualized, as shown below.\n\n![](images/88/889b83fadd26b377c4dd34143c9b16541630c04e90de3d9b005e7147ead75ad5.png)\n\nIn our case, we are given that $m=5,$ $n=4,$ and $r=3.$ \n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{dim}(\\text{Null}(A)) & =n-r=4-3=1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "6f121dc0-6e9f-506e-8370-6a73e7b21516" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186407249}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186407251 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186407244 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0e0fd37b-833c-5581-bf94-abc93beffe65" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407214} {:answer/id #uuid "6203c04e-a6ad-568e-b086-f63c9a0600d1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407220} {:answer/id #uuid "a5ba999d-5dc0-5e42-af53-78dc73ee4de4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407224} {:answer/id #uuid "ba9ba337-a858-55b1-bf28-9f915ba4dcc2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407226} {:answer/id #uuid "e18de25a-171d-5acb-88a3-6812297610fa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407234}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186407226} :answer-field/id #uuid "e82e089a-caba-5439-8d15-a646e7c8e9fa" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407242}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "66ca173a-d952-58d1-b484-502590c17693" :question/math-academy-id "q-109873" :question/problem "Consider a $5\\times 6$ matrix $A.$ If $\\text{dim}(\\text{Col}({A}^{T}))=3,$ then $\\text{dim}(\\text{Null}({A}^{T}))=$" :question/worked-solution "The four fundamental subspaces of a matrix $A$ can be schematically visualized, as shown below.\n\n![](images/88/889b83fadd26b377c4dd34143c9b16541630c04e90de3d9b005e7147ead75ad5.png)\n\nIn our case, we are given that $m=5,$ $n=6,$ and $r=3.$ \n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{dim}(\\text{Null}({A}^{T})) & =m-r=5-3=2.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "9114f2aa-68cd-5878-a5bf-ad32cd59fd29"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186407252 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186407247 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0d3a4ec2-bd1c-569f-bc25-a735fbf6e8f7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407213} {:answer/id #uuid "4624e774-8f8e-50d7-922a-9f2511cb84dc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\text{Null}(AB)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407218} {:answer/id #uuid "ab8461c5-2db5-555b-98b8-ef3d5aadb4ce" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\text{Col}({B}^{T}{A}^{T})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407225} {:answer/id #uuid "ca1a752d-a1b9-5c75-a6eb-005f23fda936" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\text{Null}({B}^{T}{A}^{T})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407230} {:answer/id #uuid "cafe991c-8437-5728-ad05-fe57c0cef2d4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\text{Col}(AB)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407231}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186407231} :answer-field/id #uuid "a14d9fc2-b7d8-5af8-b07b-319f63dc6ba1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407241}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "de769de3-3ab7-5536-ae28-2dcbd744b48f" :question/math-academy-id "q-109861" :question/problem "Let $A$ and $B$ be $m\\times p$ and $p\\times n$ matrices, respectively. What is the image of $\\text{Col}({B}^{T}\\,{A}^{T})$ under the action of $AB?$" :question/worked-solution "Pre-multiplying a vector $x\\in {R}^{n}$ by $AB$ gives a linear combination of the columns of $AB:$\n\n$$\n{{[\\begin{aligned}| & | & ⋯ & | \\\\ (ab)_{1} & (ab)_{2} & ⋯ & (ab)_{n} \\\\ | & | & ⋯ & |\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{AB}\\cdot {{[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ ⋮ \\\\ {x}_{n}\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{x}={x}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}| \\\\ (ab)_{1} \\\\ |\\end{aligned}]+{x}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}| \\\\ (ab)_{2} \\\\ |\\end{aligned}]+⋯+{x}_{n}[\\begin{aligned}| \\\\ (ab)_{n} \\\\ |\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nTherefore, the image of ${R}^{n}$ under the action of $AB$ must be $\\text{Col}(AB),$ the column space of $AB.$\n\nFurthermore, since $\\text{Null}(AB)$ is mapped to the zero vector $0$ by the definition of the null subspace, the image of its orthogonal complement $\\text{Col}((AB)^{T})=\\text{Col}({B}^{T}\\,{A}^{T})$ must be $\\text{Col}(AB).$\n\nThis can be schematically visualized, as shown below.\n\n![](images/88/885bf8a3061f93ad400897a7273ffe4157d22dee20b8e4ee7ebb982b1c4319bb.png)"} :step/id #uuid "d8a9f51b-41b7-5b08-9832-d19d696012d7" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186407251}}] :activity/title "The Four Fundamental Subspaces of a Matrix" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407212}] [{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167794} :activity/id #uuid "fd917f34-57d2-5f05-a29a-d33c8b5c96a5" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049957 :topic/id #uuid "21390ce8-05ac-5fcf-ba45-89cb6f33aadc" :topic/math-academy-id 3147} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167790 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166883 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166488 :question/id #uuid "22a44ff4-55e7-51db-a54e-26ef23764fa4" :question/math-academy-id "e-12490"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d13b0d31-65b8-550f-b97b-f4ece63bf834" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049280} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049997} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050264}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167132} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167133} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281612} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281614} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281634} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281635}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding Points That Satisfy the Mean Value Theorem"} :step/id #uuid "360dd2dd-b48a-554c-9341-3a3e93d1e334" :step/math-academy-id 44978 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167791}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167791 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186168149 :tutorial/content "Recall that the average value of a continuous function $f(x)$ on a closed interval $D = [a,b]$ is given by\n\n$$\nf_\\textrm{ave} = \\dfrac{1}{\\textrm{Length}(D)}\\int_a^b f(x) \\: \\textrm{d}x.\n$$\n\nThere is an analogous definition for functions of two variables, stated below:\n\n> *Suppose $D$ is a closed and bounded region in $\\mathbb{R}^2$ which has non-zero finite area $\\textrm{Area}(D).$ If $f (x, y)$ is a continuous function on $D,$ then **the average value of $\\boldsymbol f$ on $\\boldsymbol D$** is given by*\n> \n> \n> $$\n> f_{\\textrm{ave}} =\\dfrac{1}{\\textrm{Area}(D)} \\, \\iint\\limits_D f(x,y) \\: \\textrm{d}A.\n> $$\n\nFor example, let's find the average value of the function \n\n\\[\nf(x,y) =x+3y^2+2\n\\]\n\nover the region \n\n\\[\nD = \\big\\{ (x,y) \\: : \\: 0 \\leq x \\leq 2, \\: 0 \\leq y \\leq 2 \\big\\}.\n\\] \n\nEarlier, we found that \n\n\\[\n\\iint\\limits_D f(x,y) \\: \\textrm{d}A = 28, \\qquad \\textrm{Area}(D) = 4.\n\\]\n\nTherefore, the average value of the function over [math]D[/math] is\n\n\\begin{align*}\nf_{\\textrm{ave}}  \t\n\n&= \\dfrac{1}{\\textrm{Area}(D)} \\, \\iint\\limits_D f(x,y) \\: \\textrm{d}A \\\\[5pt]\n\n&= \\dfrac{1}{4} \\cdot 28 \\\\[5pt]\n\n&=  7. \n\\end{align*}" :tutorial/id #uuid "c862ff42-4e5b-5bbb-aed6-3d9760d339b1" :tutorial/math-academy-id 7770 :tutorial/title "The Average Value of a Function"} :step/id #uuid "c77b7ad4-9d8d-58f4-bd79-4c0b0b47cfea" :step/math-academy-id 44981 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167792}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167792 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166884 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166489 :question/id #uuid "0a06a8ae-69d6-5ded-979e-149f650a1676" :question/math-academy-id "e-12491"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b43eea5b-9da3-5fe0-a28c-cc01aed3627a" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167134} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167135} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186235532} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281645} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281646} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281649} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281652} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186374220} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186374227} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186374234}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Average Value of a Function"} :step/id #uuid "c0151bcd-ba24-5681-9ec6-52888fa06a36" :step/math-academy-id 44982 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167793}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167793 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166885 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166490 :question/id #uuid "13800660-08b4-50af-93b4-1013bbf41e66" :question/math-academy-id "e-12492"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c4e5deee-e910-5bb1-9d9a-0b24886ab6c1" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049084}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167136} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167137} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281588} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281653} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281654} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281662} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281663} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186374241} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186411860}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Average Value of a Function Over a Polar Region"} :step/id #uuid "a1446b8a-1f48-5f9e-b01f-42db997299c2" :step/math-academy-id 44985} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167794 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186168150 :tutorial/content "Given a continuous function $f(x)$ on a closed interval $[a, b],$ the mean value theorem for integrals guarantees that there exists a number $c \\in [a,b]$ such that\n\n$$\n\\int_a^b f(x)\\, \\textrm d x = f(c) \\cdot (b-a),\n$$\n\nwhich can be visualized as shown below.\n\n![](images/0a/0afb9b15787eb89f73f322290bdbe2288027c54924d274c438773ce151a17291.png)\n\nWe have a similar theorem for functions with two variables. The so-called **mean value theorem for double integrals** states:\n\n> *If the function $f(x,y)$ is continuous on a closed and bounded region $D,$ then there exists a point $(a,b) \\in D$ such that*\n> \n> \n> $$\n> \\iint\\limits_D f(x,y) \\: \\textrm{d}A = f(a,b) \\cdot \\textrm{Area}(D).\n> $$\n\nThis has a nice geometrical interpretation when $f$ is non-negative on $D.$ In this case, the theorem tells us that there exists a point $(a,b) \\in D$ such that the volume of the solid enclosed between the surface $z=f(x,y)$ and the $xy$-plane over the region $D$ equals the volume of the right cylinder with base $D$ and height $f(a,b).$ \n\nThe diagram below shows the case when $D$ is rectangular, and the corresponding cylinder is a rectangular solid.\n\n![](images/8e/8ed477207ee61fbdaf963ed8a127e3f1d4cf76707d2eeb4b450fd49f78e15b7e.png)\n\nIn general, the point $(a,b)$ mentioned in the theorem is not unique. Let's see an example." :tutorial/id #uuid "b65eeb5f-8a44-55b9-819c-1c6c0d979d1e" :tutorial/math-academy-id 7778 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "037c5a22-eee3-557c-b683-370d2389ad82" :step/math-academy-id 45031 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167795}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167795 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186168151 :tutorial/content "Consider the continuous function \n\n$$\nf(x,y) = x+3y^2+2\n$$\n\nand the region $D$ given by\n\n$$\nD = \\big\\{ (x,y) \\: : \\: 0 \\leq x \\leq 2, \\: 0 \\leq y \\leq 2 \\big\\}.\n$$\n\nAccording to the mean value theorem for double integrals, there exists a point $(a,b) \\in D$ such that\n\n$$\n\\iint\\limits_D f(x,y) \\: \\textrm{d}A = f(a,b) \\cdot \\textrm{Area}(D).\n$$\n\nEvaluating the integral on the left-hand side, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{D}(x+3{y}^{2}+2)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{2}(x+3{y}^{2}+2)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{[xy+{y}^{3}+2y]}_{y=0}^{y=2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}(2x+12)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[{x}^{2}+12x]}_{x=0}^{x=2} \\\\  & =28.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we have\n\n$$\n\\textrm{Area}(D) = 2 \\cdot 2 = 4.\n$$\n\nSo, our mean-value equation simplifies to\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}28 & =f(a,b)\\cdot 4 \\\\ 7 & =f(a,b) \\\\ 7 & =a+3{b}^{2}+2 \\\\ a & =5-3{b}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis equation describes part of a parabola in the $xy$-plane, as shown below.\n\n![](images/46/46873c4ecb290380488e589082fc0759324d3540494f5b83e2d64db5d9cdf62a.png)\n\nTo find a particular point that satisfies the mean value theorem, we can set, for example, $b=1 \\in [0,2]$ and obtain $a=2.$ So, the point $(2,1)$ satisfies the mean value theorem for double integrals. Consequently, the two solids below must have the same volume.\n\n![](images/82/82d3a762ab323339570e6010038e75ce95f3fdbde958a2ffa876972cbf91e8fb.png)" :tutorial/id #uuid "b890c915-3364-5ac3-b88b-298ed92d9def" :tutorial/math-academy-id 7784 :tutorial/title "A Worked Example"} :step/id #uuid "32b5b570-9467-5bff-bc3b-29964ed86c09" :step/math-academy-id 45050 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167790}}] :activity/title "The Average Value of a Multivariable Function" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186166974}] [{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134753} :activity/id #uuid "cb02e51b-dee7-54a8-8f76-914d0b42e66b" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049997 :topic/id #uuid "24e61144-3626-5d23-b2e6-a33ee94f32f3" :topic/math-academy-id 2151} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134753 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186135082 :tutorial/content "How do we represent the double integral of a function over a non-rectangular region as a repeated integral? \n\nSpecifically, let's consider\n![](images/3a/3ac99dd8336c3a877fbf6ba63a3074a490b85c8a350adad00da423412dd60ee3.svg)\n\nwhere ![](images/59/5914524b1d3db63c66998b75ca1d00a5dc4e294d2a8f10fe384d2b2ebf2af38e.svg)  is the finite region enclosed by the lines ![](images/df/df9822243c11ec9f0fabca5cc578976d0bdea2c521b4f61964f5f3850a970504.svg), ![](images/1f/1fb9f2f73a09599931892c1dee7629975b4d851e5dd6f0d07b7c641bbf17bf54.svg), ![](images/23/238e977be23f692486c8bf68f6b40255435b3d1796cc50d260a014a8c670be7b.svg) and ![](images/b5/b5489c125683eb29c2cababe2e1b7094d6a16b89d09970c42af649df70d045e4.svg) as shown below.\n\n![](images/af/af0a1a74df8aec0d5189dc2402fc1840c25d4cd94d8861fa06110cae7b449843.png)\n\nFirst, notice that ![](images/59/5914524b1d3db63c66998b75ca1d00a5dc4e294d2a8f10fe384d2b2ebf2af38e.svg) is a *type I* region. \nTo determine the lower and upper functions, we draw a vertical arrow through the region.\n\n![](images/39/39629b33e25aed05bf9a5ca36180f984e7ff8f863c6966d47f60483d672a8af4.png)\n\nSo, the region ![](images/59/5914524b1d3db63c66998b75ca1d00a5dc4e294d2a8f10fe384d2b2ebf2af38e.svg) can be defined as \n\n![](images/78/78cf2ea7f960497f7cc9beea602bf6ca0e82b74491f9f56cce76130d965d4a64.svg)\n\nNow, we can express the integral as follows:\n\n![](images/3c/3c423b0d423203621ab89258a6fff4cea190279b4dcc7170c8a7070bf1f85e24.svg)\n\nAnd that's our answer! Notice that, when we have a type I region, \n\n- the limits for ![](images/1d/1d9b846cface9f9348e4081a0dd87dd254bf26663c12aa0d1a15aa24d2393261.svg) are functions of ![](images/5a/5aac2c81021f3f9e468e7840c5f95ece35c10b894bdae19ec1d247bdeb47ac6f.svg)\n\n- the limits for ![](images/fc/fcc5df999e3b49fae1685ee0ab5bd03709804e1dd49f2ee722c4269eb0f5506d.svg) are constants, and\n\n- we integrate over the variable ![](images/1d/1d9b846cface9f9348e4081a0dd87dd254bf26663c12aa0d1a15aa24d2393261.svg) before integrating over the variable ![](images/cf/cff7b257e3c71ec70dc7cd273bdd1cced4254993a43cffbfc338460c21bc7319.svg)" :tutorial/id #uuid "7c641021-bc5e-5b05-8a23-b198735ee5a3" :tutorial/math-academy-id 4261 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "4a5c014d-2a5f-5ef2-b451-9d6636c3241e" :step/math-academy-id 25905 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134754}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134754 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133833 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133648 :question/id #uuid "51467bae-f181-53ba-adf2-1218dc16275f" :question/math-academy-id "e-7024"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "2e891363-fea2-5de4-9bbb-67fddb077200" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050822}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134308} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134517} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348426} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351291} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186380922} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186380929} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186380936}] :knowledge-point/title "Representing a Double Integral as a Repeated Integral When the Limits are Given"} :step/id #uuid "3edf61a9-ab06-533a-ad5f-906f95245bb3" :step/math-academy-id 25906 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134755}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134755 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133834 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133649 :question/id #uuid "7f443df5-1c26-5af4-9541-e83522126197" :question/math-academy-id "e-7025"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "853b2770-5057-50aa-9c15-7426cf4cc6a4" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134307} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134320} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186326875} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186326882} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186326883} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351293} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186380943} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186380950} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186382476} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186394154} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408131}] :knowledge-point/title "Representing a Double Integral as a Repeated Integral When the Limits are Not Given"} :step/id #uuid "621aac0e-49f3-510c-b684-d0f438398280" :step/math-academy-id 25907 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134756}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134756 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186135083 :tutorial/content "Suppose that we want to evaluate the repeated integral\n\n$$\n\\int_{0}^{1}\\int_{x}^{2x} (x-2y) \\ \\mathrm{d}  y \\ \\mathrm{d}  x.\n$$\n\nNotice that the domain of integration is a type I region. \n\nTo compute this integral, we must bear the following important points in mind.\n\n- We start with the inner integral, as usual.\n\n- When integrating with respect to $y,$ we treat $x$ as though it were a constant.\n\n- The limits of integration for the inner integral are functions of $x.$ Once again, we treat $x$ as though it were a constant and substitute the integration limits as usual. After this step, there should be no more $y$'s in our integral.\n\n- Finally, we compute the outer integral.\n\nLet's apply these steps to solve the given problem.\n\nFirst, we evaluate the inner integral by integrating with respect to $y,$ treating $x$ as a constant:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}[{\\int }_{x}^{2x}(x-2y)\\text{ }dy]\\,dx & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}[xy-{y}^{2}]_{x}^{2x}\\text{ }dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}[x\\cdot (2x)-{(2x)}^{2}]-[x\\cdot (x)-(x)^{2}]dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}[2{x}^{2}-4{x}^{2}]-[{x}^{2}-{x}^{2}]dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}-2{x}^{2}\\,dx \\\\  & =-2{\\int }_{0}^{1}{x}^{2}\\,dx\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we integrate with respect to $x\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-2{\\int }_{0}^{1}{x}^{2}\\,dx & =-2{[\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{3}{[{x}^{3}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{3}[{1}^{3}-{0}^{3}] \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "3698e7e3-4bc2-5db0-96b5-172131284b4a" :tutorial/math-academy-id 4262 :tutorial/title "Evaluating Repeated Integrals With Variable Limits"} :step/id #uuid "59bc86e8-4dae-564b-ab2b-c8ac5309d3be" :step/math-academy-id 25908 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134757}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134757 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133835 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133650 :question/id #uuid "b151be22-91a1-5303-b86d-4f9ba748f000" :question/math-academy-id "e-7026"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a28c4320-e2dd-5195-81ac-b910c2328691" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050207}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134194} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134520} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186268853} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186268854} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186268855} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323929} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323951} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351294} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186380964} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186382079} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186382483} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408132}] :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating a Repeated Integral Defined Over a Type I Region"} :step/id #uuid "9095d8fd-3ce2-5af6-bda4-4a49a820addf" :step/math-academy-id 25909 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134758}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134758 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133836 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133651 :question/id #uuid "24dd94c8-2821-5614-9ccf-43c3656267f4" :question/math-academy-id "e-7027"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "69f6b2a7-7a3a-5c2c-90db-5aa321784048" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134193} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134195} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323953} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351295} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351296} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351369} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351370} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351371} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186380971} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186380978} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186382059} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186411861}] :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Double Integral Defined Over a Type I Region"} :step/id #uuid "bb2a0a7b-340c-53a3-a510-9a559cc6d898" :step/math-academy-id 25911}] :activity/title "Double Integrals Over Type I Regions" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186134020}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046659} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408133} :activity/id #uuid "74e0d3a9-0075-5d33-b25c-7e801fde9553" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049997 :topic/id #uuid "24e61144-3626-5d23-b2e6-a33ee94f32f3" :topic/math-academy-id 2151} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408133 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323929 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "04d2a009-9a40-5d25-99e2-4f97af963231" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{11}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321192} {:answer/id #uuid "2c533ee8-f9db-5f4a-a560-8035da471b44" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{6}{11}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321490} {:answer/id #uuid "3a0552d2-cfa6-5c1e-901c-be3cad62fa64" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{3}{11}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321590} {:answer/id #uuid "70ec84ef-6655-53bf-9eb0-7897a3753db2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{11}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322030} {:answer/id #uuid "80d50f5a-f000-539b-a1db-5c8a13952505" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{11}{12}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322160}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186322160} :answer-field/id #uuid "4b2db87a-5385-53da-8e27-89a6fe772ff2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323309}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "39356ac1-0a31-5e6e-9a6f-6a96bbbbe55c" :question/math-academy-id "q-37683" :question/problem "Calculate ${\\int }_{1/2}^{1}{\\int }_{x}^{1/x}\\frac{y}{{x}^{2}}\\,dy\\,dx.$" :question/worked-solution "The integral is defined over a type I region.\n\nFirst, we evaluate the inner integral by integrating with respect to $y$, treating $x$ as a constant:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1/2}^{1}{\\int }_{x}^{1/x}\\frac{y}{{x}^{2}}\\,dy\\,dx & ={\\int }_{1/2}^{1}\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}[{\\int }_{x}^{1/x}y\\,dy]dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1/2}^{1}\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}{[\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2}]}_{x}^{1/x}dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1/2}^{1}\\frac{1}{2{x}^{2}}(\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}-{x}^{2})dx \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{1/2}^{1}(\\frac{1}{{x}^{4}}-1)\\,dx\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we integrate with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{1/2}^{1}(\\frac{1}{{x}^{4}}-1)\\,dx & =\\frac{1}{2}{[-\\frac{1}{3{x}^{3}}-x]}_{1/2}^{1} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}[\\frac{1}{3}+1-(\\frac{8}{3}+\\frac{1}{2})] \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}(-\\frac{11}{6}) \\\\  & =\\frac{11}{12}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "098028bf-3706-5127-9bb7-debd78e7b253" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408136}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408134 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408132 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "100fac5f-cc12-56dc-9567-ee01010ba781" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186408119} {:answer/id #uuid "1377aca7-a017-58fe-8e23-256c042e11b6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186408120} {:answer/id #uuid "3aabdbf2-aad9-5028-8b69-34e437771408" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{14}{15}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186408121} {:answer/id #uuid "41ea5617-4c16-5bdc-a231-c9432f86dc81" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{18}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186408122} {:answer/id #uuid "93d7f804-0816-5794-84e7-a2fe566f8c71" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{7}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186408127}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408121} :answer-field/id #uuid "85ef2574-1c06-549a-a456-4b22b82491c8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186408130}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "ffe5914e-4912-5ff3-9b15-3a1462960003" :question/math-academy-id "q-132285" :question/problem "${\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{{x}^{2}}^{1}({x}^{2}+2y)\\,dy\\,dx=$" :question/worked-solution "The integral is defined over a type I region.\n\nFirst, we evaluate the inner integral by integrating with respect to $y$, treating $x$ as a constant:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{{x}^{2}}^{1}({x}^{2}+2y)\\,dy\\,dx & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}[{\\int }_{{x}^{2}}^{1}({x}^{2}+2y)\\,dy]dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}[{x}^{2}y+{y}^{2}]_{{x}^{2}}^{1}\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}({x}^{2}+1-{x}^{4}-{x}^{4})\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}({x}^{2}+1-2{x}^{4})\\,dx\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we integrate with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}({x}^{2}+1-2{x}^{4})\\,dx & ={[\\frac{1}{3}{x}^{3}+x-\\frac{2}{5}{x}^{5}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}+1-\\frac{2}{5} \\\\  & =\\frac{14}{15}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "20fb188a-faf6-5a84-b51f-1b3b2dd89239"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408135 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351296 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0a7ddc79-cbfd-59b5-9638-d178b71104a6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "e" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348536} {:answer/id #uuid "4b79160f-37ab-5192-8514-437d9b279188" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349138} {:answer/id #uuid "544819eb-b6d1-5a40-bb3a-d34d78eef696" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{e}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349218} {:answer/id #uuid "a06fce6a-f06f-595a-9863-76102c8d8305" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349871} {:answer/id #uuid "f06aa1a1-4329-543e-8ec1-01bf70f9f867" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350550}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186349871} :answer-field/id #uuid "08077c01-ccff-5e05-a424-ef1243bb7696" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350720}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "b8f9d362-d296-527d-b3eb-8711496d6537" :question/math-academy-id "q-81367" :question/problem "Evaluate the double integral\n${∬}_{D}\\frac{1}{yx\\operatorname{ln}⁡y}\\,dA,$\nwhere $D$ is the finite region enclosed by the lines\n$x=e,$ $y=e$ and the curve $y={e}^{x}.$" :question/worked-solution "Let's make a sketch of our region.\n\n![](images/aa/aa635ca5ef3198fea56d684b034de476a72e4ace2a4c51b37c6c59977036eb76.png)\n\nNotice that $D$ is a type I region.\n\n- The line $y=e$ intercepts the curve $y={e}^{x}$ at $x=1.$\nSo, the $x$-limits are \n$1\\le x\\le e.$\n\n- To determine the lower and upper functions, we draw a vertical arrow through the region.\n\n![](images/b7/b78021a06c9fea100b75c66a619c425345cda3666d84da018895be15a53af06d.png)\n\nAs a result, the integral is defined over the following type I region: \n\n$$\nD={(x,y)\\,:\\,1\\le x\\le e,\\,e\\le y\\le {e}^{x}}\n$$\n\nSo, we can express the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}\\frac{1}{yx\\operatorname{ln}⁡y}\\,dA={\\int }_{1}^{e}{\\int }_{e}^{{e}^{x}}\\frac{1}{yx\\operatorname{ln}⁡y}\\,dy\\,dx\n$$\n\nFirst, we evaluate the inner integral by integrating with respect to $y$, treating $x$ as a constant:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{e}{\\int }_{e}^{{e}^{x}}\\frac{1}{yx\\operatorname{ln}⁡y}\\,dy\\,dx & ={\\int }_{1}^{e}\\frac{1}{x}[{\\int }_{e}^{{e}^{x}}\\frac{1}{y\\operatorname{ln}⁡y}\\,dy]\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{e}\\frac{1}{x}{[\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{ln}⁡y)]}_{e}^{{e}^{x}}\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{e}\\frac{1}{x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{ln}⁡{e}^{x})-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{ln}⁡e))\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{e}\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{x}dx\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we integrate with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{e}\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{x}dx & ={[\\frac{{ln}^{2}⁡x}{2}]}_{1}^{e} \\\\  & =\\frac{{ln}^{2}⁡e}{2}-\\frac{{ln}^{2}⁡1}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "597be508-6945-5d32-9627-435849b9418f" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408134}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408136 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323953 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0b726bfa-ba4c-5d91-9f6d-6a3764d8755d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321242} {:answer/id #uuid "1594e17d-7011-5f7d-aa6a-12336ec25a8a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)-\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321322} {:answer/id #uuid "2b5f94c5-0d1b-5f79-944c-8cb9d821beed" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}+\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321479} {:answer/id #uuid "564621d9-f224-5fff-b159-cf3615e51a9b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)+\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321828} {:answer/id #uuid "d4f698ad-bda0-5b89-8175-c64721d84bac" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322805}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186321479} :answer-field/id #uuid "9eb45d35-7041-5bf4-8524-44adec58ddbf" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323477}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "ad4a8e76-c297-5cff-8131-747df131b332" :question/math-academy-id "q-37865" :question/problem "Evaluate the double integral\n${∬}_{D}\\frac{1}{(1+x+y)^{2}}\\,dA,$\nwhere $D$ is the finite region enclosed by the line\n$y=1-x$ and the coordinate axes." :question/worked-solution "Let's make a sketch of our region.\n\n![](images/95/95ebd0a4eb874972b459e53eecf1d57650f63284698fcad967c09620ac01cd99.png)\n\nNotice that $D$ is a type I region.\n\n- The line $y=1-x$ intercepts the $x$-axis at $x=1.$\nSo, the $x$-limits are \n$0\\le x\\le 1.$\n\n- To determine the lower and upper functions, we draw a vertical arrow through the region.\n\n![](images/d0/d048f20f2299722cee598cc1005237627ecdd7643e996b269f26b5a096f01f2f.png)\n\nAs a result, the integral is defined over the following type I region: \n\n$$\nD={(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 1,\\,0\\le y\\le 1-x}\n$$\n\nSo, we can express the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}\\frac{1}{(1+x+y)^{2}}\\,dA={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1-x}\\frac{1}{(1+x+y)^{2}}\\,dy\\,dx\n$$\n\nFirst, we evaluate the inner integral by integrating with respect to $y$, treating $x$ as a constant:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1-x}\\frac{1}{(1+x+y)^{2}}\\,dy\\,dx & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}[{\\int }_{0}^{-x+1}\\frac{1}{(1+x+y)^{2}}\\,dy]\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{[-\\frac{1}{1+x+y}]}_{0}^{-x+1}\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(-\\frac{1}{1+x+(1-x)}-(-\\frac{1}{1+x+0}))\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(-\\frac{1}{2}+\\frac{1}{1+x})dx\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we integrate with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}(-\\frac{1}{2}+\\frac{1}{1+x})dx & ={[-\\frac{1}{2}x+\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1+x)]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}+\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)-(0+\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1)) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}+\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "780a6e09-0224-5a5e-9ae0-33ed21780a56" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408137}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408137 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408131 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "58f2f8c3-eca9-558a-ae7f-9a78208457ea" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-1}^{-{x}^{2}}{\\int }_{-1}^{1}({x}^{2}y-3x)\\,dy\\,dx" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186408123} {:answer/id #uuid "647e13d7-fe24-5171-9f25-59459873bf42" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-{x}^{2}}^{-1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}({x}^{2}y-3x)\\,dy\\,dx" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186408124} {:answer/id #uuid "93175eb3-2514-5cc0-a423-e088c96ac28d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{-1}^{-{x}^{2}}({x}^{2}y-3x)\\,dy\\,dx" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186408125} {:answer/id #uuid "93b1f2e7-cf7b-5b8a-b5bb-c62e89e19fa2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-1}^{1}{\\int }_{-1}^{-{x}^{2}}({x}^{2}y-3x)\\,dy\\,dx" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186408126} {:answer/id #uuid "ef3be0a4-83b9-54e6-81b8-88c21e425fc7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-1}^{1}{\\int }_{-{x}^{2}}^{-1}({x}^{2}y-3x)\\,dy\\,dx" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186408128}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408126} :answer-field/id #uuid "3df51106-2408-5663-8451-c957471724da" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186408129}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "3676ec52-02c6-5b83-a547-e552c5ceb042" :question/math-academy-id "q-81338" :question/problem "![](images/41/4121d975786de30f34e493e7ac49e5cdc8e66621e800321008ba4ba501fb818f.png)\n\nIf $D$ is the finite region enclosed by the curves $y=-{x}^{2},$ and $y=-1,$ as shown above, then ${∬}_{D}({x}^{2}y-3x)\\,dA=$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's represent $D$ as a type I region. \n\n- To determine the $x$-limits, we need to find the $x$-coordinates of the points at which the curves intersect. So, we set the following equation and solve it for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-{x}^{2} & =-1 \\\\ {x}^{2} & =1 \\\\ x & =\\pm 1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $x$-limits are \n$-1\\le x\\le 1.$\n\n- To determine the lower and upper functions, we draw a vertical arrow through the region.\n\n![](images/ef/ef6450f795d0edea8a3217e28d7f1a966d5b1c62f9241826213de0485af5f860.png)\n\nAs a result, we get\n\n$$\nD={(x,y)\\,:\\,-1\\le x\\le 1,\\,-1\\le y\\le -{x}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can express the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}({x}^{2}y-3x)\\,dA & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}{\\int }_{-1}^{-{x}^{2}}({x}^{2}y-3x)\\,dy\\,dx\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "c5dbc16c-f16e-55e5-b468-49e1c21fdfb0" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408135}}] :activity/title "Double Integrals Over Type I Regions" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186408118}] [{:activity/expected-seconds 1200.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186158396} :activity/id #uuid "97fd4430-aac7-55ae-afde-da3c6748fa89" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050309 :topic/id #uuid "3ece60c2-2440-5884-ade0-5ec738e1db78" :topic/math-academy-id 2819} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186158396 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186158469 :tutorial/content "For a $2 \\times 2$ matrix differential equation\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x}'(t) = A \\mathbf{x}(t),\n$$\n\n \n\nwe know that if $A$ has distinct eigenvalues, the general solution is\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x}(t) = c_1{\\color{black}\\mathbf{v}_1} e^{{\\color{black}\\lambda_1} t} + c_2{\\color{black}\\mathbf{v}_2} e^{{\\color{black}\\lambda_2} t}.\n$$\n\nThis formula works even when the eigenvalues $\\lambda_1, \\lambda_2$ are complex. However, using it directly requires working with complex exponentials, which can be cumbersome.\n\nA more direct method is to find just *one* of the complex solutions, say $\\mathbf x_1(t) = \\mathbf{v}_1 e^{{\\color{black}\\lambda_1} t},$ and separate it into its *real and imaginary parts*.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}(t) & ={v}_{1}{e}^{{\\lambda }_{1}t} \\\\  & ={{{w}_{1}(t)}_{⏟}}_{\\text{Real Part}}\\,+\\,\\text{i}\\,\\,\\,{{{w}_{2}(t)}_{⏟}}_{\\text{Imaginary Part}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe general *real-valued* solution is then a linear combination of these two parts:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf x(t) = c_1 \\mathbf w_1(t) + c_2 \\mathbf w_2(t)\n$$\n\n**Note:** This method may seem surprising. Why can we construct the general solution using only *one* of the complex solutions, $\\mathbf x_1(t)?$\n\nThe reason is that the second solution, $\\mathbf x_2(t),$ doesn't provide any new information. For a real matrix $A,$ the complex eigenvalues and eigenvectors come in *conjugate pairs*, which means $\\mathbf x_2(t)$ is simply the complex conjugate of $\\mathbf x_1(t).$\n\nSince $\\mathbf x_2(t) = \\mathbf w_1 (t) - \\textrm{i} \\, \\mathbf w_2(t),$ it is also a linear combination of the real and imaginary parts of $\\mathbf x_1(t).$\n\nTo build our general solution, we just need two *linearly independent* real solutions. The key facts are the following:\n\n- For a system with complex eigenvalues, the real part $\\mathbf w_1(t)$ and the imaginary part $\\mathbf w_2(t)$ are *always* linearly independent.\n\n- Since they are two linearly independent solutions, they can form the basis for the general solution.\n\nSo, we can use them directly and bypass the need to work with $\\mathbf x_2(t)$ at all.\n\nLet's see a concrete example." :tutorial/id #uuid "a93ff6d6-1eb0-5e42-8b69-ae73fc891839" :tutorial/math-academy-id 4283 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "24b6ab49-7a81-5a3b-a140-aabc60a9f348" :step/math-academy-id 577657 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186158397}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186158397 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157448 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157069 :question/id #uuid "2a284986-5300-5bad-a33c-aa2bc47712b1" :question/math-academy-id "e-7043"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9e3dd935-2058-5b28-bdbb-0c45586730d9" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048535} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048973}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157741} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157748} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308528} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308529} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308531} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186414138}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the General Solution to a System of Linear Equations: Purely Imaginary Eigenvalues"} :step/id #uuid "33bad08d-6b45-5628-aba5-8acb1bac5825" :step/math-academy-id 577658 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186158398}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186158398 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157449 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157070 :question/id #uuid "8e797f4b-fe53-5d0a-a4b0-8fb51ee04c92" :question/math-academy-id "e-7044"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ec2743cd-4e26-59d5-9368-7a77fa4d0527" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050098}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157742} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157743} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308545} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308546} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308548} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186391373} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186391380} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186391394} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186414137}] :knowledge-point/title "Solving an Initial Value Problem: Purely Imaginary Eigenvalues"} :step/id #uuid "9044a6cf-4cf6-55f0-81fe-5f88ad0b36be" :step/math-academy-id 577661 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186158399}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186158399 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157450 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157071 :question/id #uuid "53243f33-77a1-59f1-bce9-3f084e087593" :question/math-academy-id "e-7045"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e3692fa3-14c5-5db9-8fee-e99ae35e386f" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157744} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157745} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308557} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308558} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308560} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308564} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308565} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186414136}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the General Solution to a System of Linear Equations: Complex Eigenvalues"} :step/id #uuid "671c8d98-22a1-54a0-98cc-259902e11176" :step/math-academy-id 577664 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186158400}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186158400 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157451 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157072 :question/id #uuid "9bd3af1e-e569-5cf1-8c4e-97b9ecc35174" :question/math-academy-id "e-7046"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b1633293-c176-5bfe-8fa9-cfd872ceca26" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157746} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157747} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308581} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308582} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308583} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308584} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186391446} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186391453} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186414135} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186414139}] :knowledge-point/title "Solving an Initial Value Problem: Complex Eigenvalues"} :step/id #uuid "911f4d76-3073-51d2-8506-25330ab817ac" :step/math-academy-id 577667}] :activity/title "Solving Homogeneous Systems of ODEs With Complex Eigenvalues" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186157477}] [{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186163006} :activity/id #uuid "f2f0c9ac-d76a-5aea-a567-9866ed3c86f0" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050351 :topic/id #uuid "41e17e9e-452a-583f-8f59-6524e41f2803" :topic/math-academy-id 3049} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186163006 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186163290 :tutorial/content "We often want to measure the strength of the (linear) dependence between two random variables. One measure that we use for this purpose is covariance.\n\nThe **covariance** between two discrete random variables $X$ and $Y$ is defined as\n\n$$\n\\textrm{Cov}[X, Y] = \\textrm{E}\\Big[\\left(X - \\textrm{E}[X]\\right)\\left(Y - \\textrm{E}[Y]\\right) \\Big]\n$$\n\nLet's break this formula down a bit:\n\n- $\\left(X - \\textrm{E}[X]\\right)$ is the deviation of $X$ from its mean\n- $\\left(Y - \\textrm{E}[Y]\\right)$ is the deviation of $Y$ from its mean\n\nTherefore, the covariance measures the expected value of the product of these deviations.\n\nThe covariance can also be expressed as\n\n$$\n\\textrm{Cov}[X, Y] =\\sum_{(x,y)\\in S}(x - \\textrm E[X])(y - \\textrm E[Y])f(x,y)\n$$\n\nwhere $f(x,y)$ is the joint PMF of $X$ and $Y$, and $S$ is the joint support.\n\nLet's sketch a few diagrams to get a feel for what the covariance tells us.\n\n- A *positive* covariance means that if $X$ increases, then $Y$ is likely to increase. We say that $X$ and $Y$ are **positively correlated**.![](images/bb/bbfae3f0fb38bc8652cd26716e4b0b128cf1f2039350267034d97161d7f5dac4.png)\n- A *negative* covariance means that if $X$ increases, then $Y$ is likely to decrease. We say that $X$ and $Y$ are **negatively correlated**.![](images/fd/fd45053fdc39f070e3438c3b8c61fe01613201595cfe00bd4cc6c6e481bf73ab.png)\n\nNote the following:\n\n- If $X$ and $Y$ are independent, then\n$\\textrm{Cov}[X, Y] = 0$.\nThe reverse implication does not always hold! For example, two random variables can have zero covariance yet be dependent. This is because covariance measures *linear* dependence only.\n- The covariance of a random variable with itself is its variance:\n$Cov[X, X]|= E[(X - E[X])(X - E[X])]; = E[(X - E[X])^{2}]; = Var[X]$\n\n- Aside from indicating whether two random variables are positively or negatively correlated, the covariance *by itself* does not tell us very much. For example, if\n$\\textrm{Cov}[X_1, Y_1] > \\textrm{Cov}[X_2, Y_2] > 0$\nthis does not necessarily mean that the dependence between $X_1$ and $Y_1$ is somehow \"stronger\" than the dependence between $X_2$ and $Y_2$. This is because the covariance does not take the variance of the individual random variables into account. In a future lesson, we'll use the covariance to define the so-called **correlation coefficient,** which *does* consider the variance of the individual random variables and so *can* be used to compare the relative strength of the linear dependence between two pairs of random variables." :tutorial/id #uuid "aa0d2bd6-235b-5d66-abc0-b8869af0ae0e" :tutorial/math-academy-id 5624 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "a07ef0b9-b98f-5301-a5fd-80d0a003d49d" :step/math-academy-id 34589 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186163114}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186163008 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186163292 :tutorial/content "Let's now prove the following formula:\n\n$$\n\\textrm{Cov}[X, Y] =\\textrm E [XY] - \\textrm E[X]\\cdot \\textrm E[Y]\n$$\n\nWe start with the definition of covariance, given by\n\n$$\n\\textrm{Cov}[X, Y] =\\textrm E\\Big[\\left(X- \\textrm E[X]\\right)\\left(Y-\\textrm E[Y]\\right)\\Big]\n$$\n\nExpanding the product in the brackets, we get\n\n$$\nCov[X, Y] = E[XY - X \\cdot E[Y] - E[X] \\cdot Y + E[X] \\cdot E[Y]]\n$$\n\nNow, recall that if $a$ and $b$ are constants, then\n\n$$\n\\textrm E[a]=a, \\qquad \\textrm{E}[aX + bY] = a\\cdot \\textrm{E}[X] + b \\cdot \\textrm{E}[Y]\n$$\n\nSince $\\textrm{E}[X]$ and are $\\textrm{E}[Y]$ are both constants, the covariance formula simplifies as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nCov[X, Y] &= E[XY - X \\cdot E[Y] - E[X] \\cdot Y + E[X] \\cdot E[Y]] \\\\\n&= E[XY] - E[X \\cdot E[Y]] - E[E[X] \\cdot Y] + E[E[X] \\cdot E[Y]] \\\\\n&= E[XY] - E[X] \\cdot E[Y] - E[X] \\cdot E[Y] + E[X] \\cdot E[Y] \\\\\n&= E[XY] - E[X] \\cdot E[Y]\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "57fd5059-dc1e-5473-9f12-db4e68dc88af" :tutorial/math-academy-id 5635 :tutorial/title "Proof of the Covariance Formula"} :step/id #uuid "7b510b48-c0dd-5ca6-a760-7383939e9b88" :step/math-academy-id 34611} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186163009 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186163293 :tutorial/content "If $X$ and $Y$ are continuous random variables, the covariance between $X$ and $Y$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\textrm{Cov}[X, Y] =\\iint_\\limits{\\mathbb R^2}(x - \\textrm E[X])(y - \\textrm E[Y])f(x,y) \\, \\textrm d x\\textrm d y\n$$\n\nAs is the case with discrete random variables, it's often easier to compute the covariance using the following formula:\n\n$$\n\\textrm{Cov}[X,Y] = \\textrm{E} [XY] - \\textrm{E}[X]\\cdot \\textrm{E}[Y]\n$$\n\nSuppose we're given that the random variables $X$ and $Y$ have the joint probability density function\n\n$$\n{\\frac{1}{2}(x + \\frac{y}{2}),\\begin{vmatrix}0 \\le x \\le 1, 0 \\le y \\le 2, \\\\ 0,\\end{vmatrix}otherwise\n$$\n\nTo compute the covariance of this distribution, we first use the rule of the lazy statistician to find $\\textrm{E}[XY]$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nE[XY] &= \\int_{0}^{2}\\int_{0}^{1}xyf(x, y)dxdy \\\\\n&= \\int_{0}^{2}\\int_{0}^{1}xy \\cdot \\frac{1}{2}(x + \\frac{y}{2})dxdy \\\\\n&= \\int_{0}^{2}\\frac{1}{2}y\\int_{0}^{1}(x^{2} + \\frac{1}{2}xy)dxdy \\\\\n&= \\int_{0}^{2}\\frac{1}{2}y[\\frac{1}{3}x^{3} + \\frac{1}{4}x^{2}y]_{x = 0}^{x = 1}dy \\\\\n&= \\int_{0}^{2}\\frac{1}{2}y[\\frac{1}{3} + \\frac{1}{4}y]dy \\\\\n&= \\int_{0}^{2}(\\frac{1}{6}y + \\frac{1}{8}y^{2})dy \\\\\n&= [\\frac{1}{12}y^{2} + \\frac{1}{24}y^{3}]_{y = 0}^{y = 2} \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{12} \\cdot 2^{2} + \\frac{1}{24} \\cdot 2^{3} \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{3} + \\frac{1}{3} \\\\\n&= \\frac{2}{3}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing methods discussed in previous lessons, we can compute $\\textrm{E}[X]$ and $\\textrm{E}[Y]{:}$\n\n$$\n\\textrm{E}[X] = \\dfrac{7}{12}, \\qquad \\textrm{E}[Y] = \\dfrac76\n$$\n\nFinally, using the covariance formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nCov[X, Y] &= E[XY] - E[X] \\cdot E[Y] \\\\\n&= \\frac{2}{3} - (\\frac{7}{12})(\\frac{7}{6}) \\\\\n&=-\\frac{1}{72}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRemember that if two random variables are independent then their covariance equals zero. Let's see an example." :tutorial/id #uuid "066864ea-8aaa-5094-bf78-7c862b1dd64e" :tutorial/math-academy-id 5637 :tutorial/title "Covariance for Continuous Random Variables"} :step/id #uuid "bb7de1b2-0c12-5011-8752-ebd8e223e8d9" :step/math-academy-id 34623 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186163113}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186163111 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162132 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186161698 :question/id #uuid "9716c386-896d-5b3b-a0e6-d6d3d1a83cbf" :question/math-academy-id "e-10348"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "130d80d7-d0f9-5979-b966-e6c0bf7a27e9" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050835}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162426} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162444}] :knowledge-point/title "Calculating Covariance Using the Definition"} :step/id #uuid "6c5298f8-ee76-510b-9852-47db3df79bd2" :step/math-academy-id 38977 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186163115}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186163113 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162134 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186161700 :question/id #uuid "ebf98f0c-765c-5cc4-bfa0-89ffefe450f4" :question/math-academy-id "e-10356"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "fab3dcab-d386-5f54-bfba-f2e5951af9ba" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050368} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050682}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162429} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162437} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186411854}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Covariance Given a Joint Probability Density Function"} :step/id #uuid "f84ee295-698e-570e-8b1d-f95c9890f4ba" :step/math-academy-id 39007 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186163008}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186163114 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162135 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186161701 :question/id #uuid "5e69ba34-d7f9-512b-9d53-f00f8f556049" :question/math-academy-id "e-10357"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b4549d88-dfbd-57e0-a673-99e394563613" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048466} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049126}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162436} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162445}] :knowledge-point/title "Stating the Covariance Using the Definition"} :step/id #uuid "d02007b7-e508-588a-8b76-1a1887b8fb3d" :step/math-academy-id 39008 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186163111}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186163115 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186163343 :tutorial/content "You may have noticed that computing the covariance using the definition is quite a lot of work. Is there a formula that makes it easier?\n\nIndeed, there is. It can be shown that\n\n$$\n\\textrm{Cov}[X, Y] =\\textrm E [XY] - \\textrm E [X]\\cdot \\textrm E [Y]\n$$\n\nWe'll prove this formula at the end of the lesson. But for now, let's get some practice at applying it." :tutorial/id #uuid "8bb09eee-27a4-5c3e-b10d-923dbaa105df" :tutorial/math-academy-id 6455 :tutorial/title "A Formula for Quickly Computing Covariance"} :step/id #uuid "54ee6fbf-8ad8-5c2d-b013-e251b532c2d5" :step/math-academy-id 39009 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186163116}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186163116 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162136 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186161702 :question/id #uuid "08ae7ab0-00d9-51db-9c5d-d3b13ddaedac" :question/math-academy-id "e-10358"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "929a4b02-6c3d-59ce-b774-7ca6487077ff" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050368}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162422} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162423}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Covariance Using the Shortcut Formula"} :step/id #uuid "2be6a036-867e-5d15-93fd-01acf1d032ce" :step/math-academy-id 39010 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186163009}}] :activity/title "The Covariance of Two Random Variables" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186162187}] [{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186158103} :activity/id #uuid "3237638b-b8fc-5ada-8f93-3509e05153ed" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050366 :topic/id #uuid "4395903b-598c-5c34-b488-0d6a79550dd7" :topic/math-academy-id 2825} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186158103 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186158470 :tutorial/content "Given an orthogonal basis $\\mathcal{B}=\\{\\mathbf{q}_1, \\mathbf{q}_2, \\ldots, \\mathbf{q}_n\\}$ of $S=\\textrm{Span}\\{\\mathcal{B}\\},$ there is an easy way to find the components of a vector $\\mathbf{x}\\in S$ with respect to this basis. The vector $\\mathbf{x}$ with respect to the basis $\\mathcal{B}$ is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ ⋮ \\\\ {x}_{n}\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nFurthermore, if the basis is *orthonormal*, then we know that $\\mathbf{q}_i\\cdot\\mathbf{q}_i=1$. So, the formula for each component is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ ⋮ \\\\ {x}_{n}\\end{aligned}]\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "67f07988-4aba-56fb-b9e7-aef39a3de89b" :tutorial/math-academy-id 4302 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "4accdfe0-74c3-5830-95d3-ff10c16738c1" :step/math-academy-id 26151 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186158104}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186158104 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157276 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157080 :question/id #uuid "5f10a215-83bb-545a-a0e1-ce702f773e6d" :question/math-academy-id "e-7112"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "15b92bed-c97f-5b87-9811-fbfc5c03314d" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048762} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049811} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050394}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157768} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157771} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186237889} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186237890} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186237891} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186237893} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186326837} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351219} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351220} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351221} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351233} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186373463} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186375425}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Component of a Vector With Respect to a Given Orthogonal Basis"} :step/id #uuid "de4c2603-0fe6-5fca-ad09-e02417514da9" :step/math-academy-id 26152 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186158105}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186158105 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157277 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157081 :question/id #uuid "d7df79a9-2489-5fed-9e00-29890bd01f82" :question/math-academy-id "e-7113"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "39b12711-22c8-5cb7-bd61-e128734e7e6f" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157769} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157770} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186237886} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186237888} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186326838} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351222} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351234} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351235} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186373449} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186373456} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186375418} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186386000} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186392347}] :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Components of a Vector With Respect to a Given Orthonormal Basis"} :step/id #uuid "f247c384-fdd7-5b12-a6d5-f04393f4df78" :step/math-academy-id 26153 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186158106}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186158106 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186158471 :tutorial/content "Given any vector $\\mathbf{x}$ and any subspace $W$ in $\\mathbb{R}^n,$ we can always write $\\mathbf{x}$ as a sum of a vector from $W$ and a vector from $W^{\\perp}.$ This fact is known as the **orthogonal decomposition theorem.** We can state it formally as follows:\n\n> *Let $W$ be a subspace of $\\mathbb{R}^n.$ Then each $\\mathbf{x}\\in\\mathbb{R}^n$ can be written uniquely in the form\n> \n> \n> $$\n> \\mathbf{x}= \\mathbf{y}+\\mathbf{z}\n> $$\n> \n> \n> where $\\mathbf{y} \\in W$ and $\\mathbf{z} \\in W^{\\perp}.$*\n\n> *Moreover, since $\\mathbf y$ is the orthogonal projection of $\\mathbf x$ onto the subspace $W,$ if $\\{\\mathbf{u}_1\\,\\dots,\\mathbf{u}_p\\}$ is any orthogonal basis of $W,$ then*\n> \n> \n> $$\n> \\begin{aligned}y & ={\\text{proj}}_{W}\\,x \\\\  & ={\\text{proj}}_{{u}_{1}}\\,x+{\\text{proj}}_{{u}_{2}}\\,x+⋯+{\\text{proj}}_{{u}_{p}}\\,x \\\\  & =\\frac{x\\cdot {u}_{1}}{{u}_{1}\\cdot {u}_{1}}{u}_{1}+\\frac{x\\cdot {u}_{2}}{{u}_{2}\\cdot {u}_{2}}{u}_{2}+⋯+\\frac{x\\cdot {u}_{p}}{{u}_{p}\\cdot {u}_{p}}{u}_{p}.\\end{aligned}\n> $$" :tutorial/id #uuid "789dc38d-c1e0-5bdc-9f01-5940371a326b" :tutorial/math-academy-id 4304 :tutorial/title "The Orthogonal Decomposition Theorem"} :step/id #uuid "450eea64-01cc-591d-9042-fba21e4feb99" :step/math-academy-id 26173}] :activity/title "The Components of a Vector with Respect to an Orthogonal or Orthonormal Basis" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186157482}] [{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186153290} :activity/id #uuid "d59d69ae-798b-5a20-9d5f-60ce55a98799" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050522 :topic/id #uuid "503f6e39-70fd-54b8-a04b-77e9fcd7b979" :topic/math-academy-id 2740} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186153290 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186153382 :tutorial/content "Consider the second-order linear homogeneous differential equation\n\n$$\ny'' - 5y' + 6y = 0.\n$$\n\nIt can be shown that a fundamental set of solutions is given by\n\n$$\ny_1(x) = e^{3x}, \\qquad y_2(x) = e^{2x}.\n$$\n\nThe superposition principle tells us that any linear combination of solutions to a linear, homogeneous differential equation is also a solution to the equation. Therefore, the **general solution** to this differential equation is\n\n$$\ny(x) = Ae^{3x} + Be^{2x}\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ and $B$ are **arbitrary constants.**\n\nIn general, if an $n$th order homogeneous, linear ODE has a fundamental set of solutions given by \n\n$$\ny_1(x), \\quad y_2(x),\\quad \\ldots, \\quad y_n(x),\n$$\n\nthen the general solution to the equation is \n\n$$\n\\bbox[4pt,border: 1px solid lightgray]{y(x) = C_1y_1(x)+C_2y_2(x)+\\cdots+C_ny_n(x)}\n$$\n\nfor arbitrary constants $C_1, C_2,\\ldots C_n.$\n\n**Note:** Using sigma notation, we can write the general solution more compactly as\n\n$$\ny(x) = \\sum_{i=1}^n C_i y_i(x).\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "d0ae06ba-917f-56b1-8d5d-fac54f464ee8" :tutorial/math-academy-id 4197 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "c268f62a-7fd0-540c-9ff9-832b5389a31e" :step/math-academy-id 601711 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186153291}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186153291 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152291 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186151984 :question/id #uuid "bbf72227-f0da-5dab-88cd-bfed2ed773f7" :question/math-academy-id "e-6792"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "6302f81d-4834-56a5-b014-0a97c3dccd6f" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049486} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050032} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050150}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152635} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152643} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265795} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345315} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348312} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186357466} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186400443}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements About General Solutions of Second-Order Linear ODEs"} :step/id #uuid "f0a0cdaa-70e3-5041-a39e-64dbcbe7a4a1" :step/math-academy-id 601712 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186153292}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186153292 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152292 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186151982 :question/id #uuid "bacb284c-88db-5ad5-bd7d-cbe6fbc604df" :question/math-academy-id "e-6789"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a67ca0bc-c946-5963-a733-2ec9eb6b07ce" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049102}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152640} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152641} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265788} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265790} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265791} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186335797} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348313} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348314} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186357490} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186380076} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186380277}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding General Solutions to Second-Order Homogeneous ODEs Using Reduction of Order"} :step/id #uuid "66cf83ce-b3bc-50bd-88ae-2b9f54096657" :step/math-academy-id 601715 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186153293}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186153293 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152293 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186151985 :question/id #uuid "f41bad20-5a72-5d6d-82c4-cf36f7ac74cd" :question/math-academy-id "e-6794"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9ef8a6ef-3e77-5e3e-ac75-c3dece0af96e" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152636} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152642} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265797} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265798} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265800} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265806} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186335798} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348315} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348316} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186357476}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding General Solutions to Second-Order Homogeneous ODEs With Variable Coefficients"} :step/id #uuid "38e288bc-7c3f-536d-a984-63e199038d6a" :step/math-academy-id 601718 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186153294}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186153294 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186153335 :tutorial/content "Similar to first-order linear ODEs, for inhomogeneous differential equations, the **general solution** is given by\n\n$$\ny(x) = y_c(x) + y_p(x),\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n- $y_c(x)$ is the **complementary solution** (the general solution of the corresponding homogeneous equation), and\n\n- $y_p(x)$ is a **particular solution** of the original inhomogeneous equation.\n\nWe will apply this structure to a specific differential equation in the next slide." :tutorial/id #uuid "d323d4d9-40c7-5a00-aafe-a0c1ba46fd4c" :tutorial/math-academy-id 14780 :tutorial/title "General Solutions of Inhomogeneous ODEs"} :step/id #uuid "4499c456-0b30-59ea-92cd-0a6e8cc58d01" :step/math-academy-id 601721 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186153295}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186153295 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152294 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186151912 :question/id #uuid "58f2bf06-7449-5a66-9662-532ce70a8ff9" :question/math-academy-id "e-21879"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "7640be27-8277-5a9c-bf14-83bcc7a38604" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049370}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152577} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152578} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186314878} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186314879} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186314880} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186379528} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186379548} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186397359} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186397379}] :knowledge-point/title "Constructing General Solutions of Inhomogeneous ODEs"} :step/id #uuid "2779fb81-53c1-5556-969d-409a83b9a273" :step/math-academy-id 601722}] :activity/title "General Solutions of Linear ODEs" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186152359}] [{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186163007} :activity/id #uuid "fa26629c-b4dd-57db-9661-3b43692ea236" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050535 :topic/id #uuid "51d67380-dd70-5f91-a192-de8ab2b56b57" :topic/math-academy-id 3022} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186163007 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186163291 :tutorial/content "We can easily extend the notion of conditional probability distributions for discrete random variables to continuous random variables.\n\nFor continuous random variables $X$ and $Y$, the **conditional probability density function of $X$ given $Y = y$** is given by\n\n$$\nf_{X \\mid Y} (x \\mid y) =\\dfrac{f(x, y)}{f_Y (y)}, \\qquad f_Y(y) \\neq 0\n$$\n\nand the conditional probability density function of $Y$ given $X = x$ is given by\n\n$$\nf_{Y \\mid X} (y \\mid x) =\\dfrac{f(x, y)}{f_X (x)}, \\qquad f_X(x) \\neq 0\n$$\n\nNotice that these definitions are analogous to those for discrete random variables.\n\nTo compute probabilities, we work directly with these conditional PDFs. For example, to calculate the **conditional probability of $X$ given $Y$,** we use\n\n$$\nP(X \\in A \\mid Y = y) =\\int_A f_{X \\mid Y} (x \\mid y)\\, \\textrm dx\n$$\n\nIn general, the conditional distribution of $X$ given $Y$ does not equal the conditional distribution of $Y$ given $X$. That is\n\n$$\nf_{X\\mid Y}(x \\mid y) \\ne f_{Y\\mid X}(y \\mid x)\n$$\n\nFinally, if $X$ and $Y$ are independent, then\n\n$$\nf_{X\\mid Y}(x \\mid y)=f_X(x),\\qquad f_{Y\\mid X}(y \\mid x)=f_Y(y)\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "4e66c559-ad43-5152-a04e-7aa8b01374ef" :tutorial/math-academy-id 5625 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "cc469a65-06a8-5cb9-9eed-1c3d9f00c4c2" :step/math-academy-id 34590 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186163078}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186163078 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162112 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186161678 :question/id #uuid "29e739b1-8745-517f-9011-d1761364c4c3" :question/math-academy-id "e-10099"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "444ad045-3c7b-5668-bc3a-e3cb49a5b6f2" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049149} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049553}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162389} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162393}] :knowledge-point/title "Working With the Definition of a Conditional PDF"} :step/id #uuid "5d645e06-ba3d-5245-bff3-396e4a4a04e4" :step/math-academy-id 38091 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186163080}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186163079 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162113 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186161679 :question/id #uuid "3439786b-905b-5642-89d1-61b0345f79e6" :question/math-academy-id "e-10100"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "184d9e09-3335-5617-8989-e5ab08ca04e7" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162391} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162392} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186411871} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186413415}] :knowledge-point/title "Computing a Conditional Probability"} :step/id #uuid "f08e3c7b-ff47-5108-9d4d-3471aa625605" :step/math-academy-id 38092} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186163080 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162114 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186161680 :question/id #uuid "f1c9e136-57b3-59ae-90e0-b28921be44ee" :question/math-academy-id "e-10101"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "eb3b4b6a-3a24-5079-bf33-ed1c5576ffa7" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162390} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162396}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Conditional PDF"} :step/id #uuid "ccc00629-902d-501f-86c2-7072c1aad6a6" :step/math-academy-id 38093 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186163079}}] :activity/title "Conditional Distributions for Continuous Random Variables" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186162176}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046660} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186413418} :activity/id #uuid "8d71dbb8-3f6b-51a3-b4b2-a5b2c8b23a9b" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050535 :topic/id #uuid "51d67380-dd70-5f91-a192-de8ab2b56b57" :topic/math-academy-id 3022} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186413416 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186413415 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0232ff5b-0d05-5778-88a0-ff0af66fc111" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{15}{16}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413397} {:answer/id #uuid "0d7ebaa8-7f99-5427-8a2f-237d9f6e4410" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{27}{64}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413398} {:answer/id #uuid "291ffaa1-af34-572c-8208-632d599387b4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{8}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413399} {:answer/id #uuid "2bab8694-81fe-5f61-ab63-c2f3224466f2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413401} {:answer/id #uuid "cb999cce-827d-57cd-aed2-f09c922819e1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{16}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413407}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186413398} :answer-field/id #uuid "76914fbd-7e46-5e1e-a2ea-82b62a7c5f3c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413414}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "6fd86b7a-aa64-5ea3-a90b-7af5da2353f8" :question/math-academy-id "q-143574" :question/problem "Let $X$ and $Y$ be two continuous random variables with the following joint probability density function:\n\n$$\nf(x,y)={\\begin{aligned}8xy, & \\,0\\le x\\le y,\\,0\\le y\\le 1 \\\\ 0, & \\,\\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nFind $P(\\frac{1}{4}<X<\\frac{1}{2}\\,|\\,Y=\\frac{2}{3}).$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that \n\n$$\nP(Y\\in A\\,|\\,X=x)={\\int }_{A}{f}_{X\\,|\\,Y}(x\\,|\\,y)\\,\\text{d}y,\n$$\n\nwhere ${f}_{X\\,|\\,Y}(x\\,|\\,y)$ is the conditional probability density function of $X$ given $Y=y.$ \n\nUsing the formula for the marginal density function for $Y,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{Y}(y) & ={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{y}8xy\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =8y{\\int }_{0}^{y}x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =8y{[\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}]}_{0}^{y} \\\\  & =4y({y}^{2}-0) \\\\  & =4{y}^{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the full expression for ${f}_{Y}(y)$ is the following:\n\n$$\n{f}_{Y}(y)={\\begin{aligned}4{y}^{3}, & \\,0\\le y\\le 1 \\\\ 0, & \\,\\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow recall that the conditional probability density function of $X$ given $Y=y$ for two continuous random variables $X$ and $Y$ is given by\n\n$$\n{f}_{X\\,|\\,Y}(x\\,|\\,y)=\\frac{f(x,y)}{{f}_{Y}(y)}.\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n- $f(x,y)$ is the joint probability density function for $X$ and $Y,$ and\n\n- ${f}_{Y}(y)$ is the marginal density function for $Y.$\n\nNow, for $0\\le x\\le 1,0\\le y\\le x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{X\\,|\\,Y}(x\\,|\\,y) & =\\frac{f(x,y)}{{f}_{Y}(y)} \\\\  & =\\frac{8xy}{4{y}^{3}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2x}{{y}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor other values of $x$ and $y,$ the function $f(x,y)$ equals $0.$ As a result, ${f}_{X\\,|\\,Y}(x\\,|\\,y)$ will be equal to $0$ too.\n\nTherefore, the conditional probability density function is the following:\n\n$$\n{f}_{X\\,|\\,Y}(x\\,|\\,y)={\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2x}{{y}^{2}}, & \\,0\\le x\\le y,\\,0\\le y\\le 1 \\\\ 0, & \\,\\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we compute our probability:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(\\frac{1}{4}<X<\\frac{1}{2}\\,|\\,Y=\\frac{2}{3}) & ={\\int }_{1/4}^{1/2}{f}_{X\\,|\\,Y}(x\\,|\\,y=\\frac{2}{3})\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1/4}^{1/2}\\frac{2x}{{(\\frac{2}{3})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{4}{\\int }_{1/4}^{1/2}2x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{4}{[{x}^{2}]}_{1/4}^{1/2} \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{4}(\\frac{1}{4}-\\frac{1}{16}) \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{4}(\\frac{3}{16}) \\\\  & =\\frac{27}{64}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "9476e3d9-7d05-54ea-ba2e-44132f1512b5" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186413417}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186413417 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162396 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2b4b973f-cb9d-5e7e-8beb-4c556f2b875c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{2x+3y}{3(y+1)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413400} {:answer/id #uuid "6a95ee3c-3035-5909-a68e-b2eac9893340" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "5y(x+y)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413402} {:answer/id #uuid "726964d4-b990-596e-9009-e83d6d208a25" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{(2x+3y)(y+1)}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413403} {:answer/id #uuid "b7e4876d-bca4-5991-b345-bf1e1f8f35e9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{2(y+1)}{5(2x+3y)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413406} {:answer/id #uuid "e77674d9-5e17-517e-8f5b-956421030e91" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2x+3y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413409}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186413400} :answer-field/id #uuid "2b4844fd-48c8-5187-86c9-fcbf91d0bc32" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413412}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "228badef-897c-5f4a-80d9-699c2c689782" :question/math-academy-id "q-143634" :question/problem "Let $X$ and $Y$ be two continuous random variables with the following joint probability density function:\n\n$$\nf(x,y)={\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2y(2x+3y)}{5}, & \\,1\\le x\\le 2,\\,0\\le y\\le 1 \\\\ 0, & \\,\\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFind the expression of the conditional probability density function ${f}_{X\\,|\\,Y}(x\\,|\\,y)$ for $1\\le x\\le 2,\\,0\\le y\\le 1.$" :question/worked-solution "Using the formula for the marginal density function for $Y,$ we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{Y}(y) & ={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}\\frac{2y(2x+3y)}{5}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}(\\frac{4xy}{5}+\\frac{6{y}^{2}}{5})\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[\\frac{2{x}^{2}y}{5}+\\frac{6x{y}^{2}}{5}]}_{x=1}^{x=2} \\\\  & =\\frac{8y}{5}+\\frac{12{y}^{2}}{5}-\\frac{2y}{5}-\\frac{6{y}^{2}}{5} \\\\  & =\\frac{6{y}^{2}}{5}+\\frac{6y}{5} \\\\  & =\\frac{6y(y+1)}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the full expression for ${f}_{Y}(y)$ is the following:\n\n$$\n{f}_{Y}(y)={\\begin{aligned}\\frac{6y(y+1)}{5}, & \\,0\\le y\\le 1 \\\\ 0, & \\,\\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow recall that the conditional probability density function of $X$ given $Y=y$ for two continuous random variables $X$ and $Y$ is given by\n\n$$\n{f}_{X\\,|\\,Y}(x\\,|\\,y)=\\frac{f(x,y)}{{f}_{Y}(y)},\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n- $f(x,y)$ is the joint probability density function for $X$ and $Y,$ and\n\n- ${f}_{Y}(y)$ is the marginal density function for $Y.$\n\nNow, for $1\\le x\\le 2,\\,0\\le y\\le 1,$ we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{X\\,|\\,Y}(x\\,|\\,y) & =\\frac{f(x,y)}{{f}_{Y}(y)} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\frac{2y(2x+3y)}{5})}{(\\frac{6y(y+1)}{5})} \\\\  & =\\frac{2x+3y}{3(y+1)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor other values of $x$ and $y,$ the function $f(x,y)$ equals $0.$ As a result, ${f}_{X\\,|\\,Y}(x\\,|\\,y)$ will be equal to $0$ too.\n\nTherefore, the conditional probability density function is the following:\n\n$$\n{f}_{X\\,|\\,Y}(x\\,|\\,y)={\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2x+3y}{3(y+1)}, & \\,1\\le x\\le 2,\\,0\\le y\\le 1 \\\\ 0, & \\,\\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "a4e2ce8c-ef99-5939-91ca-5637d283ed7e"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186413418 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162392 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "76dedc47-c082-5f11-bb91-f5c53a5feef0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413404} {:answer/id #uuid "b6e703b0-4bd0-5a40-af83-db0c989a5aba" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{12}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413405} {:answer/id #uuid "db303bfa-69cf-5cf1-86a5-b4a2624334a1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413408} {:answer/id #uuid "efd7c23f-d7a0-5417-baaf-c56cd1849f93" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413410} {:answer/id #uuid "fcf61311-9fef-553a-a566-6bcfee2063e1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413411}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186413404} :answer-field/id #uuid "7133b6f3-994d-586b-8bc7-52facdb8edd3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413413}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "990850d6-75f9-5e72-98e4-4c732056938a" :question/math-academy-id "q-143461" :question/problem "Let $X$ and $Y$ be two continuous random variables with the following joint probability density function:\n\n$$\nf(x,y)={\\begin{aligned}24xy, & \\,0<x<1,0<y<1-x \\\\ 0, & \\,\\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nFind $P(Y<X\\,|\\,X=\\frac{1}{3}).$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that \n\n$$\nP(Y\\in A\\,|\\,X=x)={\\int }_{A}{f}_{Y\\,|\\,X}(y\\,|\\,x)\\,\\text{d}y,\n$$\n\nwhere ${f}_{Y\\,|\\,X}(y\\,|\\,x)$ is the conditional probability density function of $Y$ given $X=x.$ \n\nUsing the formula for the marginal density function for $X,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{X}(x) & ={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1-x}24xy\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =24x{\\int }_{0}^{1-x}y\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =24x{[\\frac{1}{2}{y}^{2}]}_{0}^{1-x} \\\\  & =12x(1-x)^{2}-0 \\\\  & =12x(x-1)^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the full expression for ${f}_{X}(x)$ is the following:\n\n$$\n{f}_{X}(x)={\\begin{aligned}12x(x-1)^{2}, & \\,0<x<1 \\\\ 0, & \\,\\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow recall that the conditional probability density function of $Y$ given $X=x$ for two continuous random variables $X$ and $Y$ is given by\n\n$$\n{f}_{Y\\,|\\,X}(y\\,|\\,x)=\\frac{f(x,y)}{{f}_{X}(x)}.\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n- $f(x,y)$ is the joint probability density function for $X$ and $Y,$ and\n\n- ${f}_{X}(x)$ is the marginal density function for $X.$\n\nNow, for $0<x<1,0<y<1-x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{Y\\,|\\,X}(y\\,|\\,x) & =\\frac{f(x,y)}{{f}_{X}(x)} \\\\  & =\\frac{24xy}{12x(x-1)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2y}{(x-1)^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor other values of $x$ and $y,$ the function $f(x,y)$ equals $0.$ As a result, ${f}_{Y\\,|\\,X}(y\\,|\\,x)$ will be equal to $0$ too.\n\nTherefore, the conditional probability density function is the following:\n\n$$\n{f}_{Y\\,|\\,X}(y\\,|\\,x)={\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2y}{(x-1)^{2}}, & \\,0<x<1,0<y<1-x \\\\ 0, & \\,\\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we compute our probability:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(Y<X\\,|\\,X=\\frac{1}{3}) & =P(Y<\\frac{1}{3}\\,|\\,X=\\frac{1}{3}) \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1/3}{f}_{Y\\,|\\,X}(y\\,|\\,x=\\frac{1}{3})\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1/3}\\frac{2y}{{(\\frac{1}{3}-1)}^{2}}\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1/3}y\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{2}{[\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2}]}_{0}^{1/3} \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{2}(\\frac{1}{18}-0) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "cd604f93-e377-5bb5-97aa-db0cfd4f422a" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186413416}}] :activity/title "Conditional Distributions for Continuous Random Variables" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413396}] [{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186158151} :activity/id #uuid "a2778177-99cd-5d9a-9c00-3f85c8361e9b" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050775 :topic/id #uuid "65bfe855-0363-58b6-a91d-f3f12be3a932" :topic/math-academy-id 2836} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186158145 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157301 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157105 :question/id #uuid "77354dd3-3f68-5d8e-b9db-3ef2dd0d1e5a" :question/math-academy-id "e-7417"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "697f82e2-e564-522b-a46b-08313bf1aaf1" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048317}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157835} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157836} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186366889} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186368824} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186368831}] :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Expected Value of a Constant"} :step/id #uuid "2d9fc28e-a025-50d3-aadd-74b750d2c00f" :step/math-academy-id 27503 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186158152}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186158146 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157302 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157106 :question/id #uuid "570b8071-d445-5782-b559-4fc220da50d1" :question/math-academy-id "e-7418"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "866ac63c-b20a-55fd-a1b5-29400355f195" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157549} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157841} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186366896} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186366903} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186366910} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186368838} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186368845} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186382867} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186382874} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186385817}] :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Expected Value of a Scaled Random Variable"} :step/id #uuid "5d6c4425-e2ac-54f8-89eb-4c5834d4d3ec" :step/math-academy-id 27504 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186158272}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186158151 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186158488 :tutorial/content "There are several important properties that we can use to simplify expressions involving expected values.\n\nThe first property is that the expected value of a constant is just the constant itself. That is, for any constant $a,$ we have $$\\textrm E[a] = a.$$\n\nFor example,\n\n$$\\textrm E[5] = 5,\\qquad \\textrm E[-99] = -99,\\qquad \\textrm E[\\sqrt 2] = \\sqrt 2.$$\n\nThe idea that the expected value of a constant is equal to the same constant makes intuitive sense. If $a$ is a constant, then it does not vary. Therefore, it is always sure to have the same value." :tutorial/id #uuid "4b67be84-b702-5707-9433-0dbd49d9264b" :tutorial/math-academy-id 4497 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "8243818e-fa41-5d84-a795-11bc994eb0ce" :step/math-academy-id 27700 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186158145}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186158152 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186158489 :tutorial/content "We can also factor constants outside of an expected value expression. So for any constant $a$ and any random variable $X,$ we have\n\n$$\\textrm E[aX] = a \\textrm E[X].$$\n\nFor example,\n\n$$\\textrm E[5X] = 5 \\textrm E[X],\\qquad\\textrm E[-9X] = -9 \\textrm E[X], \\qquad \\textrm E[\\pi X] = \\pi \\textrm E[X].$$\n\nWe can prove this rule using the definition of the expected value, as follows:\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[aX] & ={\\sum }_{x\\in S}ax\\cdot f(x) \\\\ & =a{\\sum }_{x\\in S}x\\cdot f(x) \\\\ & =a\\text{E}[X]\\end{aligned}$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "888aac3a-696d-5c89-8091-757e3f8db86b" :tutorial/math-academy-id 4506 :tutorial/title "The Expected Value of a Scaled Random Variable"} :step/id #uuid "b1582c01-ddd7-525e-a721-192543f80a68" :step/math-academy-id 27747 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186158146}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186158272 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186158546 :tutorial/content "Finally, we can combine the factor and constant rules into one simple rule.\n\nSuppose that $X$ is a random variable and $a$ and $b$ are constants. By combining our two rules, we have\n\n$$\\textrm E[aX + b] = a\\textrm E[X] + b.$$\n\nFor example, if we know that $\\textrm{E}[X] = 4,$ then\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[3X+2] & =\\text{E}[3X+2] \\\\ & =3\\text{E}[X]+2 \\\\ & =3(4)+2 \\\\ & =12+2 \\\\ & =14.\\end{aligned}$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "f861e35c-2827-5655-9b71-c93c4741794b" :tutorial/math-academy-id 5452 :tutorial/title "The Expected Value of a Transformed Random Variable"} :step/id #uuid "5d5b5303-4eae-5908-bc31-6eae5cf5c87e" :step/math-academy-id 33938 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186158273}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186158273 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157384 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157204 :question/id #uuid "a92e44a1-5eaf-5dab-b1aa-83ba4e58cf3a" :question/math-academy-id "e-9130"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "5449da36-9aae-591d-bab6-7ed55d7d9d4b" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157546} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157837} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186366917} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186366924} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186366931} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186366938} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186382881} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186382888} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186411851}] :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Expected Value of a Transformed Random Variable"} :step/id #uuid "f16d6f2c-1c33-51ea-9e4e-3ffdc289dae2" :step/math-academy-id 34021}] :activity/title "Properties of Expectation for Discrete Random Variables" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186157491}] [{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134817} :activity/id #uuid "3a450e6c-560e-5c98-a021-778f5de2c088" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050841 :topic/id #uuid "6aced1c3-bd0e-5b1d-973e-105b955f9e1a" :topic/math-academy-id 2126} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134817 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186135105 :tutorial/content "Let $V$ be an inner product space with the inner product $\\langle\\cdot, \\cdot\\rangle.$ If \n\n$$\n\\big\\{ \\mathbf{a}_1, \\mathbf{a}_2 , \\ldots, \\mathbf{a}_n \\big\\}\n$$\n\n is a set of mutually orthogonal vectors in $V,$  then the **orthogonal projection** of a vector $\\mathbf{x}\\in V$ onto the subspace \n\n$$\nS = \\textrm{Span}\\{ \\mathbf{a}_1, \\mathbf{a}_2 , \\ldots, \\mathbf{a}_n \\}\n$$\n\nwith respect to the inner product is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x & ={\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{1}}x+{\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{2}}x+⋯+{\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{n}}x \\\\  & =\\frac{⟨x,{a}_{1}⟩}{⟨{a}_{1},{a}_{1}⟩}{a}_{1}+\\frac{⟨x,{a}_{2}⟩}{⟨{a}_{2},{a}_{2}⟩}{a}_{2}+⋯+\\frac{⟨x,{a}_{n}⟩}{⟨{a}_{n},{a}_{n}⟩}{a}_{n}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn other words, the orthogonal projection of $\\mathbf{x}$ onto $S$ is the sum of the orthogonal projections of $\\mathbf{x}$ onto each one-dimensional subspace $\\textrm{Span}\\big\\{ \\mathbf{a}_i\\}$ for $i=1, 2, \\cdots, n.$ \n\nNote the following:\n\n- This is entirely analogous to the corresponding formula for Euclidean spaces.\n\n- As in Euclidean spaces, the formula works *only* when the set of vectors $\\{ \\mathbf{a}_1, \\mathbf{a}_2 , \\ldots, \\mathbf{a}_n \\}$ is orthogonal with respect to the inner product!" :tutorial/id #uuid "9b462101-527b-5e26-bcd4-89b8459552bd" :tutorial/math-academy-id 5162 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "91e83dab-899c-5474-8059-ebdcc7a5278a" :step/math-academy-id 32457 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134885}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134818 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133873 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133689 :question/id #uuid "18068394-b64b-5bc8-a2da-b0db4085ecba" :question/math-academy-id "e-8729"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "df300955-830a-5347-9aeb-2b2b25f865c8" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048169} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048893}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134060} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134563} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186240902} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186240903} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186240904} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186240948} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186240949} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186240961} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186240962}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Orthogonal Projection of a Vector Onto a One-Dimensional Subspace"} :step/id #uuid "4240c631-050e-58b5-968f-46d616a5f794" :step/math-academy-id 32458 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134819}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134819 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133874 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133690 :question/id #uuid "4e4a5db0-2ea0-542e-abdd-c5b3abfe2134" :question/math-academy-id "e-8730"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "39692b40-7d68-5ae5-9fe1-0f8b0b2e0d77" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048495} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050009}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134064} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134562} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186240950} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186240955} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186240978} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186240983} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186240989} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186240996} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186241003} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186241014} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186241046} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186241049}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Orthogonal Projection of a Vector Onto a Subspace Spanned by Orthogonal Vectors"} :step/id #uuid "e60ded4d-fa5d-5393-8f16-7c1cc6abe2dd" :step/math-academy-id 32459 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134820}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134820 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186135106 :tutorial/content "Suppose that $V$ is a vector space equipped with an inner product $\\left\\langle\\cdot, \\cdot\\right\\rangle$ and that $\\mathbf{v}, \\mathbf{w} \\in V.$ The **distance $d$ between $\\mathbf v$ and $\\mathbf w$ with respect to the inner product** is defined by the *norm* of $\\mathbf{v}- \\mathbf{w}.$ That is,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & =‖v-w‖=\\sqrt{⟨v-w,v-w⟩}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor example, suppose that $V = \\mathcal{C}[0,1],$ the vector space of continuous functions over $[0,1],$ equipped with the inner product\n\n$$\n\\langle x(t),y(t) \\rangle = \\int_0^{1} x(t) y(t) \\, \\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nThe distance between the functions $f(t)=4t$ and $g(t)=t+1$ with respect to the given inner product is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & =‖f(t)-g(t)‖ \\\\  & =‖4t-(t+1)‖ \\\\  & =‖3t-1‖ \\\\  & =\\sqrt{⟨3t-1,3t-1⟩} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{\\int }_{0}^{1}(3t-1)^{2}\\,\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{\\frac{1}{9}\\cdot (3t-1)^{3}|}_{0}^{1}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{1}{9}\\cdot [(8)-(-1)]} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{1}{9}\\cdot 9} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{1} \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMore generally, given an orthogonal set \n\n$$\n\\big\\{ \\mathbf{a}_1, \\mathbf{a}_2 , \\ldots, \\mathbf{a}_n \\big\\} \\subset  V\n$$\n\nand a vector $\\mathbf x \\in V,$  the **distance between $\\mathbf{x}$ and the subspace** \n\n$$\nS=\\textrm{Span}\\{ \\mathbf{a}_1,\\mathbf{a}_2,  \\ldots, \\mathbf{a}_n \\}\n$$\n\nis defined as\n\n$$\n\\Vert{ \\color{black} \\mathbf{x} - \\text{proj}_{S}\\,\\mathbf{x} }\\Vert.\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "4ca26c36-a2e4-51d5-ae7d-a4b59234cedb" :tutorial/math-academy-id 5163 :tutorial/title "The Distance Between a Vector and a Subspace"} :step/id #uuid "7d1a7540-bb8b-5865-a0e0-852beed15e26" :step/math-academy-id 32460 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134821}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134821 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133875 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133691 :question/id #uuid "d7541321-4042-54af-9b64-59f255a6f1a3" :question/math-academy-id "e-8733"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "25e84fac-02c3-5111-98b3-bc67fe1ec9ab" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049274}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134564} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134565} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186241059} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186241063} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186241065} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186241066} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186241068} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186241070} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186241072} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186241074} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186375652}] :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Distance Between a Vector and a Subspace"} :step/id #uuid "20a627a4-468b-5c73-a4aa-e3ab9c87207e" :step/math-academy-id 32485 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134828}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134828 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186135107 :tutorial/content "The orthogonal projection of a vector $\\mathbf{x}\\in V$ onto $\\textrm{Span}\\{\\mathbf{a}\\}$ (or simply onto a vector $\\mathbf{a}$) is the vector $\\text{proj}_{\\mathbf{a}}\\,\\mathbf{x}\\in \\textrm{Span}\\{\\mathbf{a}\\}$ that is \"closest\" to $\\mathbf{x}$ with respect to the distance defined by the inner product.\n\nAlthough we've considered abstract vector spaces with any possible inner product in this lesson, we can still visualize this situation with the following diagram:\n\n![](images/09/099a0bb20552824d2376994c3eb7145e48bbf78552d16de39fec44bda6b766f2.png)\n\nAs shown in the diagram, the projection of $\\mathbf{x}$ onto $\\textrm{Span}\\{\\mathbf{a}\\}$ is the vector $\\text{proj}_{\\mathbf{a}}\\,\\mathbf{x}$ such that $\\mathbf{x} - \\text{proj}_{\\mathbf{a}}\\,\\mathbf{x}$ is orthogonal to $\\textrm{Span}\\{\\mathbf{a}\\}.$\n\nFurthermore, $\\| \\mathbf{x} - \\text{proj}_{\\mathbf{a}}\\,\\mathbf{x} \\|$ will be the smallest possible distance (with respect to our inner product) from $\\mathbf{x}$ to any vector in $\\textrm{Span}\\{\\mathbf{a}\\}.$" :tutorial/id #uuid "629bc20f-76c3-5217-b763-e4ab0c831a8d" :tutorial/math-academy-id 5343 :tutorial/title "The Intuition Behind Orthogonal Projections in Inner Product Spaces"} :step/id #uuid "97501e93-7a73-5f78-8d81-1f3d7a340746" :step/math-academy-id 33420} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134885 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186135141 :tutorial/content "Consider the functions \n\n$$\nf_1(t)=t,\\qquad f_2(t)=t^2+1, \\qquad h(t)= 5t^3,\n$$\n\nin the vector space $V = \\mathcal{C}[-1,1]$ of continuous functions over $[-1,1],$ where the inner product is defined as\n\n$$\n\\langle x(t),y(t) \\rangle = \\int_{-1}^1 x(t) y(t) \\, \\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nLet's find the orthogonal projection of $h(t)$ onto the subspace\n\n$$\nS= \\textrm{Span}\\{f_1(t), f_2(t)\\},\n$$\n\n \n\ngiven that $f_1(t) \\perp f_2(t)$ with respect to this inner product. \n\nSince the set $\\{f_1(t),f_2(t)\\}$ is orthogonal, the orthogonal projection of $h(t)$ onto $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\textrm{proj}_{S}\\,h(t) \n= \\textrm{proj}_{f_1(t)}\\,h(t) + \\textrm{proj}_{f_2(t)}\\,h(t).\n$$\n\nLet's now compute these projections:\n\n- First, we compute  the orthogonal projection of $h(t)$ onto $\\textrm{Span}\\{f_1(t)\\}\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{{f}_{1}(t)}\\,h(t) & =\\frac{⟨h(t),{f}_{1}(t)⟩}{⟨{f}_{1}(t),{f}_{1}(t)⟩}\\,{f}_{1}(t) \\\\  & =\\frac{{\\int }_{-1}^{1}5{t}^{4}\\,\\text{d}t}{{\\int }_{-1}^{1}{t}^{2}\\,\\text{d}t}\\cdot t \\\\  & =3t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Next, we compute  the orthogonal projection of $h(t)$ onto $\\textrm{Span}\\{f_2(t)\\}\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{{f}_{2}(t)}\\,h(t) & =\\frac{⟨h(t),{f}_{2}(t)⟩}{⟨{f}_{2}(t),{f}_{2}(t)⟩}\\,{f}_{2}(t) \\\\  & =\\frac{{\\int }_{-1}^{1}5{t}^{3}({t}^{2}+1)\\,\\text{d}t}{{\\int }_{-1}^{1}({t}^{2}+1)^{2}\\,\\text{d}t}\\cdot ({t}^{2}+1) \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote: We used the fact that the integrand in the numerator is an odd function, so its integral over $[-1,1]$ must be zero.\n\nFinally, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,h(t) & ={\\text{proj}}_{{f}_{1}(t)}\\,h(t)+{\\text{proj}}_{{f}_{2}(t)}\\,h(t) \\\\  & =3t+0 \\\\  & =3t.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "975c7894-a67f-5528-875e-2e55a94d99b9" :tutorial/math-academy-id 6130 :tutorial/title "A Worked Example"} :step/id #uuid "b89a94ac-0dd4-505c-b266-41783d527047" :step/math-academy-id 37245 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134818}}] :activity/title "Projecting Vectors Onto Subspaces in Inner Product Spaces" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186134005}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046660} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186413797} :activity/id #uuid "fba5b1da-9880-515c-af94-05681b35cd3b" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050841 :topic/id #uuid "6aced1c3-bd0e-5b1d-973e-105b955f9e1a" :topic/math-academy-id 2126} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186413793 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134064 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "00a97740-bf4e-5245-a223-1105ad76868c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186238086} {:answer/id #uuid "0ccb496a-1f3e-5f9c-9be5-7756bb97a385" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186238169} {:answer/id #uuid "9dbfd16e-b04d-5762-91cf-d69f6af7923d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239432} {:answer/id #uuid "a5578604-9539-5565-8bbb-579ff2d23c8a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239490} {:answer/id #uuid "ff74f82e-113d-5ba7-9d63-962e019ecfd4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186240320}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186238086} :answer-field/id #uuid "50e2f23b-8197-5ffd-805f-f3ecff761a73" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186240475}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "e7a8c44b-b300-5e66-9428-e03f400ebf5f" :question/math-academy-id "q-105416" :question/problem "Let $V=C[-\\pi ,\\pi ],$ the vector space of continuous functions over $[-\\pi ,\\pi ]$, and let the inner product on $V$ be defined as\n\n$$\n⟨x(t),y(t)⟩={\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }x(t)y(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nNow consider the following elements of $V:$\n\n$$\n{f}_{1}(t)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,{f}_{2}(t)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,g(t)={sin}^{2}⁡t-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\n$$\n\nFind the orthogonal projection of $g(t)$ onto $S=\\text{Span}{{f}_{1}(t),{f}_{2}(t)}$ given that ${f}_{1}(t)⊥{f}_{2}(t)$ with respect to the inner product, and that the orthogonal projection of $g(t)$ onto $\\text{Span}{{f}_{2}(t)}$ equals $-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t.$" :question/worked-solution "Since the set ${{f}_{1}(t),{f}_{2}(t)}$ is orthogonal, the orthogonal projection of $g(t)$ onto the subspace $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,g(t)={\\text{proj}}_{{f}_{1}(t)}\\,g(t)+{\\text{proj}}_{{f}_{2}(t)}\\,g(t).\n$$\n\nWe are given that ${\\text{proj}}_{{f}_{2}(t)}\\,g(t)=-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,$ so let's now find the orthogonal projection of $g(t)$ onto $\\text{Span}{{f}_{1}(t)}.$ This can be computed as\n\n$$\n{\\text{proj}}_{{f}_{1}(t)}\\,g(t)=\\frac{⟨g(t),{f}_{1}(t)⟩}{⟨{f}_{1}(t),{f}_{1}(t)⟩}\\,{f}_{1}(t).\n$$\n\n \n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨g(t),{f}_{1}(t)⟩ & ={\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }g(t){f}_{1}(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }({sin}^{2}⁡t-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡t({sin}^{2}⁡t-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)\\,\\text{d}t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that $\\operatorname{sin}⁡t$ is an odd function, while ${sin}^{2}⁡t-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t$ is even. Hence, their product $\\operatorname{sin}⁡t({sin}^{2}⁡t-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)$ must be an odd function. Now, since we have an integral over the interval $[-\\pi ,\\pi ],$ which is symmetric about the origin, we can conclude that\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡t({sin}^{2}⁡t-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)\\,\\text{d}t=0.\n$$\n\n  \n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n{\\text{proj}}_{{f}_{1}(t)}\\,g(t)=\\frac{0}{⟨{f}_{1}(t),{f}_{1}(t)⟩}\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡t=0.\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,g(t) & ={\\text{proj}}_{{f}_{1}(t)}\\,g(t)+{\\text{proj}}_{{f}_{2}(t)}\\,g(t) \\\\  & =0-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "0a412cdd-29f8-5273-ab55-2a8dce391dae" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186413795}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186413794 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186241003 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1a9db971-884b-5cf9-9a84-c91c9fa4fa01" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "8+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186238276} {:answer/id #uuid "39658250-b0fe-59bb-a334-a4e517b0d75e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186238550} {:answer/id #uuid "880b7d31-ec04-56db-ad2a-f754621f1b8d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239237} {:answer/id #uuid "b1b15087-e383-5255-8efc-84ad43c168b5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "6-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239607} {:answer/id #uuid "cd180c30-2f84-5a24-af62-8965f36db06d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239851}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186239607} :answer-field/id #uuid "51c982ce-3e72-5b74-bcb0-6eb543685681" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186240480}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "2bf55fb2-ca86-5707-a52a-d891899878f4" :question/math-academy-id "q-105186" :question/problem "Let $V=C[-\\pi ,\\pi ],$ the vector space of continuous functions over $[-\\pi ,\\pi ]$, and let the inner product on $V$ be defined as\n\n$$\n⟨x(t),y(t)⟩={\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }x(t)y(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nNow consider the following elements of $V:$\n\n$$\n{f}_{1}(t)=2,\\,{f}_{2}(t)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,g(t)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+6\n$$\n\nFind the orthogonal projection of $g(t)$ onto $S=\\text{Span}{{f}_{1}(t),{f}_{2}(t)}$ given that ${f}_{1}(t)⊥{f}_{2}(t)$ with respect to the inner product, and that the orthogonal projection of $g(t)$ onto $\\text{Span}{{f}_{1}(t)}$ equals $6.$" :question/worked-solution "Since the set ${{f}_{1}(t),{f}_{2}(t)}$ is orthogonal, the orthogonal projection of $g(t)$ onto the subspace $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,g(t)={\\text{proj}}_{{f}_{1}(t)}\\,g(t)+{\\text{proj}}_{{f}_{2}(t)}\\,g(t).\n$$\n\nWe are given that ${\\text{proj}}_{{f}_{1}(t)}\\,g(t)=6,$ so let's now find the orthogonal projection of $g(t)$ onto $\\text{Span}{{f}_{2}(t)}.$ This can be computed as\n\n$$\n{\\text{proj}}_{{f}_{2}(t)}\\,g(t)=\\frac{⟨g(t),{f}_{2}(t)⟩}{⟨{f}_{2}(t),{f}_{2}(t)⟩}\\,{f}_{2}(t).\n$$\n\n \n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨g(t),{f}_{2}(t)⟩ & ={\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }g(t){f}_{2}(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+6)\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t-2{\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }{sin}^{2}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t+6{\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that $\\operatorname{sin}⁡t$ is an odd function, while $\\operatorname{cos}⁡t$ is even. Hence, their product $\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t$ must be an odd function. Now, since we have integrals over the interval $[-\\pi ,\\pi ],$ which is symmetric about the origin, we can conclude that\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t={\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t=0.\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨g(t),{f}_{2}(t)⟩ & =-2{\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }{sin}^{2}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{{f}_{2}(t)}\\,g(t) & =\\frac{-2{\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }{sin}^{2}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t}{⟨{f}_{2}(t),{f}_{2}(t)⟩}\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\  & =\\frac{-2{\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }{sin}^{2}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t}{{\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }{sin}^{2}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\  & =-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,g(t) & ={\\text{proj}}_{{f}_{1}(t)}\\,g(t)+{\\text{proj}}_{{f}_{2}(t)}\\,g(t) \\\\  & =6-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "0bc7e18b-fdb5-5429-828f-e671a9abcbda" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186413793}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186413795 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186241059 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "694bbe7e-1033-5685-8db7-c579e340a4af" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186238969} {:answer/id #uuid "7187a2ab-aec2-5516-9817-82c6b1e6f0ad" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239028} {:answer/id #uuid "98f9f087-a4cb-5b2b-ba88-6c34977c1ab6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\sqrt{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239383} {:answer/id #uuid "d0b7eedf-e87f-53c1-a7c0-62ebe727a2fa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239882} {:answer/id #uuid "f69f76e8-1b93-5cd1-b517-2cdfc888efb3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186240229}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186239028} :answer-field/id #uuid "eabf99e8-310f-5528-b7c0-2d81c99774f8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186240742}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "a70f1b99-0d4b-5ef2-9e7e-0dc7f6d78417" :question/math-academy-id "q-105461" :question/problem "Let $V=C[0,1],$ the vector space of functions that are continuous over $[0,1].$ Given that the inner product on $V$ is defined as \n\n$$\n⟨x(t),y(t)⟩={\\int }_{0}^{1}x(t)y(t)\\,\\text{d}t,\n$$\n\nfind the distance between $f(t)=3t$ and $g(t)=-3t+3$ with respect to the inner product." :question/worked-solution "The distance between $f(t)=3t$ and $g(t)=-3t+3$ with respect to the given inner product is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & =‖f(t)-g(t)‖ \\\\  & =‖3t-(-3t+3)‖ \\\\  & =‖6t-3‖ \\\\  & =\\sqrt{⟨6t-3,6t-3⟩}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the inner product, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨6t-3,6t-3⟩ & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(6t-3)^{2}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(36{t}^{2}-36t+9)\\text{d}t \\\\  & =[12{t}^{3}-18{t}^{2}+9t]_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =(12-18+9)-0 \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the distance is $d=\\sqrt{3}.$"} :step/id #uuid "5b37fdbb-b243-534e-930c-c4161c64569a" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186413796}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186413796 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186241063 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "10e4eb1a-f07b-5500-9b6b-bb104419f1ab" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186238193} {:answer/id #uuid "1597005a-083a-5b2f-904c-d3601ce7fd24" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186238235} {:answer/id #uuid "189191f2-31ca-5b6a-bb47-311abb8904ac" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186238264} {:answer/id #uuid "46644984-af18-5419-96cd-d6307758e813" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186238666} {:answer/id #uuid "94691432-7499-5d85-bd53-7c03102b3e45" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239340}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186238264} :answer-field/id #uuid "e7bf2326-7e5c-5bac-b442-4f7e564308dc" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186240734}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "5d460b9a-6ed3-5d23-955f-f3e1840b55a2" :question/math-academy-id "q-105475" :question/problem "Let $V=C[0,1],$ the vector space of functions that are continuous over $[0,1].$ Given that the inner product on $V$ is defined as \n\n$$\n⟨x(t),y(t)⟩={\\int }_{0}^{1}x(t)y(t)\\,\\text{d}t,\n$$\n\nfind the distance between $f(t)=8t$ and the subspace spanned by $g(t)=4t-2$ with respect to the inner product." :question/worked-solution "The orthogonal projection of $f(t)$ onto $\\text{Span}{g(t)}$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{g(t)}\\,f(t) & =\\frac{⟨f(t),g(t)⟩}{⟨g(t),g(t)⟩}\\,g(t) \\\\  & =\\frac{{\\int }_{0}^{1}8t\\cdot (4t-2)\\,\\text{d}t}{{\\int }_{0}^{1}(4t-2)^{2}\\,\\text{d}t}\\cdot (4t-2) \\\\  & =\\frac{{\\int }_{0}^{1}(32{t}^{2}-16t)\\,\\text{d}t}{{\\int }_{0}^{1}(16{t}^{2}-16t+4)\\,\\text{d}t}\\cdot (4t-2) \\\\  & =\\frac{[\\frac{32}{3}{t}^{3}-8{t}^{2}]_{0}^{1}}{[\\frac{16}{3}{t}^{3}-8{t}^{2}+4t]_{0}^{1}}\\cdot (4t-2) \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\frac{32}{3}-8)-0}{(\\frac{16}{3}-4)-0}\\cdot (6t-3) \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\frac{8}{3})}{(\\frac{4}{3})}\\cdot (4t+2) \\\\  & =2\\cdot (4t-2) \\\\  & =8t-4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe distance between $f(t)=8t$ and the subspace spanned by $g(t)=4t-2$ with respect to our inner product is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & =‖f(t)-{\\text{proj}}_{g(t)}\\,f(t)‖ \\\\  & =‖8t-(8t-4)‖ \\\\  & =‖4‖ \\\\  & =\\sqrt{⟨4,4⟩}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the inner product, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨4,4⟩ & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}4\\cdot 4\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}16\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =16t\\,{|}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =16-0 \\\\  & =16.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the distance is $d=\\sqrt{16}=4.$"} :step/id #uuid "5b4d4a19-f8e8-5a9a-8c13-582c4e2b0863"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186413797 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186240983 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "09b29cd5-55b5-5722-ba90-23f769345723" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "5\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186238150} {:answer/id #uuid "5bc19ba5-b098-5af2-b759-18b7d61fa0df" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "5\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186238854} {:answer/id #uuid "64e8e822-ab38-5fcf-a7cb-162397992404" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "5\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186238937} {:answer/id #uuid "9cd846ec-dcb9-5576-8a82-5cbd0e68c90d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239418} {:answer/id #uuid "a19047b8-6624-5f0c-b217-a2d6a5d4fcd6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+5\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239462}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186238937} :answer-field/id #uuid "f5a8c5d5-fcb2-5bdd-8555-14fa5c61c1d7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186240761}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "645a82fe-e42f-5dc9-9e79-d8bd0747a894" :question/math-academy-id "q-105136" :question/problem "Let $V=C[-\\pi ,\\pi ],$ the vector space of continuous functions over $[-\\pi ,\\pi ]$, and let the inner product on $V$ be defined as\n\n$$\n⟨x(t),y(t)⟩={\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }x(t)y(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nNow consider the following elements of $V:$\n\n$$\n{f}_{1}(t)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,{f}_{2}(t)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,g(t)=5\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+1\n$$\n\nFind the orthogonal projection of $g(t)$ onto $S=\\text{Span}{{f}_{1}(t),{f}_{2}(t)}$ given that ${f}_{1}(t)⊥{f}_{2}(t)$ with respect to the inner product, and that the orthogonal projection of $g(t)$ onto $\\text{Span}{{f}_{1}(t)}$ equals $5\\operatorname{cos}⁡t.$" :question/worked-solution "Since the set ${{f}_{1}(t),{f}_{2}(t)}$ is orthogonal, the orthogonal projection of $g(t)$ onto the subspace $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,g(t)={\\text{proj}}_{{f}_{1}(t)}\\,g(t)+{\\text{proj}}_{{f}_{2}(t)}\\,g(t).\n$$\n\nWe are given that ${\\text{proj}}_{{f}_{1}(t)}\\,g(t)=5\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,$ so let's now find the orthogonal projection of $g(t)$ onto $\\text{Span}{{f}_{2}(t)}.$ This can be computed as\n\n$$\n{\\text{proj}}_{{f}_{2}(t)}\\,g(t)=\\frac{⟨g(t),{f}_{2}(t)⟩}{⟨{f}_{2}(t),{f}_{2}(t)⟩}\\,{f}_{2}(t).\n$$\n\n \n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨g(t),{f}_{2}(t)⟩ & ={\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }g(t){f}_{2}(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }(5\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+1)\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =5{\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t-4{\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }{sin}^{2}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t+{\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that $\\operatorname{sin}⁡t$ is an odd function, while $\\operatorname{cos}⁡t$ is even. Hence, their product $\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t$ must be an odd function. Now, since we have an integral over the interval $[-\\pi ,\\pi ],$ which is symmetric about the origin, we can conclude that\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t={\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t=0.\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨g(t),{f}_{2}(t)⟩ & =-4{\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }{sin}^{2}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{{f}_{2}(t)}\\,g(t) & =\\frac{-4{\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }{sin}^{2}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t}{⟨{f}_{2}(t),{f}_{2}(t)⟩}\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\  & =\\frac{-4{\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }{sin}^{2}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t}{{\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }{sin}^{2}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\  & =-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,g(t) & ={\\text{proj}}_{{f}_{1}(t)}\\,g(t)+{\\text{proj}}_{{f}_{2}(t)}\\,g(t) \\\\  & =5\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "681a7c6c-5a02-51e9-b790-4836129a3fb4" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186413794}}] :activity/title "Projecting Vectors Onto Subspaces in Inner Product Spaces" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413792}] [{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167938} :activity/id #uuid "aee81a52-a15f-57d8-b2cd-df2429271786" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050989 :topic/id #uuid "7742668d-ad15-5c25-bc82-4ea5b4fd800d" :topic/math-academy-id 3198} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167938 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186168045 :tutorial/content "The **Fourier convergence theorem** describes how a Fourier series behaves, especially at points where the original function is not continuous.\n\nA Fourier series is built from smooth sine and cosine waves, so it cannot “jump” instantly the way a function with a jump discontinuity can. Instead, the series *converges* to an average value at these jumps.\n\nMore precisely, let $f(x)$ be a $2L$-periodic piecewise smooth function, and let its Fourier series be\n\n$$\nS(x)=\\dfrac{a_0}{2}+\\sum_{n=1}^{\\infty}\\left(\na_n\\cos\\left(\\dfrac{n\\pi}{L}x\\right)+b_n\\sin\\left(\\dfrac{n\\pi}{L}x\\right)\n\\right).\n$$\n\nThen the Fourier series converges for every $x\\in\\mathbb{R}$. The value it converges to depends on whether $f$ is continuous at $x$.\n\n- If $f(x)$ is continuous at $x,$ the series converges to the function value:\n\n$$\nS(x)=f(x)\n$$\n\n- If $f(x)$ has a jump discontinuity at $x,$ the series converges to the midpoint of the one-sided limits:\n\n$$\nS(x)=\\dfrac{f^-(x)+f^+(x)}{2},\n$$\n\nwhere $f^-(x)$ and $f^+(x)$ are the left- and right-sided limits of $f$ at $x,$ respectively.\n\nLet's see a concrete example." :tutorial/id #uuid "7d62d7d1-1939-536d-8fef-b02c4d023b32" :tutorial/math-academy-id 14717 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "0daa028f-4325-5685-88f2-3cbbcf622af2" :step/math-academy-id 598800 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167957}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167939 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166960 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166575 :question/id #uuid "7e478876-8258-54a8-9dd7-81978b7cf9af" :question/math-academy-id "e-21865"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "5f9dcbb0-d943-5340-916e-d789b6ab03bc" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048443} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049995}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167292} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167293} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317392} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317393} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317394} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317395} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317396} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317397} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186375031} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186375034} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186375055}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Value to What a Fourier Series Converges"} :step/id #uuid "31151663-c118-573c-a32e-a71852a7ab4b" :step/math-academy-id 598801 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167940}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167940 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186168046 :tutorial/content "A powerful application of Fourier series is *evaluating infinite sums*. The strategy is to find a known Fourier series that contains the sum we want, and then choose a convenient value of $x$ to plug in.\n\nFor example, consider the function $F(x)=x^2$ defined on $x \\in [-\\pi,\\pi).$ The Fourier series of the $2\\pi$-periodic extension $f(x)$ of $F(x)$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x) \\sim \\dfrac{\\pi^2}{3} + 4\\sum_{n=1}^\\infty \\dfrac{(-1)^n}{n^2} \\cos(nx).\n$$\n\nLet's use this to find the value of the infinite sum $\\displaystyle\\sum_{n=1}^\\infty \\dfrac{(-1)^n}{n^2}.$ \n\nNow, notice that at $x=0,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\cos\\left(n\\cdot0\\right) = \\cos\\left(0\\right) = 1.\n$$\n\nBy the *pointwise convergence theorem*, since $f(x)$ is continuous at $x=0,$ the corresponding Fourier series converges to \n\n$$\nf\\left(0\\right) = 0.\n$$\n\n \n\nHence, we have the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(0) & =\\frac{{\\pi }^{2}}{3}+4{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{(-1)^{n}}{{n}^{2}}\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}(n\\cdot 0) \\\\ 0 & =\\frac{{\\pi }^{2}}{3}+4{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{(-1)^{n}}{{n}^{2}}\\cdot 1 \\\\ -\\frac{{\\pi }^{2}}{3} & =4{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{(-1)^{n}}{{n}^{2}} \\\\ -\\frac{{\\pi }^{2}}{12} & ={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{(-1)^{n}}{{n}^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $\\displaystyle\\sum_{n=1}^\\infty \\dfrac{(-1)^n}{n^2} = -\\dfrac{\\pi^2}{12}.$" :tutorial/id #uuid "8db745e6-4117-50ca-a3ea-393692db7e84" :tutorial/math-academy-id 14721 :tutorial/title "Using Fourier Series For Finding Infinite Sums"} :step/id #uuid "4bdf108a-d330-5888-ae50-49c4e0f66ede" :step/math-academy-id 598804 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167941}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167941 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166961 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166576 :question/id #uuid "0e966ca9-eab2-5443-9581-9b7cb8ecd4b2" :question/math-academy-id "e-21866"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b3cb7656-1ce6-5b6a-9e88-5ba24a943c3f" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167294} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167295} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317399} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317401} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317402} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186375037} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186402284} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186402302} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186402320} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186402338}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Value of an Infinite Sum Using Fourier Series"} :step/id #uuid "42cd3b2e-2abc-5095-b2fe-87d2a522be3b" :step/math-academy-id 598805 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167942}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167942 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186168047 :tutorial/content "Even though the Fourier series converges to the correct value at each point (using the midpoint rule at jumps), the *graphs of the partial sums* can behave strangely near a jump discontinuity.\n\nSuppose $f(x)$ has a jump discontinuity at $x=x_0$. Then:\n\n- The Fourier series converges at the jump to the midpoint:\n\n$$\nS(x_0)=\\dfrac{f^-(x_0)+f^+(x_0)}{2}.\n$$\n\n- However, for large $N$, the partial sum\n\n$$\nS_N(x)=\\dfrac{a_0}{2}+\\sum_{n=1}^{N}\\left(\na_n\\cos\\left(\\dfrac{n\\pi}{L}x\\right)+b_n\\sin\\left(\\dfrac{n\\pi}{L}x\\right)\n\\right)\n$$\n\ntypically *overshoots* above the higher side of the jump and *undershoots* below the lower side.\n\nAs $N$ increases, the oscillations get squeezed into a smaller neighborhood around $x_0$, but the size of the overshoot does *not* go to $0$. This persistent overshoot near jump discontinuities is called the **Gibbs phenomenon**.\n\nFor example, for the Fourier series of the $2\\pi$-periodic extension $f(x)$ of the function $F(x)=x,$ defined on $[-\\pi,\\pi),$ the phenomenon can be observed at each point of discontinuity on the graph below.\n\n![](images/55/55a772d1f18a80a16772e6a898917a3ad863ee33a3c5b482d9201b7d22c91089.png)" :tutorial/id #uuid "c33817ab-d849-5e36-8059-f0cb3e6146cd" :tutorial/math-academy-id 14722 :tutorial/title "Gibbs Phenomenon"} :step/id #uuid "bba9f02f-db71-5d7b-8afd-c5b08062d34c" :step/math-academy-id 598808} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167950 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186168048 :tutorial/content "As we saw, the partial sums $S_N(x)$ appear to converge to the function $f(x)$ as $N\\to\\infty$. The *Fourier convergence theorem* specifies the exact value to which the series converges.\n\n- At any point $x_0$ where $f(x)$ is *continuous*, the Fourier series converges to the function value:\n\n$$\nS(x_0)=f(x_0)\n$$\n\n- At any point $x_0$ where $f(x)$ has a *jump discontinuity*, the Fourier series converges to the midpoint of the jump (the average of the one-sided limits):\n\n$$\nS(x_0)=\\dfrac{f^-(x_0)+f^+(x_0)}{2}\n$$\n\n![](images/52/5261e83960334e21396e3c7e3e2c0af82154dba29f99a338f22d5ef615d1af80.png)\n\nFor our sawtooth function above, the discontinuities occur at $x=(2k+1)\\pi$. At $x=\\pi$, for example, the series converges to:\n\n$$\nS(\\pi)=\\dfrac{f^-(\\pi)+f^+(\\pi)}{2} = \\dfrac{\\pi + (-\\pi)}{2} = 0\n$$\n\nThe graph of the limit function $S(x)$ is shown below.\n\n![](images/c5/c5731fc49b0bca19ae60b9a5bc14eae5ee64f8ff36ebae85dcf2ffe0a4270708.png)\n\n**Note:** The limit function $S(x)$ is the same as $f(x)$ except at the jump discontinuities. If $f(x)$ were continuous everywhere, then its Fourier series would converge to $f(x)$ at every point, and we could write $f(x)=S(x)$ instead of $f(x)\\sim S(x)$." :tutorial/id #uuid "73760171-1fe4-5fe1-ae35-b5efeeb39cdc" :tutorial/math-academy-id 14761 :tutorial/title "An Example of Convergence"} :step/id #uuid "18f33be1-5014-56eb-a799-174a9420a064" :step/math-academy-id 601089 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167939}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167957 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186168054 :tutorial/content "Consider the function $F(x)=x$ defined on $[-\\pi,\\pi).$ The Fourier series of its $2\\pi$-periodic extension $f(x)$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x) \\sim S(x) = \\sum_{n=1}^{\\infty} \\dfrac{2(-1)^{n+1}}{n}\\sin\\left(nx\\right).\n$$\n\nThe graph of the function $y=f(x)$ is shown below.\n\n![](images/52/5261e83960334e21396e3c7e3e2c0af82154dba29f99a338f22d5ef615d1af80.png)\n\nWe can approximate the function $f(x)$ by summing a finite number of terms from its Fourier series. These approximations are called *partial sums*:\n\n$$\nS_N(x)=\\sum_{n=1}^{N}\\dfrac{2(-1)^{n+1}}{n}\\sin\\left(nx\\right)\n$$\n\nAs $N$ increases, the partial sums match the graph of $f(x)$ more closely.\n\n![](images/4c/4c3020fcc3aa06bec65384bb3ba852b289fd1d705e554d39cc3be9b41db6623d.png)" :tutorial/id #uuid "dcb1351c-d635-5660-9fec-0f4c5c5f9018" :tutorial/math-academy-id 14872 :tutorial/title "An Example of Approximation"} :step/id #uuid "48047656-abbd-570b-8b4c-235ee9655e83" :step/math-academy-id 607545 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167950}}] :activity/title "Convergence of Fourier Series" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186166993}] [{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162952} :activity/id #uuid "6fbac718-0db1-5d0a-8eac-581af3c3ed39" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186051001 :topic/id #uuid "789ccc3c-cca0-5e71-bdb6-b757b2abcfa0" :topic/math-academy-id 3001} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162952 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186163262 :tutorial/content "We often want to know if there is a relationship between two random variables. For this reason, we wish to formulate the idea of a joint probability distribution.\n\nTo introduce this concept, suppose the random variable $X$ represents the number of heads obtained when a fair coin is flipped. The support of $X,$ denoted $S_X,$ consists of all possible values of $X$ and in this case is given by\n\n$$\nS_X = \\{0,1\\}.\n$$\n\nNow suppose that the random variable $Y$ represents the result of rolling a fair tetrahedral die. Then the support of $Y,$ denoted $S_Y,$ is\n\n$$\nS_Y = \\{1,2,3,4\\}.\n$$\n\nThe **joint support** of $X$ and $Y,$ denoted $S,$ consists of all possible pairs $(x,y)$ such that $x$ is a possible outcome for $X$ and $y$ is a possible outcome for $Y\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(0,1), & (0,2), & (0,3), & (0,4) \\\\ (1,1), & (1,2), & (1,3), & (1,4)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that $S_X\\times S_Y$ is the cartesian product of $S_X$ and $S_Y.$\n\nA **joint probability distribution** is a probability distribution over a set of pairs [math](X, Y),[/math] where [math]X[/math] and [math]Y[/math] are both random variables. In this lesson, we will focus on the case where [math]X[/math] and [math]Y[/math] are *discrete* random variables, in which case the joint probability distribution is called a **joint probability mass function**. \n\nLet's assume that the flipping of the coin and rolling of the die are independent. Then, for any $x \\in X$ and any $y \\in Y,$ we have that\n\n$$\nP(X=x\\:\\textrm{and}\\:Y=y) = \\dfrac{1}{2} \\cdot \\dfrac{1}{4} = \\dfrac{1}{8}.\n$$\n\n \n\nThe **joint probability mass function** of $X$ and $Y,$ denoted $f(x,y),$ can be represented by the following table:\n\n| $f(x,y)$ | $Y=1$ | $Y=2$ | $Y=3$ | $Y=4$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $X=0$ | $\\dfrac{1}{8}$ | $\\dfrac{1}{8}$ | $\\dfrac{1}{8}$ | $\\dfrac{1}{8}$ |\n| $X=1$ | $\\dfrac{1}{8}$ | $\\dfrac{1}{8}$ | $\\dfrac{1}{8}$ | $\\dfrac{1}{8}$ |\n\nSimilar to the case of single random variables, we have\n\n$$\nf(x,y) = P(X=x\\:\\textrm{ and }\\:Y=y).\n$$\n\nGoing forward, we will use the shorthand notation $P(X=x, Y=y)$ to mean $P(X=x\\:\\textrm{ and }\\:Y=y).$" :tutorial/id #uuid "79b99576-5a28-5c03-bc5d-1cc472ef0795" :tutorial/math-academy-id 5348 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "9628dc39-75f0-5169-a945-48c68e6e4f31" :step/math-academy-id 33477 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186163076}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162982 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162063 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186161863 :question/id #uuid "2d698f44-e551-58a2-883b-3a992119bd76" :question/math-academy-id "e-9126"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "38398622-ca45-5eb6-a589-96aec162960a" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048368} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050432}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162280} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162291} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186247166} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186247167} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275141} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275142} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275143} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275144} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275145}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Joint Distribution of Two Discrete Random Variables"} :step/id #uuid "3e1d6fef-61c1-5a3a-805d-6a020df8bb08" :step/math-academy-id 34016 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162999}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162999 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162073 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186161873 :question/id #uuid "f2789f6c-11d5-5132-91b3-be65dac4ce24" :question/math-academy-id "e-9203"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c0e20c64-85be-5cb5-acde-17ffd8b6aff5" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162281} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162292} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186247025} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186247178} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186247179} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186247180} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186247181} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186247184}] :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Joint Probability From a Table"} :step/id #uuid "b093ac87-86c2-5281-860f-df1321b270d4" :step/math-academy-id 34339 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186163000}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186163000 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162074 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186161874 :question/id #uuid "01b86f31-f31e-567d-b798-fc0f10a4d0b1" :question/math-academy-id "e-9204"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e247e2c9-3e47-5cf4-be8d-f1f22bb30b39" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162282} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162283} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186247196} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186247197} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186247200} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186247202} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186247203} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186247204}] :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Joint Probability Containing Inequalities From a Table"} :step/id #uuid "657c560d-1a7e-5600-8883-4eaa35b157cd" :step/math-academy-id 34343} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186163076 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186163328 :tutorial/content "Let $X$ and $Y$ be discrete random variables with supports $S_X$ and $S_Y$, respectively. For a function \n\n$$\nf(x,y)=P(X=x, Y=y)\n$$\n\n \n\nto be a valid joint probability mass function with joint support $S=S_X\\times S_Y,$ it must satisfy the following conditions:\n\n- $0 \\leq f(x,y) \\leq 1$ for all $(x,y) \\in S$\n\n- $\\displaystyle\\sum\\limits_{(x,y) \\in S} f(x,y)= 1$\n\n- $P\\left((X,Y) \\in A \\right) = \\displaystyle \\sum\\limits_{(x,y) \\in A} f(x,y),$ where $A$ is a subset of $S.$\n\nThere is some nice intuition to these conditions.\n\n- The condition $0 \\leq f(x,y) \\leq 1$ for all $(x,y)\\in S$ states that any possible outcome for $(X,Y)$ must have a probability between $0$ and $1.$\n\n- The condition $\\displaystyle \\sum\\limits_{(x,y) \\in S} f(x,y) = 1$ states that the sum of all the probabilities over all possible values of $X$ and $Y$ must add up to $1.$\n\n- The third condition states that we compute the probability of an event $A$ by adding up the probabilities associated with $A.$\n\nThe joint probability mass function of a discrete random variable with finite support can be represented using a table, as shown below.\n\n|  | $Y=y_1$ | $Y=y_2$ | $\\ldots$ | $Y=y_n$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $X=x_1$ | $f(x_1,y_1)$ | $f(x_1,y_2)$ | $\\ldots$ | $f(x_1,y_n)$ |\n| $X=x_2$ | $f(x_2,y_1)$ | $f(x_2,y_2)$ | $\\ldots$ | $f(x_2,y_n)$ |\n| $\\ldots$ | $\\ldots$ | $\\ldots$ | $\\ldots$ | $\\ldots$ |\n| $X=x_k$ | $f(x_k,y_1)$ | $f(x_k,y_2)$ | $\\ldots$ | $f(x_k,y_n)$ |\n\nIn the case of two random variables, the joint distribution is sometimes called a **bivariate distribution**.\nHowever, the concept generalizes to any number of random variables, giving a **multivariate distribution**." :tutorial/id #uuid "5f5858f9-f1f3-5d98-b436-9ffd034a3e9d" :tutorial/math-academy-id 6284 :tutorial/title "Bivariate and Multivariate Distributions"} :step/id #uuid "2636297d-b114-590c-9454-08ebab970ce2" :step/math-academy-id 38084 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162982}}] :activity/title "Joint Distributions for Discrete Random Variables" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186162162}]}
